 Chapter 1. 1 Welcome}\par}{\footerl\pard\sl-240\sb770\sa910\plain\tqc\tx4680\tqr\tx9360 {}\tab {}\tab {\i\fs20 \chpgn }\par}{\headerr\pard\sl-240\sb770\sa430\plain\tqc\tx4680\tqr\tx9360 {}\tab {}\tab {\i\fs20 Chapter 1. 1 Welcome}\par}{\footerr\pard\sl-240\sb770\sa910\plain\tqc\tx4680\tqr\tx9360 {}\tab {}\tab {\i\fs20 \chpgn }\par}\pard\sb311\s1\sl539 {\*\bkmkstart _25}{\*\bkmkend _25}\b\fs41\f1 Chapter 1. 1 Welcome\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb207\li960\sl260 \b0\fs20\lang1033\f0 Welcome to the \i Alligata Server User Manual\i0 . This manual is in two parts:\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1160\sl260\fi-200 \tx1160 \fs16\lang1024 \'95\tab \fs20 This part, \i Getting Started\i0 , provides an overview of the Alligata Server, and explains how to install the program. It covers the following topics:\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260\fi-200 \tx1360 \fs14 \'95\tab \fs20 How the Alligata Server fits into the architecture of the Wireless Application Protocol (WAP), the Short Message Service (SMS) and the World Wide Web\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260\fi-200 \tx1360 \fs14 \'95\tab \fs20 The internal functioning of the Alligata Server\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260\fi-200 \tx1360 \fs14 \'95\tab \fs20 The hardware and software you need to run the Alligata Server\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260\fi-200 \tx1360 \fs14 \'95\tab \fs20 How to install the Alligata Server from a Linux/Solaris windowing system, or from the Linux/Solaris command prompt\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260\fi-200 \tx1360 \fs14 \'95\tab \fs20 How to spread an installation of the Alligata Server across several computers in order to maximise processing capacity\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1160\sl260\fi-200 \tx1160 \fs16 \'95\tab \fs20 The second part, \i Using the Alligata Server\i0 , is a detailed guide to running, configuring and using the Alligata Server. It covers the following topics:\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260\fi-200 \tx1360 \fs14 \'95\tab \fs20 How to start, stop and administer the Alligata Server\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260\fi-200 \tx1360 \fs14 \'95\tab \fs20 How to change the Alligata Server's configuration settings\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260\fi-200 \tx1360 \fs14 \'95\tab \fs20 How to view the example WAP sites provided with the Alligata Server\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260\fi-200 \tx1360 \fs14 \'95\tab \fs20 How to create WAP sites using Wireless Markup Language (WML)\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260\fi-200 \tx1360 \fs14 \'95\tab \fs20 How to create dynamic WAP sites using the Perl scripting language\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260\fi-200 \tx1360 \fs14 \'95\tab \fs20 How to create dynamic WAP sites using the PHP scripting language\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260\fi-200 \tx1360 \fs14 \'95\tab \fs20 How to use the Alligata Server's SMS features to deliver World Wide Web and other content to mobile phones\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260\fi-200 \tx1360 \fs14 \'95\tab \fs20 How to use the Alligata Server to send SMS messages from a computer workstation\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260\fi-200 \tx1360 \fs14 \'95\tab \fs20 How to configure a mobile phone for a WAP service 'over the air' using the Alligata Server's SMS features\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb259\s2\sl449 {\*\bkmkstart _66}{\*\bkmkend _66}\b\fs34\f1 1.1 The Alligata Server Package\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb173\li960\sl260 \b0\fs20\lang1033\f0 Your Alligata Server package contains:\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1160\sl260\fi-200 \tx1160 \fs16\lang1024 \'95\tab \fs20 An installation CD-ROM from which you can install the Alligata Server, some example WAP sites, the Apache Web server, the PHP scripting language and some modules for the Perl scripting language that are useful for developing Mobile Internet sites.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1160\sl260\fi-200 \tx1160 \fs16 \'95\tab \fs20 A booklet, \i Your Pocket Guide to the Mobile Internet\i0 .\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1160\sl260\fi-200 \tx1160 \fs16 \'95\tab \fs20 This \i Alligata Server User Manual\i0 .\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1160\sl260\fi-200 \tx1160 \fs16 \'95\tab \fs20 An \i Alligata Server Quick Reference Guide\i0 .\hyphpar0\par\sect\sectd\plain\pgwsxn12240\pghsxn15840\marglsxn1440\margrsxn1440\margtsxn1440\margbsxn1920\headery0\footery0\pgndec\titlepg{\headerf\pard\sl-240\sb770\sa430\plain\tqc\tx4680\tqr\tx9360 {}\tab {}\tab {}\par}{\footerf\pard\sl-240\sb770\sa910\plain\tqc\tx4680\tqr\tx9360 {}\tab {}\tab {\i\fs20 \chpgn }\par}{\headerl\pard\sl-240\sb770\sa430\plain\tqc\tx4680\tqr\tx9360 {}\tab {}\tab {\i\fs20 Chapter 2. 2 System Requirements}\par}{\footerl\pard\sl-240\sb770\sa910\plain\tqc\tx4680\tqr\tx9360 {}\tab {}\tab {\i\fs20 \chpgn }\par}{\headerr\pard\sl-240\sb770\sa430\plain\tqc\tx4680\tqr\tx9360 {}\tab {}\tab {\i\fs20 Chapter 2. 2 System Requirements}\par}{\footerr\pard\sl-240\sb770\sa910\plain\tqc\tx4680\tqr\tx9360 {}\tab {}\tab {\i\fs20 \chpgn }\par}\pard\sb311\s1\sl539 {\*\bkmkstart _81}{\*\bkmkend _81}\b\fs41\f1 Chapter 2. 2 System Requirements\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb207\li960\sl260 \b0\fs20\lang1033\f0 There are two versions of the Alligata Server, one for the Linux operating system and one for the Sun Solaris operating system.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb259\s2\sl449 {\*\bkmkstart _84}{\*\bkmkend _84}\b\fs34\lang1024\f1 2.1 Linux Version\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb216\s3\li960\sl374 {\*\bkmkstart _86}{\*\bkmkend _86}\fs28 Minimum System Requirements\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb144\li960\sl260 \fs20\f0 Hardware: \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1160\sl260\fi-200 \tx1160 \b0\fs16 \'95\tab \fs20 100% IBM-compatible PC with at least 16 MB of RAM\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1160\sl260\fi-200 \tx1160 \fs16 \'95\tab \fs20 Hard disk with at least 30 MB free\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1160\sl260\fi-200 \tx1160 \fs16 \'95\tab \fs20 Connection to the telephone network via modem or similar\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 \b Software: \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1160\sl260\fi-200 \tx1160 \b0\fs16 \'95\tab \fs20 Red Hat Linux version 6.0, 6.1 or 6.2 or Mandrake Linux version 7.0 or 7.1\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1160\sl260 \lang1033 (The Alligata Server may run on some other distributions of Linux)\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1160\sl260\fi-200 \tx1160 \fs16\lang1024 \'95\tab \fs20 Perl version 5.0 or higher. Perl is standard in most Linux distributions. If you do not have Perl installed, download it from www.perl.org (http://www.perl.org)\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb216\s3\li960\sl374 {\*\bkmkstart _108}{\*\bkmkend _108}\b\fs28\f1 Optional Hardware Accessories\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb144\li1160\sl260\fi-200 \tx1160 \b0\fs16\f0 \'95\tab \fs20 Connection to the Internet, either dial-in or dedicated\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1160\sl260\fi-200 \tx1160 \fs16 \'95\tab \fs20 GSM modem (Wavecom WM02, Nokia Premicell or Siemens M20T) \b or\b0  subscription to an SMS Centre (SMSC) \b or\b0  GSM mobile phone with integrated modem, for sending and receiving SMS messages\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb259\s2\sl449 {\*\bkmkstart _117}{\*\bkmkend _117}\b\fs34\f1 2.2 Solaris Version\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb216\s3\li960\sl374 {\*\bkmkstart _119}{\*\bkmkend _119}\fs28 Minimum System Requirements\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb144\li960\sl260 \fs20\f0 Hardware: \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1160\sl260\fi-200 \tx1160 \b0\fs16 \'95\tab \fs20 Sun UltraSPARC with at least 128 MB of RAM\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1160\sl260\fi-200 \tx1160 \fs16 \'95\tab \fs20 Hard disk with at least 30 MB free\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1160\sl260\fi-200 \tx1160 \fs16 \'95\tab \fs20 Connection to the telephone network\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 \b Software: \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1160\sl260\fi-200 \tx1160 \b0\fs16 \'95\tab \fs20 Sun Solaris version 7 or 8\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb216\s3\li960\sl374 {\*\bkmkstart _137}{\*\bkmkend _137}\b\fs28\f1 Optional Hardware Accessories\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb144\li1160\sl260\fi-200 \tx1160 \b0\fs16\f0 \'95\tab \fs20 Connection to the Internet, either dial-in or dedicated\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1160\sl260\fi-200 \tx1160 \fs16 \'95\tab \fs20 GSM modem (Wavecom WM02, Nokia Premicell or Siemens M20T) \b or\b0  subscription to an SMS Centre (SMSC) \b or\b0  GSM mobile phone with integrated modem, for sending and receiving SMS messages\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb259\s2\sl449 {\*\bkmkstart _146}{\*\bkmkend _146}\b\fs34\f1 2.3 Optional Software Included with the Alligata Server\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb173\li960\sl260 \b0\fs20\lang1033\f0 In addition to the Alligata Server software, the Alligata Server CD-ROM includes the following items of open source software, which you may find useful in setting up Mobile Internet services:\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1160\sl260\fi-200 \tx1160 \fs16\lang1024 \'95\tab \fs20 Apache Web server\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1160\sl260\fi-200 \tx1160 \fs16 \'95\tab \fs20 PHP scripting language\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1160\sl260\fi-200 \tx1160 \fs16 \'95\tab \fs20 Common Gateway Interface (CGI), HTML parser, HTML tables and WML modules for the Perl scripting language\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 \lang1033 The Alligata Server installation program gives you the option of installing any or all of these pieces of software at the same time as installing the Alligata Server.\hyphpar0\par\sect\sectd\plain\pgwsxn12240\pghsxn15840\marglsxn1440\margrsxn1440\margtsxn1440\margbsxn1920\headery0\footery0\pgndec\titlepg{\headerf\pard\sl-240\sb770\sa430\plain\tqc\tx4680\tqr\tx9360 {}\tab {}\tab {}\par}{\footerf\pard\sl-240\sb770\sa910\plain\tqc\tx4680\tqr\tx9360 {}\tab {}\tab {\i\fs20 \chpgn }\par}{\headerl\pard\sl-240\sb770\sa430\plain\tqc\tx4680\tqr\tx9360 {}\tab {}\tab {\i\fs20 Chapter 3. 3 About the Alligata Server}\par}{\footerl\pard\sl-240\sb770\sa910\plain\tqc\tx4680\tqr\tx9360 {}\tab {}\tab {\i\fs20 \chpgn }\par}{\headerr\pard\sl-240\sb770\sa430\plain\tqc\tx4680\tqr\tx9360 {}\tab {}\tab {\i\fs20 Chapter 3. 3 About the Alligata Server}\par}{\footerr\pard\sl-240\sb770\sa910\plain\tqc\tx4680\tqr\tx9360 {}\tab {}\tab {\i\fs20 \chpgn }\par}\pard\sb311\s1\sl539 {\*\bkmkstart _157}{\*\bkmkend _157}\b\fs41\f1 Chapter 3. 3 About the Alligata Server\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb259\s2\sl449 {\*\bkmkstart _159}{\*\bkmkend _159}\fs34 3.1 What is the Alligata Server?\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb173\li960\sl260 \b0\fs20\lang1033\f0 The Alligata Server is two applications in one: a WAP gateway and an SMS server.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 As a WAP gateway, the Alligata Server is an entry point to the Mobile Internet. If you are a system administrator, you can use it to link your company's employees' or clients' mobile WAP phones to a local intranet or to the whole Mobile Internet. By using your own installation of the Alligata Server rather than a WAP gateway belonging to a telephone network operator or an Internet Service Provider (ISP), you have complete control over what services you offer your WAP phone users, and you can guarantee they will not be restricted to sites 'approved' by a network operator or an ISP.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 As an SMS server, the Alligata Server allows you to send messages to SMS phone users from a computer workstation, and to implement dynamic SMS keyword services such as retrieval of information from the World Wide Web. Although SMS is much less flexible than WAP, it is available on almost all modern Global System for Mobile Telecommunications (GSM) mobile phones. With its SMS messaging features, the Alligata Server provides limited access to Internet content even to users of non-WAP phones.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 WAP and SMS complement each other. SMS is simple to use and widely available, but limited in its capabilities. WAP offers richer functionality, but as a new technology it is not yet as widespread as SMS. By offering both SMS and WAP functionality, the Alligata Server is a bridge between established and emerging technologies.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 The Alligata Server is based on the Kannel WAP and SMS gateway for Linux. Kannel is an open source software project; if you would like to know more about it or contribute to it, visit the Kannel Web site at www.kannel.org (http://www.kannel.org).\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb259\s2\sl449 {\*\bkmkstart _167}{\*\bkmkend _167}\b\fs34\lang1024\f1 3.2 The Alligata Server, WAP and SMS\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb173\li960\sl260 \b0\fs20\lang1033\f0 This section will help you to understand where the Alligata Server fits into the architecture of WAP and SMS. Before you read it, we recommend you read \i Your Pocket Guide to the Mobile Internet\i0 , included in the Alligata Server package. There you will find definitions of the terminology used by the WAP standard, and a description of how the WAP components fit together.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 Every WAP phone is configured by default to use a single WAP gateway to gain access to the Mobile Internet. This gateway can be hosted by an ISP, by the phone user's employer, or by any third party with access to the Mobile Internet. A phone can contain configurations for several gateways, but it can only use a single gateway for the duration of a session. If you want to change gateways, you have to close down your Mobile Internet session, switch your phone's settings to the new gateway, and reconnect to the Mobile Internet.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 The conceptual model into which the Alligata Server fits is shown in Figure 3.1.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960{\field\flddirty{\*\fldinst INCLUDEPICTURE "wapdiagsmall.ps" }{\fldrslt }}\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 A signal sent by a mobile device is picked up by one of a network operator's base stations. It is then routed through the mobile network to the Alligata Server using User Datagram Protocol (UDP). If it is an SMS message, it is first sent to the network's \b SMS Centre (SMSC)\b0 . The SMSC's main job is to hold pending SMS messages for recipients whose phones are turned off or otherwise inaccessible.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 Once the Alligata Server has received a WAP data packet from a client and decompressed it, it uses Hypertext Transfer Protocol (HTTP) to retrieve data from the relevant content server on the Mobile Internet.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 On receiving an SMS message, the Alligata Server scans it for keywords and parameters listed in its configuration file (see Section 13.1), then sends an automatic reply to the client device depending on which keyword and parameters it encountered. This reply can consist of static text, or it can be retrieved from a local file, a WAP site or a World Wide Web page.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 The Alligata Server's reply to an SMS message is another SMS message. This means that, in principle, it has to be sent to the relevant network operator's SMSC in order to be routed to the recipient device (or stored if the recipient device is not accessible). Most network operators charge a fee for terrestrial access to their SMSCs, but the Alligata Server allows you to circumvent this if you own a GSM modem, or a mobile phone that includes a GSM modem such as the Nokia 7110 or 6210. The Alligata Server can use the modem to send SMS messages directly over the airwaves. This way, you will get charged the same rates as mobile phone users for sending SMS messages from your computer workstation. See Section 9.3.2 for details of how to send messages using a GSM modem.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb259\s2\sl449 {\*\bkmkstart _181}{\*\bkmkend _181}\b\fs34\lang1024\f1 3.3 Inside the Alligata Server\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb173\li960\sl260 \b0\fs20\lang1033\f0 The Alligata Server is a collection of three programs: a Bearer Box, a WAP Box and an SMS Box. In fact, an implementation of the Alligata Server can include any number of WAP Boxes and SMS Boxes, all linked to a single Bearer Box.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 All incoming messages pass through the Bearer Box. The Bearer Box performs preliminary operations on each message and forwards it on to a WAP or an SMS Box depending on whether it is a WAP or an SMS message.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 A WAP Box implements the WAP protocol stack on incoming WAP messages (apart from Wireless Datagram Protocol (WDP), which is implemented by the Bearer Box). It decompresses the WAP data and forwards requests to Internet servers using HTTP (see the booklet \i Your Pocket Guide to the Mobile Internet\i0  for more details). It then handles the replies from the HTTP servers, compresses the replies, implements the Wireless Session Protocol (WSP) and Wireless Transaction Protocol (WTP) layers of the WAP protocol stack on them, and sends them on to the Bearer Box. The Bearer Box implements the WDP layer, and sends the replies to the client device.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 An SMS Box scans incoming SMS messages for keywords listed in the Alligata Server configuration file. It carries out the function associated with the keyword - for example, retrieving a piece of text from a Web page - and sends a reply SMS message, via the Bearer Box, to the client device.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb216\s3\li960\sl374 {\*\bkmkstart _188}{\*\bkmkend _188}\b\fs28\lang1024\f1 3.3.1 Program States\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb144\li960\sl260 \b0\fs20\lang1033\f0 At any given time, the Alligata Server can be in one of four states:\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1160\sl260\fi-200 \tx1160 \fs16\lang1024 \'95\tab \fs20 Running. The Alligata Server accepts, processes and sends files and messages normally.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1160\sl260\fi-200 \tx1160 \fs16 \'95\tab \fs20 Suspended. The Alligata Server does not accept any new WAP requests or messages from SMSCs. The Bearer Box does not allow new WAP or SMS Boxes to connect to it. No messages or files are sent out.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1160\sl260\fi-200 \tx1160 \fs16 \'95\tab \fs20 Isolated. The Alligata Server does not accept any new WAP requests or messages from SMSCs. However, it continues processing messages already in its system, and accepts SMS messages sent via HTTP (see Section 13.2).\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1160\sl260\fi-200 \tx1160 \fs16 \'95\tab \fs20 Shutdown. The Alligata Server performs the shutdown procedure. New WAP requests and SMS messages are refused. Requests already being processed are carried through. When all queues in the boxes have been processed, the Alligata Server closes down.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 \lang1033 You can change the state of the Alligata Server using HTTP administration commands. See Section 8.2.2.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 There follows a more detailed look at the components of the Alligata Server. You do not need to know this information to use the Alligata Server, but you may find it of interest.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb216\s3\li960\sl374 {\*\bkmkstart _202}{\*\bkmkend _202}\b\fs28\lang1024\f1 3.3.2 Bearer Box\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb144\li960\sl260 \b0\fs20\lang1033\f0 The Bearer Box is the interface between the mobile telephone network and the rest of the Alligata Server. It receives data from mobile phones via network bearer services or SMSCs.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 On receiving a WAP data packet from a mobile device, the Bearer Box implements the WDP layer of the WAP protocol stack on it, and sends it on to a WAP Box. If a session is already running between the Alligata Server and the client device, then the message is sent to the same WAP Box as previous messages from the device. If, on the other hand, the message is the first to be received from that client in the current session, then it is sent to a WAP box at random. A basic system of load balancing is used to avoid bottlenecks forming in any single WAP Box. Load balancing works as follows: at regular intervals, each WAP Box sends a 'heartbeat' message (see Section 3.3.3) to the Bearer Box indicating its load - that is, how many sessions it is currently handling. If this load is significantly higher than the load of the least busy WAP box, then the Bearer Box stops sending requests from new clients to the busier WAP box. When the load of the busier WAP Box has fallen sufficiently, or the load of the other WAP Boxes has increased to near its own, the busier WAP Box starts to accept requests from new clients again.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 Messages are distributed among the SMS Boxes in the same way as among as the WAP Boxes. That is, the Bearer Box sends all messages from a particular client to the same SMS Box; it assigns messages from new clients to an SMS Box at random; and it uses load balancing to prevent unnecessary congestion in any particular SMS Box.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 Within the Alligata Server, the Bearer Box acts as a server to the WAP or SMS Boxes. Once running, it keeps a list of all the WAP and SMS Boxes connected to it. This list starts out empty, and is extended as boxes start up and announce their presence. The Bearer Box is in constant communication with the WAP and SMS Boxes via 'heartbeat' messages (see Section 3.3.3), and dynamically updates its list of connected WAP and SMS Boxes as new boxes connect and old ones disconnect.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 As well as receiving incoming messages, the Bearer Box processes all outgoing messages from the Alligata Server's WAP and SMS Boxes, adapting each message to the bearer service of the client's network. In the case of WAP messages, it also implements the WDP layer of the WAP protocol stack.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 Since all incoming and outgoing messages pass through a single Bearer Box, there is a risk of bottlenecks arising when traffic becomes very heavy. The Alligata Server is designed so that the Bearer Box does as little processing on each data packet as possible, thereby reducing this risk (particularly if there are several WAP and SMS Boxes running on different computers).\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb216\s3\li960\sl374 {\*\bkmkstart _210}{\*\bkmkend _210}\b\fs28\lang1024\f1 3.3.3 Communication Between the Boxes\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb144\li960\sl260 \b0\fs20\lang1033\f0 Data is exchanged between the Bearer Box and the WAP and SMS Boxes via Transmission Control Protocol (TCP) stream. TCP is a protocol suited for use in wide area networks (such as the Internet), and its incorporation into the Alligata Server means that the Bearer Box, WAP Boxes and SMS Boxes can be run on separate computers, if required. The advantages of this set-up are clear: it means that, outside the Bearer Box, groups of packets can be processed simultaneously, and therefore the Alligata Server can process high volumes of messages very quickly.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 If the TCP stream between the Bearer Box and a WAP or SMS Box fails for any reason, the Bearer Box notices this and removes the disconnected box from its list of available boxes. Any packets from phones that were routed to this box are then treated as packets from new clients, and redirected by the Bearer Box to an alternative WAP Box. When a WAP Box receives a message from a new client that it identifies as being from the middle of a session, it sends an error message to the client device.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 A WAP or SMS Box can also go 'catatonic': its TCP stream to the Bearer Box is still running, but it is not responding to any messages. So that the Bearer Box knows when this has happened, WAP and SMS Boxes send 'heartbeat' packets at regular intervals, effectively telling the Bearer Box that they are running and functioning normally. If the Bearer Box stops receiving heartbeat messages from another box, it assumes there is a problem even though the TCP stream may still be running, and removes the box from its list. When the ailing box recovers and announces its presence, the Bearer Box reopens the connection.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb216\s3\li960\sl374 {\*\bkmkstart _215}{\*\bkmkend _215}\b\fs28\lang1024\f1 3.3.4 WAP Box\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb144\li960\sl260 \b0\fs20\lang1033\f0 A WAP Box receives data in WDP packets through its TCP connection with the Bearer Box. It implements the and WSP layers of the WAP protocol stack on the packets, and then decompresses them.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 Each incoming WDP packet is handled by a single program thread. This thread creates and manages a WTP state machine and, where necessary, a WSP state machine for the packet. The same WTP machine and WSP machine handle all packets from a given client session.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 If a packet arrives in a WAP Box while the WTP machine for which it is destined is still processing the previous packet, then the WAP Box sets up a queue specific to that WTP machine. This is preferable to creating a new thread for waiting WDP packets, as it reduces the risk of the WAP Box's thread table becoming full when traffic is very heavy.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 Once the WTP and WSP layers of the WAP protocol stack have been implemented on the incoming message, the WAP Box converts it into an HTTP request, and sends it to the relevant server on the Mobile Internet.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 On receiving an HTTP reply from the Mobile Internet server, the WAP Box compresses the reply, implements the WSP and WTP layers of the WAP protocol stack on it, and sends it to the Bearer Box along the TCP connection.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb216\s3\li960\sl374 {\*\bkmkstart _222}{\*\bkmkend _222}\b\fs28\lang1024\f1 3.3.5 SMS Box\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb144\li960\sl260 \b0\fs20\lang1033\f0 The design of an SMS Box is straightforward. An SMS Box receives SMS messages from the Bearer Box via its TCP connection, parses each message in order to extract keywords and parameters from it, and then executes a service according to which keyword the message contains. The keyword is the first word in the message; any other words are interpreted as parameters. See Section 13.1 for more information.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 If the SMS service in question has a url variable defined in the configuration file (see Section 9.3.3), then the SMS Box sends an HTTP request to the appropriate URL, retrieves the data, pulls out the content between the prefix and the suffix strings as specified in the configuration file, formats this content as an SMS message or messages, and sends the SMS message(s) to the Bearer Box via the TCP stream. The Bearer Box sends the message back to the client device via an SMSC.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 Other services the SMS Box can provide include sending a fixed text message or the content of a local file in response to a keyword. For more information, see Sections 9.3 and 13.1.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 The SMS Box also listens for SMS messages arriving via HTTP from computer workstations. It converts these messages from HTTP into true SMS format, and sends them on to the Bearer Box to be conveyed to mobile devices. See Section 13.2 for details of how to send SMS messages from a computer workstation.\hyphpar0\par\sect\sectd\plain\pgwsxn12240\pghsxn15840\marglsxn1440\margrsxn1440\margtsxn1440\margbsxn1920\headery0\footery0\pgndec\titlepg{\headerf\pard\sl-240\sb770\sa430\plain\tqc\tx4680\tqr\tx9360 {}\tab {}\tab {}\par}{\footerf\pard\sl-240\sb770\sa910\plain\tqc\tx4680\tqr\tx9360 {}\tab {}\tab {\i\fs20 \chpgn }\par}{\headerl\pard\sl-240\sb770\sa430\plain\tqc\tx4680\tqr\tx9360 {}\tab {}\tab {\i\fs20 Chapter 4. 4 Installation from a Graphical User Interface}\par}{\footerl\pard\sl-240\sb770\sa910\plain\tqc\tx4680\tqr\tx9360 {}\tab {}\tab {\i\fs20 \chpgn }\par}{\headerr\pard\sl-240\sb770\sa430\plain\tqc\tx4680\tqr\tx9360 {}\tab {}\tab {\i\fs20 Chapter 4. 4 Installation from a Graphical User Interface}\par}{\footerr\pard\sl-240\sb770\sa910\plain\tqc\tx4680\tqr\tx9360 {}\tab {}\tab {\i\fs20 \chpgn }\par}\pard\sb311\s1\sl539 {\*\bkmkstart _228}{\*\bkmkend _228}\b\fs41\f1 Chapter 4. 4 Installation from a Graphical User Interface\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb207\li960\sl260 \b0\fs20\lang1033\f0 If your Linux or Solaris system includes a graphical user interface, the installation program automatically installs the Alligata Server using the graphical procedure described in this section.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 \b\lang1024 To install the Alligata Server from a Linux/Solaris graphical user interface: \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1320\sl260\fi-360 \tqr\tx1270\tx1320\tab \b0 1.\tab Place the Alligata Server CD-ROM in the CD-ROM drive.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1320\sl260 \lang1033 If your version of Linux or Solaris automounts CD-ROMs, a file manager window will open, showing the contents of the directory into which the CD-ROM has been mounted. (In Linux this directory is usually \fs18\f2 /mnt/cdrom\fs20\f0 , in Solaris it is usually \fs18\f2 /cdrom/cdrom0\fs20\f0 .)\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1320\sl260 If you are running your Linux/Solaris session as \b root\b0 , you can continue by double-clicking the \f1 install\f0  icon in the file manager window. This will take you straight to step 8.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1320\sl260\fi-360 \tqr\tx1270\tx1320\tab \lang1024 2.\tab Open a terminal window.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1320\sl260\fi-360 \tqr\tx1270\tx1320\tab 3.\tab In the terminal window, type\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1320\sl260 \b\fs18\lang1033\f2  su \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1320\sl260 \b0\fs20\f0 at the command prompt and press ENTER.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1320\sl260 You will be prompted for the \b root\b0  password.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1320\sl260\fi-360 \tqr\tx1270\tx1320\tab \lang1024 4.\tab Type the password of the \b root\b0  user and press ENTER.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1320\sl260 \lang1033 You will be returned to the command prompt.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1320\sl260\fi-360 \tqr\tx1270\tx1320\tab \lang1024 5.\tab At the command prompt, type\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1320\sl260 \b\fs18\lang1033\f2  mount \i\fs16 path_of_your_system's_CD-ROM_mount_point\i0\fs18  \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1320\sl260 \b0\fs20\f0 (In Linux the path of the CD-ROM mount point is usually \fs18\f2 /mnt/cdrom\fs20\f0 , in Solaris it is usually \fs18\f2 /cdrom/cdrom0\fs20\f0  or something similar.) For example:\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1320\sl260 \b\fs18\f2  mount /mnt/cdrom \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1320\sl260 \b0\fs20\f0 Press ENTER.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1320\sl260 If your version of Linux or Solaris automounts CD-ROMs, you can ignore this step.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1320\sl260\fi-360 \tqr\tx1270\tx1320\tab \lang1024 6.\tab Change directory to your system's CD-ROM mount point. For example, Linux users should type\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1320\sl260 \b\fs18\lang1033\f2  cd /mnt/cdrom \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1320\sl260 \b0\fs20\f0 and press ENTER.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1320\sl260\fi-360 \tqr\tx1270\tx1320\tab \lang1024 7.\tab Type\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1320\sl260 \b\fs18\lang1033\f2 ./install\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1320\sl260 \b0\fs20\f0 and press ENTER.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1320\sl260\fi-360 \tqr\tx1270\tx1320\tab \lang1024 8.\tab \b Solaris users. \b0 The installation program requires Perl to be installed on your system. If Perl is not found (for example, if you are running the default Solaris 7 system), you will be prompted to install it:\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1320\sl234 \fs18\f2 Perl is not installed or is not in your command path.\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
  Do you want to install Perl now ? (y/N)\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1320\sl260 \fs20\lang1033\f0 Type Y, ENTER to install Perl or N, ENTER to abort installation.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1320\sl260\fi-360 \tqr\tx1270\tx1320\tab \lang1024 9.\tab A dialog box opens containing information on the licences used by the Alligata Server package (see Figure 4.1).\hyphpar0\par\pard\li1320{\field\flddirty{\*\fldinst INCLUDEPICTURE "licences.ps" }{\fldrslt }}\par\pard\sb100\li1320\sl260 \lang1033 Figure 4.1: Licence dialog box\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1320\sl260 If you agree with the terms of the listed licence agreements, click 'I Agree'.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1320\sl260\fi-360 \tqr\tx1270\tx1320\tab \lang1024 10.\tab The main installation dialog box appears (see Figure 4.2).\hyphpar0\par\pard\li1320{\field\flddirty{\*\fldinst INCLUDEPICTURE "maininst.ps" }{\fldrslt }}\par\pard\sb100\li1320\sl260 \lang1033 Figure 4.2: Main Alligata Server installation dialog box\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1320\sl260 \b  Note: \b0 the Alligata Server installation program detects whether each item is already installed on your system. Items already installed do not appear in the installation dialog box. For example, if your computer already contains an installation of the Apache server, the option 'Apache Web server' will not appear in the dialog box.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1320\sl260 In the dialog box, select the items you want to install. See Figure 4.3 for an explanation of the options in the installation dialog box.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb180\s4\li960\sl312 \b\fs24\lang1024\f1 Figure 4.3(a) Global Options \keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li1320\sl260 \b0\fs20\f0  \f1 Install path \f0 \keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1720\sl260\fi-400 \tx1720 \~\tab Shows the directory into which the Alligata Server program files are installed. Defaults to \fs18\f2 /usr/share/alligata\fs20\f0  in Linux, and \fs18\f2 /opt/TGLBallig\fs20\f0  in Solaris. The install path cannot be changed.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li1320\sl260  \f1 Binary directory \f0 \keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1720\sl260\fi-400 \tx1720 \~\tab Shows the directory into which the Alligata Server's binary files are installed. Defaults to \fs18\f2 /usr/bin\fs20\f0 , and cannot be changed.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb180\s4\li960\sl312 \b\fs24\f1 Figure 4.3(b) Installation Options \keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li1320\sl260 \b0\fs20\f0  \f1 Alligata Gateway (Kannel core) \f0 \keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1720\sl260\fi-400 \tx1720 \~\tab Installs the Alligata Server on the current computer, including the Bearer Box, one WAP Box and one SMS Box. If you want to install extra WAP and SMS Boxes on other computers, see Section 6.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li1320\sl260  \f1 Start the Bearer Box at boot \f0 \keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1720\sl260\fi-400 \tx1720 \~\tab If selected, causes the Alligata Server's Bearer Box to be started automatically every time the computer is booted.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li1320\sl260  \f1 Start the WAP Box at boot \f0 \keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1720\sl260\fi-400 \tx1720 \~\tab If selected, causes the WAP Box to be started automatically every time the computer is booted.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li1320\sl260  \f1 Start the SMS Box at boot \f0 \keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1720\sl260\fi-400 \tx1720 \~\tab If selected, causes the SMS Box to be started automatically every time the computer is booted.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li1320\sl260  \f1 Alligata example sites \f0 \keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1720\sl260\fi-400 \tx1720 \~\tab Installs the Alligata Server example WAP sites. In Linux, these are installed into \fs18\f2 /home/httpd/alligata/wml\fs20\f0 . In Solaris 7, they are installed into \fs18\f2 /usr/local/apache/alligata/wml\fs20\f0 . In Solaris 8, they are installed into \fs18\f2 /etc/apache/alligata/wml\fs20\f0 .\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1720\sl260 \lang1033 Section 12 provides a guide to viewing, using and understanding the example sites.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li1320\sl260 \lang1024  \f1 Apache Web server \f0 \keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1720\sl260\fi-400 \tx1720 \~\tab Installs the Apache Web server. You will need Apache, or a similar HTTP server, to view the example sites.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li1320\sl260  \f1 Development files for the Apache Web server (Linux only) \f0 \keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1720\sl260\fi-400 \tx1720 \~\tab Installs the Apache development package. You will need this package if you plan to extend Apache, for example to write a new module.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1720\sl260 \lang1033 If the computer already contains an installation of Apache, the Alligata Server installation program automatically updates the Apache configuration for WML and Wireless Bitmap (WBMP) file formats.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li1320\sl260 \lang1024  \f1 Apache manuals (Linux only) \f0 \keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1720\sl260\fi-400 \tx1720 \~\tab Installs the Apache manuals into the directory \fs18\f2 /home/httpd/html/manual\fs20\f0 . To view the index page, open the file \fs18\f2 index.html\fs20\f0  in a Web browser such as Netscape or Lynx.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li1320\sl260  \f1 PHP language \f0 \keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1720\sl260\fi-400 \tx1720 \~\tab Installs the PHP scripting language. PHP is used by two of the Alligata Server example WAP sites. It can be very useful for developing Web and WAP applications.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1720\sl260 \lang1033 In the Solaris installation, this option includes the PHP IMAP module (see below).\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li1320\sl260 \lang1024  \f1 IMAP module for the PHP language (Linux only) \f0 \keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1720\sl260\fi-400 \tx1720 \~\tab Installs the IMAP PHP module. This module is used in the example WAP site PAT for liaising with your e-mail server.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li1320\sl260  \f1 Documentation for the PHP language (Linux only) \f0 \keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1720\sl260\fi-400 \tx1720 \~\tab Installs documentation for the PHP language into the directory \fs18\f2 /home/httpd/html/manual/mod/mod_php3\fs20\f0 . To view the index page, open the file \fs18\f2 index.html\fs20\f0  in a Web browser such as Netscape or Lynx.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li1320\sl260  \f1 CGI module for Perl \f0 \keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1720\sl260\fi-400 \tx1720 \~\tab Installs the CGI Perl module, version 2.69. This module facilitates the output of Web files from Perl scripts. It is needed to view the Alligata Server's Perl example site. (The CGI modules supplied with most Linux distributions are too old and will not work with the example Perl site.)\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li1320\sl260  \f1 HTML module for Perl \f0 \keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1720\sl260\fi-400 \tx1720 \~\tab Installs the HTML Perl module. This module is needed to view the Alligata Server's Perl example site.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li1320\sl260  \f1 HTML tables module for Perl \f0 \keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1720\sl260\fi-400 \tx1720 \~\tab Installs the HTML tables Perl module. This module is needed to view the Alligata Server's Perl example site.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li1320\sl260  \f1 WML module for Perl \f0 \keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1720\sl260\fi-400 \tx1720 \~\tab Installs the WML Perl module. This module is needed to view the Alligata Server's Perl example site.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1320\sl260\fi-360 \tqr\tx1270\tx1320\tab 11.\tab Click \f1 Begin Install\f0 .\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1320\sl260 \lang1033 The Alligata Server will be installed on your system. Installation progress information appears in the terminal window from which you launched the installation program.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1320\sl260\fi-360 \tqr\tx1270\tx1320\tab \lang1024 12.\tab When all the files have been copied to your system, the dialog box in Figure 4.4 is displayed.\hyphpar0\par\pard\li1320{\field\flddirty{\*\fldinst INCLUDEPICTURE "nearlydone.ps" }{\fldrslt }}\par\pard\sb100\li1320\sl260 \lang1033 Figure 4.4: 'Thank You' dialog box\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1320\sl260 Click \f1 Launch browser\f0  to go to the 3G LAB Web site, where you can receive information about Alligata Server updates and support.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1320\sl260 Click \f1 Exit\f0  to cancel the installation.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1320\sl260 Click \f1 View Readme\f0  to view the Readme file.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1320\sl260 Click \f1 Continue\f0  to complete the installation.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1320\sl260\fi-360 \tqr\tx1270\tx1320\tab \lang1024 13.\tab If you clicked \f1 Continue\f0 , the dialog box in Figure 4.5 will appear.\hyphpar0\par\pard\li1320{\field\flddirty{\*\fldinst INCLUDEPICTURE "done.ps" }{\fldrslt }}\par\pard\sb100\li1320\sl260 \lang1033 Figure 4.5: 'Install Complete' dialog box\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1320\sl260\fi-360 \tqr\tx1270\tx1320\tab \lang1024 14.\tab Click \f1 Exit\f0  to finish the installation, or \f1 View Readme\f0  to view the Readme file.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1320\sl260\fi-360 \tqr\tx1270\tx1320\tab 15.\tab To start the Alligata Server, follow the instructions in Section 8.1.\hyphpar0\par\sect\sectd\plain\pgwsxn12240\pghsxn15840\marglsxn1440\margrsxn1440\margtsxn1440\margbsxn1920\headery0\footery0\pgndec\titlepg{\headerf\pard\sl-240\sb770\sa430\plain\tqc\tx4680\tqr\tx9360 {}\tab {}\tab {}\par}{\footerf\pard\sl-240\sb770\sa910\plain\tqc\tx4680\tqr\tx9360 {}\tab {}\tab {\i\fs20 \chpgn }\par}{\headerl\pard\sl-240\sb770\sa430\plain\tqc\tx4680\tqr\tx9360 {}\tab {}\tab {\i\fs20 Chapter 5. 5 Installation from the Command Line}\par}{\footerl\pard\sl-240\sb770\sa910\plain\tqc\tx4680\tqr\tx9360 {}\tab {}\tab {\i\fs20 \chpgn }\par}{\headerr\pard\sl-240\sb770\sa430\plain\tqc\tx4680\tqr\tx9360 {}\tab {}\tab {\i\fs20 Chapter 5. 5 Installation from the Command Line}\par}{\footerr\pard\sl-240\sb770\sa910\plain\tqc\tx4680\tqr\tx9360 {}\tab {}\tab {\i\fs20 \chpgn }\par}\pard\sb311\s1\sl539 {\*\bkmkstart _439}{\*\bkmkend _439}\b\fs41\f1 Chapter 5. 5 Installation from the Command Line\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb207\li960\sl260 \b0\fs20\lang1033\f0 If your Linux or Solaris system does not include a graphical user interface, the installation program will install the Alligata Server using the command line procedure described in this section.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 \b\lang1024 To install the Alligata Server from the command line: \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1320\sl260\fi-360 \tqr\tx1270\tx1320\tab \b0 1.\tab Place the Alligata Server CD-ROM in the CD-ROM drive.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1320\sl260\fi-360 \tqr\tx1270\tx1320\tab 2.\tab At the command prompt, type\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1320\sl260 \b\fs18\lang1033\f2  su \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1320\sl260 \b0\fs20\f0 and press ENTER.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1320\sl260 You will be prompted for the \b root\b0  password.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1320\sl260\fi-360 \tqr\tx1270\tx1320\tab \lang1024 3.\tab Type the password of the \b root\b0  user and press ENTER.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1320\sl260 \lang1033 You will be prompted for the \b root\b0  password.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1320\sl260\fi-360 \tqr\tx1270\tx1320\tab \lang1024 4.\tab At the command prompt, type\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1320\sl260 \b\fs18\lang1033\f2  mount \i\fs16 path_of_your_system's_CD-ROM_mount_point \i0\fs18 \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1320\sl260 \b0\fs20\f0 (In Linux the path of the CD-ROM mount point is usually \fs18\f2 /mnt/cdrom\fs20\f0 , in Solaris it is usually \fs18\f2 /cdrom/cdrom0\fs20\f0  or something similar.) For example:\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1320\sl260 \b\fs18\f2  mount /mnt/cdrom \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1320\sl260 \b0\fs20\f0 Press ENTER.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1320\sl260 If your version of Linux or Solaris automounts CD-ROMs, you can ignore this step.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1320\sl260\fi-360 \tqr\tx1270\tx1320\tab \lang1024 5.\tab Change directory to your system's CD-ROM mount point. For example, Linux users should type\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1320\sl260 \b\fs18\lang1033\f2  cd /mnt/cdrom \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1320\sl260 \b0\fs20\f0 and press ENTER.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1320\sl260\fi-360 \tqr\tx1270\tx1320\tab \lang1024 6.\tab Type\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1320\sl260 \b\fs18\lang1033\f2  ./install \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1320\sl260 \b0\fs20\f0 and press ENTER.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1320\sl260\fi-360 \tqr\tx1270\tx1320\tab \lang1024 7.\tab \b Solaris users. \b0 The installation program requires Perl to be installed on your system. If Perl is not found (for example, if you are running the default Solaris 7 system), you will be prompted to install it:\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1320\sl234 \fs18\f2 Perl is not installed or is not in your command path.\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
  Do you want to install Perl now ? (y/N)\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1320\sl260 \fs20\lang1033\f0 Type Y, ENTER to install Perl or N, ENTER to abort installation.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1320\sl260\fi-360 \tqr\tx1270\tx1320\tab \lang1024 8.\tab Information on the licence agreements used by the Alligata Server package will be displayed on the screen, together with the question,\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1320\sl234 \fs18\f2 Do you agree with the licence? [Y/n]\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1320\sl260 \fs20\lang1033\f0 If you agree, type Y, ENTER or just ENTER.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1320\sl260\fi-360 \tqr\tx1270\tx1320\tab \lang1024 9.\tab Information on the installation and binary paths appears on the screen. For example:\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1320\sl234 \fs18\f2 Install path set to: /usr/share/alligata\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
Binary path set to: /usr/bin\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1320\sl260 \b\fs20\lang1033\f0 Note:\b0  you cannot change the installation and binary paths.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1320\sl260\fi-360 \tqr\tx1270\tx1320\tab \lang1024 10.\tab You will be presented with the questions listed in Figure 5.1. In response to each question, type Y, ENTER for 'yes', N, Enter for 'no', or ?, ENTER to receive help.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1320\sl260 \lang1033 Pressing just ENTER in response to a question selects the first (capitalised) option in the square brackets after the question. For example, if the square brackets look like this:\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1320\sl234 \fs18\lang1024\f2 [N/y/?]\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1320\sl260 \fs20\lang1033\f0 then pressing ENTER is equivalent to pressing N, ENTER.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1320\sl260 \b Note:\b0  the Alligata Server installation program detects whether each item is already installed on your system. You will not be asked whether you want to install items that are already installed. For example, if your computer already contains an installation of the Apache server, the question 'Install Apache Web server?' will be omitted.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb180\s4\li960\sl312 \b\fs24\lang1024\f1 Figure 5.1 Command Line Installation Options \keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li1320\sl260 \b0\fs20\f0  \fs18\f2 Install the Alligata Gateway (Kannel core)? [Y/n/?] \fs20\f0 \keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1720\sl260\fi-400 \tx1720 \~\tab Y installs the Alligata Server on the current computer, including the Bearer Box, one WAP Box and one SMS Box. If you want to install extra WAP and SMS Boxes on other computers, see Section 6.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li1320\sl260  \fs18\f2 Start the Bearer Box at boot? [N/y/?] \fs20\f0 \keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1720\sl260\fi-400 \tx1720 \~\tab Y causes the Alligata Server's Bearer Box to be started automatically every time the computer is booted.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li1320\sl260  \fs18\f2 Start the WAP Box at boot [N/y/?] \fs20\f0 \keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1720\sl260\fi-400 \tx1720 \~\tab Y causes the WAP Box to be started automatically every time the computer is booted.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li1320\sl260  \fs18\f2 Start the SMS Box at boot [N/y/?] \fs20\f0 \keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1720\sl260\fi-400 \tx1720 \~\tab Y causes the SMS Box on the current computer to be started automatically every time the computer is booted.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li1320\sl260  \fs18\f2 Install the Alligata examples? [Y/n/?] \fs20\f0 \keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1720\sl260\fi-400 \tx1720 \~\tab Y installs the Alligata Server example WAP sites. In Linux, these are installed into \fs18\f2 /home/httpd/alligata/wml\fs20\f0 . In Solaris 7, they are installed into \fs18\f2 /usr/local/apache/alligata/wml\fs20\f0 . In Solaris 8, they are installed into \fs18\f2 /etc/apache/alligata/wml\fs20\f0 .\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1720\sl260 \lang1033 Section 12 provides a guide to viewing, using and understanding the example sites.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li1320\sl260 \lang1024  \fs18\f2 Install Apache Web server? [N/y/?] \fs20\f0 \keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1720\sl260\fi-400 \tx1720 \~\tab Y installs the Apache Web server. You will need Apache to view the example sites.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li1320\sl260  \fs18\f2 Install Development files for the Apache Web server? [N/y/?] (Linux only) \fs20\f0 \keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1720\sl260\fi-400 \tx1720 \~\tab Y installs the Apache development package. You will need this package if you plan to extend Apache, for example to write a new module.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1720\sl260 \lang1033 If the computer already contains an installation of Apache, the Alligata Server installation program updates the Apache configuration for WML and WBMP file formats.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li1320\sl260 \lang1024  \fs18\f2 Install Apache manuals? [N/y/?] (Linux only) \fs20\f0 \keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1720\sl260\fi-400 \tx1720 \~\tab Y installs the Apache manuals into the directory \fs18\f2 /home/httpd/html/manual\fs20\f0 . To view the index page, open the file \fs18\f2 index.html\fs20\f0  in a Web browser such as Netscape or Lynx.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li1320\sl260  \fs18\f2 Install PHP language? [N/y/?] \fs20\f0 \keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1720\sl260\fi-400 \tx1720 \~\tab Y installs the PHP scripting language. PHP is used by two of the Alligata Server example WAP sites. It can be very useful for developing Web and WAP applications.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1720\sl260 \lang1033 In the Solaris installation, this option includes the PHP IMAP module (see below).\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li1320\sl260 \lang1024  \fs18\f2 Install IMAP module for the PHP language? [N/y/?] (Linux only) \fs20\f0 \keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1720\sl260\fi-400 \tx1720 \~\tab Y installs the IMAP PHP module. This module is used in the example WAP site PAT for liaising with your e-mail server.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li1320\sl260  \fs18\f2 Install Documentation for the PHP language? [N/y/?] (Linux only) \fs20\f0 \keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1720\sl260\fi-400 \tx1720 \~\tab Y installs documentation for the PHP language into the directory \fs18\f2 /home/httpd/html/manual/mod/mod_php3\fs20\f0 . To view the index page, open the file \fs18\f2 index.html\fs20\f0  in a Web browser such as Netscape or Lynx.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li1320\sl260  \fs18\f2 Install CGI module for Perl? [N/y/?] \fs20\f0 \keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1720\sl260\fi-400 \tx1720 \~\tab Y installs the Common Gateway Interface (CGI) Perl module, version 2.69. This module facilitates the output of Web files from Perl scripts. It is needed to view the Alligata Server's Perl example site. (The CGI modules supplied with most Linux distributions are too old and will not work with the example Perl site.)\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li1320\sl260  \fs18\f2 Install HTML module for Perl? [N/y/?] \fs20\f0 \keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1720\sl260\fi-400 \tx1720 \~\tab Y installs the HTML Perl module. This module is needed to view the Alligata Server's Perl example site.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li1320\sl260  \fs18\f2 Install HTML tables modules for Perl? [N/y/?] \fs20\f0 \keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1720\sl260\fi-400 \tx1720 \~\tab Y installs the HTML tables Perl module. This module is needed to view the Alligata Server's Perl example site.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li1320\sl260  \fs18\f2 Install WML module for Perl? [N/y/?] \fs20\f0 \keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1720\sl260\fi-400 \tx1720 \~\tab Y installs the WML Perl module. This module is needed to view the Alligata Server's Perl example site.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1320\sl260\fi-360 \tqr\tx1270\tx1320\tab 11.\tab The following messages (or equivalents) will be displayed on the screen:\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1320\sl234 \fs18\f2 Installing to /usr/share/alligata\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
xMb available, 5Mb will be installed\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
Continue install? [Y/n]\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1320\sl260 \fs20\lang1033\f0 Selecting Y installs the Alligata Server and any other selected items on your system.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1320\sl260 Installation progress is displayed on the screen.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1320\sl260\fi-360 \tqr\tx1270\tx1320\tab \lang1024 12.\tab When installation is complete, the following messages will be displayed on the screen:\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1320\sl234 \fs18\f2 Thank you for installing the Alligata Server, \sa0\par\fi0\sb0
3G LAB's Mobile Internet Server!\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
Please visit our Web site for updates and support.\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
Would you like to launch a Web browser? [Y/n]\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1320\sl260 \fs20\lang1033\f0 To visit the 3G LAB Web site, select Y.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1320\sl260 If you select N, the following message will be displayed on the screen:\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1320\sl234 \fs18\lang1024\f2 Installation complete.\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1320\sl260\fi-360 \tqr\tx1270\tx1320\tab \fs20\f0 13.\tab To start the Alligata Server, follow the instructions in Section 8.1.\hyphpar0\par\sect\sectd\plain\pgwsxn12240\pghsxn15840\marglsxn1440\margrsxn1440\margtsxn1440\margbsxn1920\headery0\footery0\pgndec\titlepg{\headerf\pard\sl-240\sb770\sa430\plain\tqc\tx4680\tqr\tx9360 {}\tab {}\tab {}\par}{\footerf\pard\sl-240\sb770\sa910\plain\tqc\tx4680\tqr\tx9360 {}\tab {}\tab {\i\fs20 \chpgn }\par}{\headerl\pard\sl-240\sb770\sa430\plain\tqc\tx4680\tqr\tx9360 {}\tab {}\tab {\i\fs20 Chapter 6. 6 Installing Multiple WAP and SMS Boxes}\par}{\footerl\pard\sl-240\sb770\sa910\plain\tqc\tx4680\tqr\tx9360 {}\tab {}\tab {\i\fs20 \chpgn }\par}{\headerr\pard\sl-240\sb770\sa430\plain\tqc\tx4680\tqr\tx9360 {}\tab {}\tab {\i\fs20 Chapter 6. 6 Installing Multiple WAP and SMS Boxes}\par}{\footerr\pard\sl-240\sb770\sa910\plain\tqc\tx4680\tqr\tx9360 {}\tab {}\tab {\i\fs20 \chpgn }\par}\pard\sb311\s1\sl539 {\*\bkmkstart _604}{\*\bkmkend _604}\b\fs41\f1 Chapter 6. 6 Installing Multiple WAP and SMS Boxes\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb207\li960\sl260 \b0\fs20\lang1033\f0 The Alligata Server installation program installs a Bearer Box, a WAP Box and an SMS Box onto a personal computer running Linux or Solaris.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 The following instructions assume you have already installed the Alligata Server on a personal computer running Linux or Solaris ('Computer A'). If you have not yet installed the Alligata Server, please follow the instructions in Section 4 for installation from a graphical user interface, or Section 5 for installation from the command line.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 You can add any number of extra WAP and SMS Boxes to an installation of the Alligata Server.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 \b\lang1024 To add a WAP Box to an installation of the Alligata Server: \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1320\sl260\fi-360 \tqr\tx1270\tx1320\tab \b0 1.\tab Connect a computer running Linux or Solaris ('Computer B') to Computer A using TCP/IP.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1320\sl260\fi-360 \tqr\tx1270\tx1320\tab 2.\tab Install a complete copy of the Alligata Server onto Computer B as described in Sections 4 and 5.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1320\sl260\fi-360 \tqr\tx1270\tx1320\tab 3.\tab On Computer B, open the configuration file \fs18\f2 /etc/alligata.conf\fs20\f0  in a text editor such as vi or Emacs.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1320\sl260 \b\lang1033 About the configuration file:\b0  the Alligata Server configuration is a list of variables divided into groups. Each group begins with the line\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1320\sl260 \b\fs18\f2  group = \i\fs16 x\i0\fs18  \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1320\sl260 \b0\fs20\f0 where \i\fs18\f2 x\i0\fs20\f0  is a group identifier. A blank line signifies the end of a group. (Full instructions for configuring the Alligata Server are provided in Section 9.)\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1320\sl260\fi-360 \tqr\tx1270\tx1320\tab \lang1024 4.\tab In the configuration group beginning with the line\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1320\sl260 \b\fs18\lang1033\f2  group = wapbox \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1320\sl260 \b0\fs20\f0 , change the value of the variable\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1320\sl260 \b\fs18\f2  bearerbox-host \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1320\sl260 \b0\fs20\f0 to the IP address or URL of the computer hosting the Bearer Box (that is, computer A). For example:\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1320\sl260 \b\fs18\f2  bearerbox-host = 10.0.0.1 \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1320\sl260\fi-360 \tqr\tx1270\tx1320\tab \b0\fs20\lang1024\f0 5.\tab Repeat steps 1-4 on a new computer for each WAP Box you want to add.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 \b To add an SMS Box to an installation of the Alligata Server: \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1320\sl260\fi-360 \tqr\tx1270\tx1320\tab \b0 1.\tab Connect a computer running Linux or Solaris ('Computer C') to Computer A using TCP/IP.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1320\sl260\fi-360 \tqr\tx1270\tx1320\tab 2.\tab Install a complete copy of the Alligata Server onto Computer C as described in Sections 4 and 5.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1320\sl260\fi-360 \tqr\tx1270\tx1320\tab 3.\tab On Computer C, open the configuration file \fs18\f2 /etc/alligata.conf\fs20\f0  in a text editor such as vi or Emacs.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1320\sl260 \b\lang1033 About the configuration file:\b0  the Alligata Server configuration is a list of variables divided into groups. Each group begins with the line where \i\fs18\f2 x\i0\fs20\f0  is a group identifier. A blank line signifies the end of a group. (Full instructions for configuring the Alligata Server are provided in Section 9.)\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1320\sl260 \b\fs18\f2  group = \i\fs16 x\i0\fs18  \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1320\sl260\fi-360 \tqr\tx1270\tx1320\tab \b0\fs20\lang1024\f0 4.\tab In the configuration group beginning with the line\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1320\sl260 \b\fs18\lang1033\f2  group = smsbox \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1320\sl260 \b0\fs20\f0 , change the value of the variable bearerbox-host to the IP address or URL of the computer hosting the Bearer Box (that is, computer A). For example:\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1320\sl260 \b\fs18\f2  bearerbox-host = 10.0.0.1 \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1320\sl260\fi-360 \tqr\tx1270\tx1320\tab \b0\fs20\lang1024\f0 5.\tab Repeat steps 1-4 on a new computer for each SMS Box you want to add.\hyphpar0\par\sect\sectd\plain\pgwsxn12240\pghsxn15840\marglsxn1440\margrsxn1440\margtsxn1440\margbsxn1920\headery0\footery0\pgndec{\headerl\pard\sl-240\sb770\sa430\plain\tqc\tx4680\tqr\tx9360 {}\tab {}\tab {}\par}{\footerl\pard\sl-240\sb770\sa910\plain\tqc\tx4680\tqr\tx9360 {}\tab {}\tab {}\par}{\headerr\pard\sl-240\sb770\sa430\plain\tqc\tx4680\tqr\tx9360 {}\tab {}\tab {}\par}{\footerr\pard\sl-240\sb770\sa910\plain\tqc\tx4680\tqr\tx9360 {}\tab {}\tab {}\par}\pard\sl20 {\*\bkmkstart _665}{\*\bkmkend _665}\fs20 \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb373\s1\sl647\qc \b\fs49\f1 II. Part Two: Using the Alligata Server\hyphpar0\par\sect\sectd\plain\pgwsxn12240\pghsxn15840\marglsxn1440\margrsxn1440\margtsxn1440\margbsxn1920\headery0\footery0\pgndec\titlepg{\headerf\pard\sl-240\sb770\sa430\plain\tqc\tx4680\tqr\tx9360 {}\tab {}\tab {}\par}{\footerf\pard\sl-240\sb770\sa910\plain\tqc\tx4680\tqr\tx9360 {}\tab {}\tab {\i\fs20 \chpgn }\par}{\headerl\pard\sl-240\sb770\sa430\plain\tqc\tx4680\tqr\tx9360 {}\tab {}\tab {\i\fs20 Chapter 7. 7 Introduction to Part Two}\par}{\footerl\pard\sl-240\sb770\sa910\plain\tqc\tx4680\tqr\tx9360 {}\tab {}\tab {\i\fs20 \chpgn }\par}{\headerr\pard\sl-240\sb770\sa430\plain\tqc\tx4680\tqr\tx9360 {}\tab {}\tab {\i\fs20 Chapter 7. 7 Introduction to Part Two}\par}{\footerr\pard\sl-240\sb770\sa910\plain\tqc\tx4680\tqr\tx9360 {}\tab {}\tab {\i\fs20 \chpgn }\par}\pard\sb311\s1\sl539 {\*\bkmkstart _667}{\*\bkmkend _667}\b\fs41\f1 Chapter 7. 7 Introduction to Part Two\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb207\li960\sl260 \b0\fs20\lang1033\f0 Welcome to Part Two of the \i Alligata Server User Manual, Using the Alligata Server\i0 . This part of the user manual explains how to:\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1160\sl260\fi-200 \tx1160 \fs16\lang1024 \'95\tab \fs20 start, stop and administer the Alligata Server\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1160\sl260\fi-200 \tx1160 \fs16 \'95\tab \fs20 change the Alligata Server's configuration settings\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1160\sl260\fi-200 \tx1160 \fs16 \'95\tab \fs20 view the example WAP sites provided with the Alligata Server\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1160\sl260\fi-200 \tx1160 \fs16 \'95\tab \fs20 create WAP sites using Wireless Markup Language (WML)\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1160\sl260\fi-200 \tx1160 \fs16 \'95\tab \fs20 create dynamic WAP sites using the Perl scripting language\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1160\sl260\fi-200 \tx1160 \fs16 \'95\tab \fs20 create dynamic WAP sites using the PHP scripting language\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1160\sl260\fi-200 \tx1160 \fs16 \'95\tab \fs20 use the Alligata Server's SMS features to deliver World Wide Web and other content to mobile phones\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1160\sl260\fi-200 \tx1160 \fs16 \'95\tab \fs20 use the Alligata Server to send SMS messages from a computer workstation\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1160\sl260\fi-200 \tx1160 \fs16 \'95\tab \fs20 configure a mobile phone for a WAP service 'over the air' using the Alligata Server's SMS features\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 \lang1033 This part of the user manual assumes you have installed the Alligata Server successfully on a computer workstation running Linux or Solaris. If you have not yet installed the Alligata Server, please follow the installation instructions in Sections 4 to 6.\hyphpar0\par\sect\sectd\plain\pgwsxn12240\pghsxn15840\marglsxn1440\margrsxn1440\margtsxn1440\margbsxn1920\headery0\footery0\pgndec\titlepg{\headerf\pard\sl-240\sb770\sa430\plain\tqc\tx4680\tqr\tx9360 {}\tab {}\tab {}\par}{\footerf\pard\sl-240\sb770\sa910\plain\tqc\tx4680\tqr\tx9360 {}\tab {}\tab {\i\fs20 \chpgn }\par}{\headerl\pard\sl-240\sb770\sa430\plain\tqc\tx4680\tqr\tx9360 {}\tab {}\tab {\i\fs20 Chapter 8. 8 Running the Alligata Server}\par}{\footerl\pard\sl-240\sb770\sa910\plain\tqc\tx4680\tqr\tx9360 {}\tab {}\tab {\i\fs20 \chpgn }\par}{\headerr\pard\sl-240\sb770\sa430\plain\tqc\tx4680\tqr\tx9360 {}\tab {}\tab {\i\fs20 Chapter 8. 8 Running the Alligata Server}\par}{\footerr\pard\sl-240\sb770\sa910\plain\tqc\tx4680\tqr\tx9360 {}\tab {}\tab {\i\fs20 \chpgn }\par}\pard\sb311\s1\sl539 {\*\bkmkstart _691}{\*\bkmkend _691}\b\fs41\f1 Chapter 8. 8 Running the Alligata Server\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb207\li960\sl260 \fs20\lang1033\f0 Note:\b0  to run an Alligata Server command line command, you must be logged in as \b root\b0 . Therefore, before using any of the command line commands in this section, change to \b root\b0  as follows:\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1320\sl260\fi-360 \tqr\tx1270\tx1320\tab \lang1024 1.\tab At the command prompt, type su and press ENTER.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1320\sl260 \lang1033 You will be prompted for the password of \b root\b0 .\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1320\sl260\fi-360 \tqr\tx1270\tx1320\tab \lang1024 2.\tab Type the password of \b root\b0  and press ENTER.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1320\sl260 \lang1033 You will be returned to the command prompt as \b root\b0 .\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 Once the Alligata Server is running, its user will change from \b root\b0  to that specified by the user variable in the Core group of the configuration file. By default, this user is \b alligata\b0 .\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb259\s2\sl449 {\*\bkmkstart _710}{\*\bkmkend _710}\b\fs34\lang1024\f1 8.1 Starting the Alligata Server\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb173\li960\sl260 \b0\fs20\lang1033\f0 Each box in the Alligata Server needs to be started separately. The Bearer Box must be started before any of the WAP or SMS Boxes. There are three methods of starting the Alligata Server.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 To start the Alligata Server:\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb216\s3\li960\sl374 {\*\bkmkstart _714}{\*\bkmkend _714}\b\fs28\lang1024\f1 8.1.1 Method 1\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb144\li960\sl260 \b0\fs20\lang1033\f0 If, when you installed the Alligata Server, you selected 'Start the Bearer Box at boot', 'Start the WAP Box at boot' or 'Start the SMS Box at boot', then the relevant box will start automatically each time you boot up the computer.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb216\s3\li960\sl374 {\*\bkmkstart _717}{\*\bkmkend _717}\b\fs28\lang1024\f1 8.1.2 Method 2\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\s4\li960\sl312 {\*\bkmkstart _719}{\*\bkmkend _719}\fs24 Linux users\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb120\li960\sl260 \b0\fs20\lang1033\f0 At the command prompt, type\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 \b\fs18\f2  /etc/rc.d/init.d/\i\fs16 box\i0\fs18  start \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 \b0\fs20\f0 where \i\fs18\f2 box\i0\fs20\f0  is one of the strings \b\fs18\f2 bearerbox\b0\fs20\f0 , \b\fs18\f2 wapbox\b0\fs20\f0  or \b\fs18\f2 smsbox\b0\fs20\f0 . Press ENTER.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 The procedure to start the Bearer Box, WAP Box and SMS Box on a single Linux workstation is therefore as follows:\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1320\sl260\fi-360 \tqr\tx1270\tx1320\tab \lang1024 1.\tab To start the Bearer Box, type\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1320\sl260 \b\fs18\lang1033\f2  /etc/rc.d/init.d/bearerbox start \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1320\sl260 \b0\fs20\f0 at the command prompt and press ENTER.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1320\sl260 The Bearer Box will start up.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1320\sl260\fi-360 \tqr\tx1270\tx1320\tab \lang1024 2.\tab To start the WAP Box, type\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1320\sl260 \b\fs18\lang1033\f2  /etc/rc.d/init.d/wapbox start \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1320\sl260 \b0\fs20\f0 at the command prompt and press ENTER.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1320\sl260 The WAP Box will start up.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1320\sl260\fi-360 \tqr\tx1270\tx1320\tab \lang1024 3.\tab To start the SMS Box, type\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1320\sl260 \b\fs18\lang1033\f2  /etc/rc.d/init.d/smsbox start \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1320\sl260 \b0\fs20\f0 at the command prompt and press ENTER.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1320\sl260 The SMS Box will start up.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\s4\li960\sl312 {\*\bkmkstart _750}{\*\bkmkend _750}\b\fs24\lang1024\f1 Solaris users\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb120\li960\sl260 \b0\fs20\lang1033\f0 At the command prompt, type\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 \b\fs18\f2  /etc/init.d/ \i\fs16 box\i0\fs18  start \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 \b0\fs20\f0 where \i\fs18\f2 box\i0\fs20\f0  is one of the strings \b\fs18\f2 bearerbox\b0\fs20\f0 , \b\fs18\f2 wapbox\b0\fs20\f0  or \b\fs18\f2 smsbox\b0\fs20\f0 . Press ENTER.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 The procedure to start the Bearer Box, WAP Box and SMS Box on a single Linux workstation is therefore as follows:\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1320\sl260\fi-360 \tqr\tx1270\tx1320\tab \lang1024 1.\tab To start the Bearer Box, type\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1320\sl260 \b\fs18\lang1033\f2  /etc/init.d/bearerbox start \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1320\sl260 \b0\fs20\f0 at the command prompt and press ENTER.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1320\sl260 The Bearer Box will start up.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1320\sl260\fi-360 \tqr\tx1270\tx1320\tab \lang1024 2.\tab To start the WAP Box, type\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1320\sl260 \b\fs18\lang1033\f2  /etc/init.d/wapbox start \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1320\sl260 \b0\fs20\f0 at the command prompt and press ENTER.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1320\sl260 The WAP Box will start up.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1320\sl260\fi-360 \tqr\tx1270\tx1320\tab \lang1024 3.\tab To start the SMS Box, type\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1320\sl260 \b\fs18\lang1033\f2  /etc/init.d/smsbox start \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1320\sl260 \b0\fs20\f0 at the command prompt and press ENTER.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1320\sl260 The SMS Box will start up.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb216\s3\li960\sl374 {\*\bkmkstart _781}{\*\bkmkend _781}\b\fs28\lang1024\f1 8.1.3 Method 3\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb144\li960\sl260 \b0\fs20\lang1033\f0 This method of starting the Alligata Server allows you to enter command line options that customise the way each box runs. Using this method, the procedure to start up the Bearer Box, WAP Box and SMS Box on a single workstation is as follows:\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1320\sl260\fi-360 \tqr\tx1270\tx1320\tab \lang1024 1.\tab To start the Bearer Box, type\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1320\sl260 \b\fs18\lang1033\f2  bearerbox \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1320\sl260 \b0\fs20\f0 at the command prompt and press ENTER. You can use command line options to override some of the settings in the configuration file. See \i Command Line Options\i0  below.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1320\sl260 The Bearer Box will start up.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1320\sl260\fi-360 \tqr\tx1270\tx1320\tab \lang1024 2.\tab To start the WAP Box, type\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1320\sl260 \b\fs18\lang1033\f2  wapbox \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1320\sl260 \b0\fs20\f0 at the command prompt and press ENTER. You can use command line options to override some of the settings in the configuration file. See \i Command Line Options\i0  below.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1320\sl260 The WAP Box will start up.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1320\sl260\fi-360 \tqr\tx1270\tx1320\tab \lang1024 3.\tab To start the SMS Box, type\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1320\sl260 \b\fs18\lang1033\f2  smsbox \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1320\sl260 \b0\fs20\f0 at the command prompt and press ENTER. You can use command line options to override some of the settings in the configuration file. See \i Command Line Options\i0  below.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1320\sl260 The SMS Box will start up.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 For each box, you can use a different configuration file from the default file by including the path and name of the relevant file after the command. For example:\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 \b\fs18\f2  bearerbox /etc/alligata2.conf \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\s4\li960\sl312 {\*\bkmkstart _809}{\*\bkmkend _809}\fs24\lang1024\f1 Command Line Options\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb120\li960\sl260 \b0\fs20\lang1033\f0 If you start up the Alligata Server using Method 3, you can include options in the command line you use to start up each box. The format for command line options is\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 \b\fs18\f2  \i\fs16 box option value\i0\fs18  \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 \b0\fs20\f0 For example:\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 \b\fs18\f2  bearerbox -v 2 \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 \b0\fs20\f0 The available command line options are shown in Figure 8.1. \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl260 {\*\bkmkstart _819}{\*\bkmkend _819}\b Table 8-1. Figure 8.1: Alligata Server command line options\sa100\keepn\par\trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx5160\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20\lang1033  \b\fs18\f2 -F \i\fs16 file_name\i0\fs18  \b0\fs20\f0 \sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20\lang1033 Log file name. Does not override the log file name setting in the configuration file, but outputs to both files. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx5160\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20\lang1033  \b\fs18\f2 -V \i\fs16 number_0-4\i0\fs18  \b0\fs20\f0 \sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20\lang1033 Verbosity level for output to the log file specified with -F. 0 provides most detail, 4 least. The default value is 1. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx5160\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20\lang1033  \b\fs18\f2 -S\b0\fs20\f0  \sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20\lang1033 Suspended. Starts the Alligata Server in suspended state. See Section 3.3.1 for details. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx5160\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20\lang1033  \b\fs18\f2 -I\b0\fs20\f0  \sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20\lang1033 Isolated. Starts the Alligata Server in isolated state. See Section 3.3.1 for details.\sa60\cell \row \pard\plain\sl-1\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb259\s2\sl449 {\*\bkmkstart _841}{\*\bkmkend _841}\b\fs34\f1 8.2 Administering the Alligata Server\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb216\s3\li960\sl374 {\*\bkmkstart _843}{\*\bkmkend _843}\fs28 8.2.1 Administering the Alligata Server from the Command Line\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb144\li960\sl260 \b0\fs20\lang1033\f0 Each Alligata Server box understands four administration commands that can be entered from the Linux or Solaris command line. These commands must be in the following formats.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 \b\lang1024 Linux users:. \fs18\f2 /etc/rc.d/init.d/\i\fs16 box command\i0\fs18  \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 \fs20\f0 Solaris users:. \fs18\f2 /etc/init.d/\i\fs16 box command \i0\fs18 \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 \b0\fs20\lang1033\f0 Replace box with one of the strings bearerbox, wapbox or smsbox. For example:\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 \b\fs18\f2  /etc/rc.d/init.d/wapbox restart \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 \b0\fs20\f0 The available command line commands are shown in Figure 8.2.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl260 {\*\bkmkstart _860}{\*\bkmkend _860}\b\lang1024 Table 8-2. Figure 8.2: Alligata Server command line administration commands\sa100\keepn\par\trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx5160\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  \b\fs18\f2 start\b0\fs20\f0  \sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 Starts the specified box. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx5160\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  \b\fs18\f2 restart\b0\fs20\f0  \sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 Shuts down the specified box, then immediately restarts it. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx5160\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  \b\fs18\f2 stop\b0\fs20\f0  \sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 Shuts down the specified box.\sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx5160\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  \b\fs18\f2 status\b0\fs20\f0  \sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 Outputs a summary of processes currently being run by the specified box.\sa60\cell \row \pard\plain\sl-1\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\s4\li960\sl312 {\*\bkmkstart _880}{\*\bkmkend _880}\b\f1 8.2.2 Administering the Alligata Server Using HTTP\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb120\li960\sl260 \b0\fs20\lang1033\f0 You can use a set of HTTP commands to set the Alligata Server's program state (see Section 3.3.1) while it is running. You can also use the \b\fs18\f2 status\b0\fs20\f0  command to retrieve information about the Alligata Server's current activity. Figure 8.3 lists the Alligata Server's HTTP administration commands.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 \b\lang1024 To send the Alligata Server an HTTP administration command: \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1160\sl260\fi-200 \tx1160 \b0\fs16 \'95\tab \fs20 In the 'Location' box of a Web browser, type the command using the following syntax:\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1160\sl260 \b\fs18\lang1033\f2  http://\i\fs16 hostname:port\i0\fs18 /cgi-bin/\i\fs16 command\i0\fs18 ?password=\i\fs16 password\i0\fs18  \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1160\sl260  http://localhost:13004/cgi-bin/suspend?password=PAPaya \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1160\sl260 \b0\fs20\f0 The port number (13004 in the example) must be the same as that defined by the admin-port variable in the Core configuration group (see Section 9.1). The command must be one of the five HTTP administration commands listed in Figure 8.3. The password must be that defined by the admin-password variable in the Core configuration group. (Note that the status command requires no password.)\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1160\sl260 To save having to type a whole URL for each command, you can create an HTML form. Here is one for the \b\fs18\f2 shutdown\b0\fs20\f0  command:\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1160\sl234 \fs18\lang1024\f2 <form name="httpadmin" method="get" \sa0\par\fi0\sb0
action="http://localhost:13004/cgi-bin/shutdown">\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
Enter Alligata Server administration password:\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
<br>\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
<input type="text" name="password" value="">\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
<input type="submit" value="Shut down the Alligata Server">\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
</form>\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li1160\sl260 {\*\bkmkstart _901}{\*\bkmkend _901}\b\fs20\f0 Table 8-3. Figure 8.3: Alligata Server HTTP administration commands\sa100\keepn\par\trowd\trleft1160 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx5260\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li300\ri100\sl260 \fs20  \b\fs18\f2 status\b0\fs20\f0  \sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li300\ri100\sl260 \fs20 Retrieves the following information about the Alligata Server's current activity: the program state; the total number of data packets queuing in the system; the total number of WAP Box and SMS Box connections. No password is required. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft1160 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx5260\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li300\ri100\sl260 \fs20  \b\fs18\f2 suspend\b0\fs20\f0  \sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li300\ri100\sl260 \fs20 Sets the Alligata Server's state to 'suspended'. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft1160 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx5260\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li300\ri100\sl260 \fs20  \b\fs18\f2 isolate\b0\fs20\f0  \sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li300\ri100\sl260 \fs20 Sets the Alligata Server's state to 'isolated'. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft1160 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx5260\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li300\ri100\sl260 \fs20  \b\fs18\f2 resume\b0\fs20\f0  \sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li300\ri100\sl260 \fs20 Sets the Alligata Server's state to 'running', if it has been suspended or isolated. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft1160 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx5260\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li300\ri100\sl260 \fs20  \b\fs18\f2 shutdown\b0\fs20\f0  \sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li300\ri100\sl260 \fs20 Sets the Alligata Server's state to 'shutdown'. The shutdown process, once begun, cannot be stopped - though the Alligata Server's \b\fs18\f2 status\b0\fs20\f0  can still be queried. If a second \b\fs18\f2 shutdown\b0\fs20\f0  command is sent, the Alligata Server will close down immediately, regardless of whether any packets are still held in queues.\sa60\cell \row \pard\plain\sl-1\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\s4\li960\sl312 {\*\bkmkstart _927}{\*\bkmkend _927}\b\f1 8.2.3 Monitoring the Alligata Server Using \fs21\f2 less\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb120\li960\sl260 \b0\fs20\lang1033\f0 You can monitor the Alligata Server's activity by opening the log files using the UNIX less command (for example, \b\fs18\f2 less /var/log/alligata/bearerbox.log\b0\fs20\f0 ). To watch a log file update while the Alligata Server is running, press SHIFT+F from within \b\fs18\f2 less\b0\fs20\f0 .\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb259\s2\sl449 {\*\bkmkstart _933}{\*\bkmkend _933}\b\fs34\lang1024\f1 Shutting down an Alligata Server box\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb173\li960\sl260 \b0\fs20\lang1033\f0 There are two methods of shutting down an Alligata Server box.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 To shut down an Alligata Server box:\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb216\s3\li960\sl374 {\*\bkmkstart _937}{\*\bkmkend _937}\b\fs28\lang1024\f1 8.3.1 Method 1\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb144\li960\sl260 \fs20\f0 Linux users. \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1160\sl260\fi-200 \tx1160 \b0\fs16 \'95\tab \fs20 From the command line, type\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1160\sl260 \b\fs18\lang1033\f2  /etc/rc.d/init.d/\i\fs16 box\i0\fs18  stop \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1160\sl260 \b0\fs20\f0 replacing \i\fs18\f2 box\i0\fs20\f0  with the relevant box name, \b\fs18\f2 bearerbox\b0\fs20\f0 , \b\fs18\f2 wapbox\b0\fs20\f0  or \b\fs18\f2 smsbox\b0\fs20\f0 . For example:\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1160\sl260 \b\fs18\f2  /etc/rc.d/init.d/wapbox stop \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 \fs20\lang1024\f0 Solaris users. \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1160\sl260\fi-200 \tx1160 \b0\fs16 \'95\tab \fs20 From the command line, type\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1160\sl260 \b\fs18\lang1033\f2  /etc/init.d/\i\fs16 box\i0\fs18  stop \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1160\sl260 \b0\fs20\f0 replacing \i\fs18\f2 box\i0\fs20\f0  with the relevant box name, \b\fs18\f2 bearerbox\b0\fs20\f0 , \b\fs18\f2 wapbox\b0\fs20\f0  or \b\fs18\f2 smsbox\b0\fs20\f0 . For example:\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1160\sl260 \b\fs18\f2  /etc/rc.d/init.d/wapbox stop \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb216\s3\li960\sl374 {\*\bkmkstart _971}{\*\bkmkend _971}\fs28\lang1024\f1 8.3.2 Method 2\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb144\li1160\sl260\fi-200 \tx1160 \b0\fs16\f0 \'95\tab \fs20 Send the Alligata Server an HTTP \b\fs18\f2 shutdown\b0\fs20\f0  administration command (see Section 8.2.2).\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 \lang1033 Note that this method can only be used to shut down the Bearer Box, not individual WAP and SMS Boxes.\hyphpar0\par\sect\sectd\plain\pgwsxn12240\pghsxn15840\marglsxn1440\margrsxn1440\margtsxn1440\margbsxn1920\headery0\footery0\pgndec\titlepg{\headerf\pard\sl-240\sb770\sa430\plain\tqc\tx4680\tqr\tx9360 {}\tab {}\tab {}\par}{\footerf\pard\sl-240\sb770\sa910\plain\tqc\tx4680\tqr\tx9360 {}\tab {}\tab {\i\fs20 \chpgn }\par}{\headerl\pard\sl-240\sb770\sa430\plain\tqc\tx4680\tqr\tx9360 {}\tab {}\tab {\i\fs20 Chapter 9. 9 Configuring the Alligata Server}\par}{\footerl\pard\sl-240\sb770\sa910\plain\tqc\tx4680\tqr\tx9360 {}\tab {}\tab {\i\fs20 \chpgn }\par}{\headerr\pard\sl-240\sb770\sa430\plain\tqc\tx4680\tqr\tx9360 {}\tab {}\tab {\i\fs20 Chapter 9. 9 Configuring the Alligata Server}\par}{\footerr\pard\sl-240\sb770\sa910\plain\tqc\tx4680\tqr\tx9360 {}\tab {}\tab {\i\fs20 \chpgn }\par}\pard\sb311\s1\sl539 {\*\bkmkstart _978}{\*\bkmkend _978}\b\fs41\f1 Chapter 9. 9 Configuring the Alligata Server\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb207\li960\sl260 \b0\fs20\lang1033\f0 The Alligata Server is installed with a default configuration that allows you to use it as a WAP gateway. If you want to customise its WAP features, or implement SMS messaging services, you need to make changes to the configuration file.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 All the configuration data for the Alligata Server is held in a single file. By default this file is called \fs18\f2 alligata.conf\fs20\f0  and is held in the directory \fs18\f2 /etc\fs20\f0 . If you want to use a different configuration file, specify the path and file after the relevant command when you start up a box. (To do this, you must start the box using Method 3 - see Section 8.1.3.) For example:\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 \b\fs18\f2  bearerbox /etc/alligata2.conf \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 \b0\fs20\f0 An example Alligata Server configuration file is shown in Figure 9.1.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 Figure 9.1: Example Alligata Server configuration:\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl234 \fs18\lang1024\f2 # Example Alligata Server configuration\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
