workstation via HTTP. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx2640\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx4320\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx6000\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx7680\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 OTA\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 No\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 No\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  \b\fs18\f2 otaconfig\b0\fs20\f0  \sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 Provides settings for over-the-air (OTA) configuration of client devices.\sa60\cell \row \pard\plain\sl-1\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl260 \fs20\lang1033 The rest of this section consists of a full list of all the variables that can be used in the Alligata Server configuration file. Variables are arranged by group.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb259\s2\sl449 {\*\bkmkstart _1125}{\*\bkmkend _1125}\b\fs34\lang1024\f1 9.1 Core Configuration Variables (Core group)\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb173\li960\sl260 \b0\fs20\lang1033\f0 The Core group provides general configuration information for the Alligata Server. This information is used by the Bearer Box.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl260 \lang1024  \fs18\f2 group = core \fs20\f0 \keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260\fi-400 \tx1360 \~\tab Group. Introduces the Core group. (Required.)\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl260  \fs18\f2 user = \i\fs16 user_name\i0\fs18  \fs20\f0 \keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260\fi-400 \tx1360 \~\tab The user name under which the Alligata Server is run. The Alligata Server automatically switches to this user name after it has started. (You must be logged in as \b root\b0  to start the Alligata Server manually. See Section 8 for instructions on starting the Alligata Server.)\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260 \lang1033 Setting \fs18\f2 user\fs20\f0  to \fs18\f2 root\fs20\f0  is strongly discouraged, as it could compromise the security of the system.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260 Example:\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260 \fs18\f2  user = alligata \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl260 \fs20\lang1024\f0  \fs18\f2 admin-port = \i\fs16 port_number\i0\fs18  \fs20\f0 \keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260\fi-400 \tx1360 \~\tab Administration Port. Gives the port number on which the Bearer Box listens to HTTP administration commands. It can be any value between 1024 and 65535. (It may be under 1024 if you are running the Alligata Server as a \b root\b0  process, but it is not recommended that you do this.) It is not the same as the HTTP port of the local World Wide Web server. (Required.)\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260 \lang1033 Example:\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260 \fs18\f2  admin-port = 13004 \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl260 \fs20\lang1024\f0  \fs18\f2 admin-password = \i\fs16 password\i0\fs18  \fs20\f0 \keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260\fi-400 \tx1360 \~\tab Administration Password. Defines the password for HTTP administration commands (except the status command, which does not require a password). See Section 8.2.2 for HTTP administration instructions. (Required.)\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260 \lang1033 Example:\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260 \fs18\f2  admin-password = PAPaya \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl260 \fs20\lang1024\f0  \fs18\f2 smsbox-port = \i\fs16 port_number\i0\fs18  \fs20\f0 \keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260\fi-400 \tx1360 \~\tab SMS Box Port. Defines the port number to which SMS Boxes connect. It can be any value between 1024 and 65535. (It may be under 1024 if the \fs18\f2 user\fs20\f0  variable is set to \fs18\f2 root\fs20\f0 , but this is not advised.)\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260 \lang1033 This variable is required if the Alligata Server is to handle SMS traffic. Example:\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260 \fs18\f2  smsbox-port = 13005 \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl260 \fs20\lang1024\f0  \fs18\f2 wapbox-port = \i\fs16 port_number\i0\fs18  \fs20\f0 \keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260\fi-400 \tx1360 \~\tab WAP Box Port. Defines the port number to which WAP Boxes connect. It can be any value between 1024 and 65535. (It may be under 1024 if the \fs18\f2 user\fs20\f0  variable is set to \fs18\f2 root\fs20\f0 , but this is not advised.)\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260 \lang1033 This variable is required if the Alligata Server is to handle WAP traffic. Example:\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260 \fs18\f2  wapbox-port = 13002 \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl260 \fs20\lang1024\f0  \fs18\f2 wdp-interface-name = \i\fs16 IP_address\i0\fs18  \fs20\f0 \keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260\fi-400 \tx1360 \~\tab If the Alligata Server's host machine has multiple network cards, specifies the IP address of the card from which User Datagram Protocol (UDP) WAP packets are accepted. If set to \fs18\f2 0.0.0.0\fs20\f0 , accepts packets from any IP address.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260 \lang1033 Example:\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260 \fs18\f2  wdp-interface-name = 10.0.0.3 \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl260 \fs20\lang1024\f0  \fs18\f2 log-file = \i\fs16 file_name\i0\fs18  \fs20\f0 \keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260\fi-400 \tx1360 \~\tab Log File. Specifies a file to which to output a log report of the Bearer Box's activity. If the specified file already exists, new output will be added to the end of the file.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260 \lang1033 If a log file is also defined in the command line when the Bearer Box is started up, then the log report will be output to both files (as well as to the standard output). See Section 8.1.3 for command line options.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260 If \fs18\f2 log-file\fs20\f0  is not set in the configuration file, it defaults to \fs18\f2 /var/log/alligata/bearerbox.log\fs20\f0 .\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260 Example:\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260 \fs18\f2  log-file = /alliglog/bearer.log \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl260 \fs20\lang1024\f0  \fs18\f2 log-level = \i\fs16 number_0-4\i0\fs18  \fs20\f0 \keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260\fi-400 \tx1360 \~\tab Log Level. Sets the level of information to be recorded in the log file. Settings are as follows (with 0 providing the most detail and 4 the least):\sa100\par\trowd\trleft1360 \clvertalt\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\li60\ri60\sl260 \fs20 0 Debug\cell \row \trowd\trleft1360 \clvertalt\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\li60\ri60\sl260 \fs20 1 Information\cell \row \trowd\trleft1360 \clvertalt\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\li60\ri60\sl260 \fs20 2 Warning \cell \row \trowd\trleft1360 \clvertalt\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\li60\ri60\sl260 \fs20 3 Error \cell \row \trowd\trleft1360 \clvertalt\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\li60\ri60\sl260 \fs20 4 Panic \cell \row \pard\plain\sl-1\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260 \fs20\lang1033 If \fs18\f2 log-level\fs20\f0  is not defined in the configuration file, it defaults to a value of 1.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260 Example:\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260 \fs18\f2  log-level = 0 \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl260 \fs20\lang1024\f0  \fs18\f2 box-deny-ip = \i\fs16 List_of_IP_addresses\i0\fs18  \fs20\f0 \keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\li960\sl260  \fs18\f2 box-allow-ip = \i\fs16 List_of_IP_addresses\i0\fs18  \fs20\f0 \keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260\fi-400 \tx1360 \~\tab Denied and allowed Box IP List. These variables list the IP addresses of WAP or SMS Boxes to which the Bearer Box may, or may not, forward packets. These variables allow you to prevent third parties from intercepting TCP packets as they leave the Bearer Box.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260 \lang1033 Addresses in the Denied and Allowed Box IP Lists are separated by semicolons. An asterisk * can be used as a wildcard in place of any single group of digits, so *.*.*.* matches any IP address.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260 Examples:\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260 \fs18\f2  box-deny-ip = *.*.*.* \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260  box-allow-ip = 10.0.0.6;10.0.0.7 \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl260 \fs20\lang1024\f0  \fs18\f2 admin-deny-ip = \i\fs16 List_of_IP_addresses\i0\fs18  \fs20\f0 \keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\li960\sl260  \fs18\f2 admin-allow-ip = \i\fs16 List_of_IP_addresses\i0\fs18  \fs20\f0 \keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260\fi-400 \tx1360 \~\tab Administration Denied and Allowed IP Lists. These variables list IP addresses to be denied and allowed HTTP administration access to the Alligata Server. Addresses are formatted in the same way as in \fs18\f2 box-deny-ip\fs20\f0  and \fs18\f2 box-allow-ip\fs20\f0 .\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260 \lang1033 Examples:\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260 \fs18\f2  admin-deny-ip = *.*.*.* \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260  admin-allow-ip = 10.0.0.6;10.0.0.7 \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl260 \fs20\lang1024\f0  \fs18\f2 unified-prefix = \i\fs16 List_of_telephone_code_prefixes\i0\fs18  \fs20\f0 \keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260\fi-400 \tx1360 \~\tab Unified Prefix. Lists substitutions for telephone code prefixes in SMS messages. Substitutions are applied to the 'sender' and 'recipient' fields in incoming and outgoing SMS messages respectively, and ensure that all equivalent numbers are formatted consistently for SMSCs.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260 \lang1033 The format for each substitution is \i\fs18\f2 r,p1,p2...\i0\fs20\f0  where \i\fs18\f2 r\i0\fs20\f0  is the replacement prefix, and \i\fs18\f2 p1, p2\i0\fs20\f0 , etc. are the prefixes to be replaced.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260 You can define any number of substitutions in the configuration file by separating each definition with a semicolon ;.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260 Example:\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260 \fs18\f2  unified-prefix = 0044, 0; 00555, 05, 050 \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl260 \fs20\lang1024\f0  \fs18\f2 white-list = \i\fs16 URL\i0\fs18  \fs20\f0 \keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260\fi-400 \tx1360 \~\tab White List. Links to a file listing telephone numbers of approved senders of SMS messages to the Alligata Server. SMS messages from numbers not in this list will be automatically discarded.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260 \lang1033 The white list file should contain telephone numbers separated by carriage returns.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260 \b Note:\b0  only the last nine digits are read, so it is theoretically possible for unauthorised senders with numbers nearly identical to those in the white list to gain access to the Alligata Server. The likelihood of this occurring is, however, minimal.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260 If the configuration file contains a \fs18\f2 white-list\fs20\f0 , it should not contain a \fs18\f2 black-list\fs20\f0 .\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260 Example:\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260 \fs18\f2  white-list = http://www.awebsite.net/alligata/white.htm \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl260 \fs20\lang1024\f0  \fs18\f2 black-list = \i\fs16 URL\i0\fs18  \fs20\f0 \keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260\fi-400 \tx1360 \~\tab Black List. Links to a file listing telephone numbers of prohibited senders of SMS messages to the Alligata Server. SMS messages from numbers on this list are automatically discarded.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260 \lang1033 The black list file should contain telephone numbers separated by carriage returns.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260 As with the white list, only the last nine digits of the number are read by the Alligata Server.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260 If the configuration file contains a \fs18\f2 black-list\fs20\f0 , it should not contain a \fs18\f2 white-list\fs20\f0 .\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260 Example:\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260 \fs18\f2  black-list = http://www.awebsite.net/alligata/black.htm \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl260 \fs20\lang1024\f0  \fs18\f2 http-proxy-host = \i\fs16 IP_address/URL\i0\fs18  \fs20\f0 \keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260\fi-400 \tx1360 \~\tab HTTP Proxy Host. Identifies a proxy server to be used for HTTP requests.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260 \lang1033 Example:\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260 \fs18\f2  http-proxy-host = 10.0.0.8 \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl260 \fs20\lang1024\f0  \fs18\f2 http-proxy-port = \i\fs16 port_number\i0\fs18  \fs20\f0 \keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260\fi-400 \tx1360 \~\tab HTTP Proxy Port. Identifies the port on the proxy server to which the Alligata Server connects.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260 \lang1033 Example:\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260 \fs18\f2  http-proxy-port = 13005 \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl260 \fs20\lang1024\f0  \fs18\f2 http-proxy-exceptions = \i\fs16 list_of_IP_addresses/URLs\i0\fs18  \fs20\f0 \keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260\fi-400 \tx1360 \~\tab HTTP Proxy Exceptions. Lists Internet addresses to be reached directly rather than via the proxy server (for example, computers on a local network).\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260 \lang1033 Multiple values should be separated by spaces. Example:\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260 \fs18\f2  http-proxy-exceptions = 10.0.0.3 10.0.0.4 \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl260 \fs20\lang1024\f0  \fs18\f2 access-log = \i\fs16 file_name\i0\fs18  \fs20\f0 \keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260\fi-400 \tx1360 \~\tab Access Log File. Specifies a file to which to output a log report of SMS messages processed by the Bearer Box.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260 \lang1033 Example:\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260 \fs18\f2  access-log = bearerbox.access \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb259\s2\sl449 {\*\bkmkstart _1338}{\*\bkmkend _1338}\b\fs34\lang1024\f1 9.2 WAP Configuration Variables (WAP Box group)\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb173\li960\sl260 \b0\fs20\lang1033\f0 The WAP Box group provides the configuration for the WAP Box or Boxes. There cannot be more than one WAP Box group in the main configuration file. If you have installed several WAP Boxes on different computers (see Section 6), each WAP Box should use a separate configuration file.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 If you are using the Alligata Server for WAP services, remember also to set the wapbox-port variable in the Core group (see Section 9.1).\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 If you are not using the Alligata Server for WAP services, then no WAP Box group is necessary.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl260 \lang1024  \fs18\f2 group = wapbox \fs20\f0 \keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260\fi-400 \tx1360 \~\tab Group. Introduces the Wap box group. (Required.)\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl260  \fs18\f2 bearerbox-host = \i\fs16 IP_address/URL\i0\fs18  \fs20\f0 \keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260\fi-400 \tx1360 \~\tab Bearer Box Host. Identifies the computer hosting the Bearer Box. (Required.)\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260 \lang1033 Examples:\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260 \fs18\f2  bearerbox-host = localhost \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260  bearerbox-host = 100.100.100.100 \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260  bearerbox-host = acomputer.asite.net \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl260 \fs20\lang1024\f0  \fs18\f2 log-file = \i\fs16 file_name\i0\fs18  \fs20\f0 \keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260\fi-400 \tx1360 \~\tab Log File. Specifies a file to which to output a log report of the WAP Box's activity. If the specified file already exists, new output is added to the end of it.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260 \lang1033 If a log file is also defined in the command line when the WAP Box is started up (see Section 8.1.3), then the log report will be output to both files.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260 If \fs18\f2 log-file\fs20\f0  is not set in the configuration file, it defaults to \fs18\f2 /var/log/alligata/wapbox.log\fs20\f0 .\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260 Example:\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260 \fs18\f2  log-file = /alliglog/wap.log \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl260 \fs20\lang1024\f0  \fs18\f2 log-level = \i\fs16 number_0-4\i0\fs18  \fs20\f0 \keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260\fi-400 \tx1360 \~\tab Log Level. Sets the level of information to be recorded in the log file. Settings are as follows (with 0 providing the most detail and 4 the least):\sa100\par\trowd\trleft1360 \clvertalt\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\li60\ri60\sl260 \fs20 0 Debug \cell \row \trowd\trleft1360 \clvertalt\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\li60\ri60\sl260 \fs20 1 Information \cell \row \trowd\trleft1360 \clvertalt\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\li60\ri60\sl260 \fs20 2 Warning \cell \row \trowd\trleft1360 \clvertalt\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\li60\ri60\sl260 \fs20 3 Error \cell \row \trowd\trleft1360 \clvertalt\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\li60\ri60\sl260 \fs20 4 Panic \cell \row \pard\plain\sl-1\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260 \fs20\lang1033 If \fs18\f2 log-level\fs20\f0  is not defined in the configuration file, it defaults to a value of 1.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260 Example:\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260 \fs18\f2  log-level = 0 \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl260 \fs20\lang1024\f0  \fs18\f2 timer-freq = \i\fs16 value_in_seconds\i0\fs18  \fs20\f0 \keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260\fi-400 \tx1360 \~\tab Timer Frequency. If not set in the configuration file, defaults to a value of 1.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260 \lang1033 Example:\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260 \fs18\f2  timer-freq = 3 \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl260 \fs20\lang1024\f0  \fs18\f2 map-url = \i\fs16 url#1 url#2 \i0\fs18 \fs20\f0 \keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260\fi-400 \tx1360 \~\tab URL Mapping. On incoming URL requests, replaces \i\fs18\f2 url#1\i0\fs20\f0  with \i\fs18\f2 url#2\i0\fs20\f0 . Asterisks can be used as wildcards to right-truncate either or both of the URLs.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260 \lang1033 Example:\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260 \fs18\f2  map-url = xlwa/* http://www.extremely_longwinded _wapsite_address.com/* \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260 \fs20\f0 This example enables a mobile device user to gain access to the URL \fs18\f2 http://www.extremely_longwinded_wapsite_address.com/contacts.wml\fs20\f0  simply by keying \fs18\f2 xlwa/contacts.wml\fs20\f0 .\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260 \b Note:\b0  only one map-url is allowed in the configuration. If you need to map more than one URL, use \fs18\f2 map-url-0\fs20\f0 , etc. together with \fs18\f2 map-url-max\fs20\f0 .\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl260 \lang1024  \fs18\f2 map-url-max = \i\fs16 number\i0\fs18  \fs20\f0 \keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260\fi-400 \tx1360 \~\tab URL Mapping Maximum. Sets the maximum number of URL mappings to be defined in the configuration file.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260 \lang1033 If no maximum is specified, the Alligata Server allows a default maximum of ten mappings, numbered from 0 to 9 (see \fs18\f2 map-url-0, map-url-1\fs20\f0 , etc.).\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260 Example:\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260 \fs18\f2  map-url-max = 8 \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl260 \fs20\lang1024\f0  \fs18\f2 map-url-0 = \i\fs16 url#1 url#2 \i0\fs18 \fs20\f0 \keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\li960\sl260  \fs18\f2 map-url-1 = \i\fs16 url#1 url#2 , etc. \i0\fs18 \fs20\f0 \keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260\fi-400 \tx1360 \~\tab Multiple URL Mappings. Define multiple URL mappings, unlike \fs18\f2 map-url\fs20\f0 , which can define only one mapping. Further URL mappings would be defined by the variables \fs18\f2 map-url-2\fs20\f0 , \fs18\f2 map-url-3\fs20\f0  and so on, up to the maximum specified in \fs18\f2 map-url-max\fs20\f0 .\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260 \lang1033 Formatting syntax is as for map-url.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260 Examples:\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260 \fs18\f2  map-url-0 = xlwa/* http://www.extremely_longwinded_wapsite_address.com/* \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260  map-url-1 = olwa/* http://www.outrageously_longwinded_wapsite_address.com/* \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl260 \fs20\lang1024\f0  \fs18\f2 device-home = \i\fs16 url\i0\fs18  \fs20\f0 \keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260\fi-400 \tx1360 \~\tab Device Home. For Openwave's UP.Browser, defines the client's WML home deck. The actual URL supplied by the Openwave microbrowser is \fs18\f2 DEVICE:home\fs20\f0 . Both parts of the variable definition are implicitly right-truncated.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260 \lang1033 Thus,\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260 \fs18\f2  device-home = http://www.devicehome.com/ \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260 \fs20\f0 is equivalent to\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260 \fs18\f2  is equivalent to \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260 \fs20\f0 Example:\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260 \fs18\f2  device-home = http://www.devicehome.com/ \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb259\s2\sl449 {\*\bkmkstart _1465}{\*\bkmkend _1465}\b\fs34\lang1024\f1 9.3 SMS Configuration Variables (SMS Box, SMSC, SMS Service and Send SMS User groups)\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb216\s3\li960\sl374 {\*\bkmkstart _1467}{\*\bkmkend _1467}\fs28 9.3.1 SMS Box group\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb144\li960\sl260 \b0\fs20\lang1033\f0 The SMS Box group provides the configuration for the SMS Box. There cannot be more than one SMS Box group in the main configuration file. If you have installed several SMS Boxes on different computers (see Section 6), each SMS Box should use a separate configuration file.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 If you are using the Alligata Server for SMS messaging, remember also to set the \fs18\f2 smsbox-port\fs20\f0  variable in the Core group (see Section 9.1).\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 If you are not using the Alligata Server for SMS messaging, then no SMS Box group is necessary.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl260 \lang1024  \fs18\f2 group = smsbox \fs20\f0 \keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260\fi-400 \tx1360 \~\tab Group. Introduces the SMS Box group. (Required.)\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl260  \fs18\f2 bearerbox-host = \i\fs16 IP_address/URL\i0\fs18  \fs20\f0 \keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260\fi-400 \tx1360 \~\tab Bearer Box Host. Identifies the computer hosting the Bearer Box. (Required.)\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260 \lang1033 Examples:\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260 \fs18\f2  bearerbox-host = localhost \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260  bearerbox-host = 10.0.0.1 \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260  bearerbox-host = acomputer.asite.net \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl260 \fs20\lang1024\f0  \fs18\f2 sendsms-port = \i\fs16 port_number\i0\fs18  \fs20\f0 \keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260\fi-400 \tx1360 \~\tab Send SMS Port. Specifies the port via which SMS messages sent from a workstation by HTTP are received (see Section 9.3.4).\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260 \lang1033 The port number can be any value between 1024 and 65535. (It may be under 1024 if the \fs18\f2 user\fs20\f0  variable in the Core configuration group is set to root, but this is not advised.)\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260 Example:\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260 \fs18\f2  sendsms-port = 13001 \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl260 \fs20\lang1024\f0  \fs18\f2 \i\fs16 telephone_number\i0\fs18  \fs20\f0 \keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260\fi-400 \tx1360 \~\tab Global Sender Number. Specifies the number shown as the sender's telephone number in outgoing SMS messages.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260 \lang1033 Note that most SMSCs will automatically replace this number with their own.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260 Example:\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260 \fs18\f2  global-sender = 44998123456 \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl260 \fs20\lang1024\f0  \fs18\f2 log-file = \i\fs16 file_name\i0\fs18  \fs20\f0 \keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260\fi-400 \tx1360 \~\tab Log File. Specifies a file to which to output a log report of the SMS Box's activity. If the specified file already exists, new output will be added to the end of it.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260 \lang1033 If a log file is also defined in the command line when the SMS Box is started up, then the log report will be output to both files.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260 If \fs18\f2 log-file\fs20\f0  is not set in the configuration file, it defaults to \fs18\f2 /var/log/alligata/smsbox.log\fs20\f0 .\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260 Example:\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260 \fs18\f2  log-file = /alliglog/sms.log \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl260 \fs20\lang1024\f0  \fs18\f2 log-level = \i\fs16 number_0-4\i0\fs18  \fs20\f0 \keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260\fi-400 \tx1360 \~\tab Log Level. Sets the level of information to be recorded in the log file. Settings are as follows (with 0 providing the most detail and 4 the least):\sa100\par\trowd\trleft1360 \clvertalt\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\li60\ri60\sl260 \fs20 0 Debug \cell \row \trowd\trleft1360 \clvertalt\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\li60\ri60\sl260 \fs20 1 Information \cell \row \trowd\trleft1360 \clvertalt\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\li60\ri60\sl260 \fs20 2 Warning \cell \row \trowd\trleft1360 \clvertalt\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\li60\ri60\sl260 \fs20 3 Error \cell \row \trowd\trleft1360 \clvertalt\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\li60\ri60\sl260 \fs20 4 Panic \cell \row \pard\plain\sl-1\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260 \fs20\lang1033 If \fs18\f2 log-level\fs20\f0  is not defined in the configuration file, it defaults to a value of 1.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260 Example:\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260 \fs18\f2  log-level = 0 \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl260 \fs20\lang1024\f0  \fs18\f2 access-log = \i\fs16 file_name\i0\fs18  \fs20\f0 \keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260\fi-400 \tx1360 \~\tab Access Log File. Specifies a file to which to output a log report of SMS messages processed by the SMS Box.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260 \lang1033 Example:\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260 \fs18\f2  access-log = smsbox.access \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb216\s3\li960\sl374 {\*\bkmkstart _1552}{\*\bkmkend _1552}\b\fs28\lang1024\f1 9.3.2 SMSC group\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb144\li960\sl260 \b0\fs20\lang1033\f0 For each SMSC with which the Alligata Server is in communication, you must configure an SMSC group. If you intend to use the Alligata Server for managing SMS messages, remember also to set the \fs18\f2 smsbox-port\fs20\f0  variable in the Core group (see Section 9.1).\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 Which variables you need to include in an SMSC group depends on the protocol used by the relevant SMSC. Figure 9.3 provides a list of which protocols support which variables. For full details of each protocol, please consult the documentation for the relevant SMSC.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 AT is the protocol that the Alligata Server uses to send and receive SMS messages via a GSM wireless modem using AT commands. Using a GSM modem circumvents the need for a subscription to an SMSC. Certain mobile phones that incorporate a GSM modem can also be linked to a computer workstation and used to send and receive SMS messages in the same way as a modem.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 Modems supported by the Alligata Server include the Wavecom WM02 and the Nokia Premicell. Phones that can be used as modems include the Nokia 7110.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 If you do not want to use the Alligata Server's SMS features, you do not need any SMSC groups in your configuration file.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl260 {\*\bkmkstart _1560}{\*\bkmkend _1560}\b\lang1024 Table 9-3. Figure 9.3: SMSC group protocol-specific variables\sa100\keepn\par\trowd\trleft960\trhdr \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx3760\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx6560\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \b\fs20\f1 SMSC protocol\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \b\fs20\f1 Value of \fs18\f2 smsc\fs20\f1  variable\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \b\fs20\f1 Variables. Those in parentheses are optional.\sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx3760\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx6560\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 CIMD\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  \fs18\f2 cimd\fs20\f0  \sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  \fs18\f2 host, port, smsc-username, smsc-password\fs20\f0  \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx3760\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx6560\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 CIMD2\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  \fs18\f2 cimd2\fs20\f0  \sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  \fs18\f2 host, port, smsc-username, smsc-password, (keepalive)\fs20\f0  \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx3760\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx6560\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 EMI\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  \fs18\f2 emi\fs20\f0  \sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  \fs18\f2 phone, device, smsc-username, smsc-password\fs20\f0  \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx3760\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx6560\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 EMI IP\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  \fs18\f2 emi_ip\fs20\f0  \sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  \fs18\f2 host, port, smsc-username, smsc-password, connect-allow-ip, (receive-port), (our-port)\fs20\f0  \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx3760\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx6560\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 SMPP IP\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  \fs18\f2 smpp\fs20\f0  \sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  \fs18\f2 host, port, smsc-password, system-id, system-type, address-range, (receive-port)\fs20\f0  \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx3760\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx6560\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 SEMA X.28\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  \fs18\f2 sema\fs20\f0  \sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  \fs18\f2 device, smsc-nua, home-nua, (wait-report)\fs20\f0  \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx3760\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx6560\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 OIS\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  \fs18\f2 ois\fs20\f0  \sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  \fs18\f2 host, port, receive-port, (ois-debug-level)\fs20\f0  \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx3760\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx6560\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 AT\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  \fs18\f2 at\fs20\f0  \sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  \fs18\f2 device, modemtype, (pin)\fs20\f0  \sa60\cell \row \pard\plain\sl-1\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl260 \fs20  \fs18\f2 group = smsc \fs20\f0 \keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260\fi-400 \tx1360 \~\tab Group. Introduces the SMSC group. (Required.)\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl260  \fs18\f2 smsc = \i\fs16 smsc_type\i0\fs18  \fs20\f0 \keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260\fi-400 \tx1360 \~\tab SMSC Type. Identifies the SMSC type. Currently available types are \fs18\f2 cimd, cimd2, emi, emi_ip, smpp, sema, at\fs20\f0  and \fs18\f2 ois\fs20\f0 . See Figure 9.3. (Required.)\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260 \lang1033 Example:\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260 \fs18\f2  smsc = emi \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl260 \fs20\lang1024\f0  \fs18\f2 smsc-id = \i\fs16 string\i0\fs18  \fs20\f0 \keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260\fi-400 \tx1360 \~\tab SMSC Identifier. Provides a name by which the SMSC can be referred to elsewhere. This name can be used to associate the SMSC with particular SMS services and users, via the \fs18\f2 accepted-smsc, forced-smsc\fs20\f0  and \fs18\f2 default-smsc\fs20\f0  variables in the SMS Service and Send SMS User groups (see Sections 9.3.3 and 9.3.4).\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260 \lang1033 The value of \fs18\f2 smsc-id\fs20\f0  may contain any alphanumeric characters, and is case-insensitive. Example:\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260 \fs18\f2  smsc-id = smsc1 \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl260 \fs20\lang1024\f0  \fs18\f2 denied-prefix = \i\fs16 list_of_telephone_code_prefixes\i0\fs18  \fs20\f0 \keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260\fi-400 \tx1360 \~\tab Denied Telephone Prefix List. Lists prefixes of telephone numbers to which messages are not to be sent via this SMSC.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260 \lang1033 Numbers in the list are separated by semicolons ;.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260 The \fs18\f2 denied-prefix\fs20\f0  variable may be needed because some SMSCs do not allow messages to be sent to users on different telephone networks to their own. \fs18\f2 denied-prefix\fs20\f0  and \fs18\f2 preferred-prefix\fs20\f0  can also be useful to direct calls to the cheapest SMSC according to their destination code.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260 Example:\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260 \fs18\f2  denied-prefix = 0898;0999 \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl260 \fs20\lang1024\f0  \fs18\f2 preferred-prefix = \i\fs16 list_of_telephone_code_prefixes\i0\fs18  \fs20\f0 \keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260\fi-400 \tx1360 \~\tab Preferred Telephone Prefix List. Lists prefixes of telephone numbers that should, if possible, be sent messages via this SMSC.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260 \lang1033 Numbers in the list are separated by semicolons ;.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260 It is possible for a telephone code prefix to be listed as preferred in more than one SMSC group. Where this is the case, the Alligata Server chooses one of these SMSC groups at random.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260 Where a telephone number's prefix is not listed as preferred in any SMSC group, the Alligata Server chooses a group at random from all available SMSC groups. Example:\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260 \fs18\f2  preferred-prefix = 01487;01223 \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl260 \fs20\lang1024\f0  \fs18\f2 host = \i\fs16 IP_address/host_name\i0\fs18  \fs20\f0 \keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260\fi-400 \tx1360 \~\tab Examples:\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260 \fs18\lang1033\f2  host = 10.0.0.20 \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260  host = acomputer.acompany.net \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl260 \fs20\lang1024\f0  \fs18\f2 port = \i\fs16 port_number\i0\fs18  \fs20\f0 \keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260\fi-400 \tx1360 \~\tab SMSC Host Port. Identifies the port number on the SMSC host machine used for communication. (Required.)\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260 \lang1033 Example:\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260 \fs18\f2 port = 13004\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl260 \fs20\lang1024\f0  \fs18\f2 our-port = \i\fs16 port_number\i0\fs18  \fs20\f0 \keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260\fi-400 \tx1360 \~\tab Local Port. Identifies the port on the Alligata Server's host machine used for communication with the SMSC. Currently only the EMI IP protocol supports this.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260 \lang1033 Example:\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260 \fs18\f2  our-port = 13005 \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl260 \fs20\lang1024\f0  \fs18\f2 receive-port = \i\fs16 port_number\i0\fs18  \fs20\f0 \keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260\fi-400 \tx1360 \~\tab Receive Port. Used with protocols that accept different send and receive ports, namely EMI IP, SMPP IP and OIS.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260 \lang1033 Example:\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260 \fs18\f2  receive-port = 13006 \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl260 \fs20\lang1024\f0  \fs18\f2 smsc-username = \i\fs16 user_name\i0\fs18  \fs20\f0 \keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260\fi-400 \tx1360 \~\tab SMSC User Name. The Alligata Server's account user name on the SMSC's host machine.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260 \lang1033 Example:\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260 \fs18\f2  smsc-username = ally \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl260 \fs20\lang1024\f0  \fs18\f2 smsc-password = \i\fs16 password\i0\fs18  \fs20\f0 \keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260\fi-400 \tx1360 \~\tab SMSC Password. The Alligata Server's account password on the SMSC's host machine.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260 \lang1033 Example:\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260 \fs18\f2  smsc-password = PINEapple \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl260 \fs20\lang1024\f0  \fs18\f2 device = \i\fs16 device_name\i0\fs18  \fs20\f0 \keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260\fi-400 \tx1360 \~\tab System Device. When the Alligata Server is used with a GSM modem or the EMI or X.28 protocols, this identifies the device associated with the modem.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260 \lang1033 Example:\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260 \fs18\f2  device = /dev/ttyS0 \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl260 \fs20\lang1024\f0  \fs18\f2 connect-allow-ip = \i\fs16 list_of_IP_addresses\i0\fs18  \fs20\f0 \keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260\fi-400 \tx1360 \~\tab Allowed IP Connections. Specifies IP addresses of SMSCs to be allowed to connect to the Alligata Server. Used by EMI IP.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260 \lang1033 Example:\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260 \fs18\f2  connect-allow-ip = 10.0.0.20 10.0.0.21 \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl260 \fs20\lang1024\f0  \fs18\f2 smsc_nua = \i\fs16 X.121_address\i0\fs18  \fs20\f0 \keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260\fi-400 \tx1360 \~\tab SMSC address using X.121 protocol. Used by SEMA X.28.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260 \lang1033 Example:\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260 \fs18\f2  smsc_nua = 000001220900 \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl260 \fs20\lang1024\f0  \fs18\f2 home_nua = \i\fs16 X.121_address\i0\fs18  \fs20\f0 \keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260\fi-400 \tx1360 \~\tab Radio PAD (Product Assembler/Disassembler) address using X.121 protocol. Used by SEMA X.28.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260 \lang1033 Example:\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260 \fs18\f2  home_nua = 000001220900 \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl260 \fs20\lang1024\f0  \fs18\f2 wait_report = \i\fs16 digit_(0_or_1)\i0\fs18  \fs20\f0 \keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260\fi-400 \tx1360 \~\tab Wait Report. Used by SEMA X.28.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260 \lang1033 Example:\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260 \fs18\f2  wait_report = 1 \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl260 \fs20\lang1024\f0  \fs18\f2 phone = \i\fs16 telephone_number\i0\fs18  \fs20\f0 \keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260\fi-400 \tx1360 \~\tab The SMSC's telephone number. Used only by EMI.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260 \lang1033 Example:\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260 \fs18\f2  phone = 44999123456 \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl260 \fs20\lang1024\f0  \fs18\f2 keepalive = \i\fs16 number\i0\fs18  \fs20\f0 \keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260\fi-400 \tx1360 \~\tab The frequency of 'alive' messages in seconds. Used only by CIMD2.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl260  \fs18\f2 system_id = \i\fs16 string\i0\fs18  \fs20\f0 \keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260\fi-400 \tx1360 \~\tab System ID. Used only by SMPP IP.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl260  \fs18\f2 system_type = \i\fs16 string\i0\fs18  \fs20\f0 \keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260\fi-400 \tx1360 \~\tab System Type. Used only by SMPP IP.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl260  \fs18\f2 address_range = \i\fs16 range\i0\fs18  \fs20\f0 \keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260\fi-400 \tx1360 \~\tab Address range. Used only by SMPP IP.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl260  \fs18\f2 modemtype = \i\fs16 string\i0\fs18  \fs20\f0 \keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260\fi-400 \tx1360 \~\tab The type of modem being used to send SMS messages by the AT protocol. Modems currently supported are as follows:\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li1360\sl260 {\*\bkmkstart _1811}{\*\bkmkend _1811}\b Table 9-4. Currently supported modems\sa100\keepn\par\trowd\trleft1360\trhdr \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx5360\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li500\ri100\sl260 \b\fs20\f1 Modem\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li500\ri100\sl260 \b\fs20\f1  \fs18\f2 modemtype\fs20\f1  value \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft1360 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx5360\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li500\ri100\sl260 \fs20 Wavecom WM02 \sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li500\ri100\sl260 \fs20  \fs18\f2 wavecom\fs20\f0  \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft1360 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx5360\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li500\ri100\sl260 \fs20 Nokia Premicell\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li500\ri100\sl260 \fs20  \fs18\f2 premicell\fs20\f0  \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft1360 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx5360\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li500\ri100\sl260 \fs20 Siemens M20T\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li500\ri100\sl260 \fs20  \fs18\f2 siemens\fs20\f0  \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft1360 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx5360\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li500\ri100\sl260 \fs20 Siemens TC35\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li500\ri100\sl260 \fs20  \fs18\f2 siemens-tc35\fs20\f0  \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft1360 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx5360\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li500\ri100\sl260 \fs20 Phone with GSM modem\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li500\ri100\sl260 \fs20  \fs18\f2 nokiaphone\fs20\f0  \sa60\cell \row \pard\plain\sl-1\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li1360\sl260 \fs20\lang1033 (The Nokia Premicell does not support user data headers (UDHs), so cannot be used for over-the-air configuration messages.)\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260 Used only by AT.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260 Example:\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260 \fs18\f2  modemtype = wavecom \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl260 \fs20\lang1024\f0  \fs18\f2 pin = \i\fs16 number\i0\fs18  \fs20\f0 \keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260\fi-400 \tx1360 \~\tab Personal Identification Number. An optional variable used only by AT. Use this variable if the SIM card inserted in your GSM modem requires a PIN for activation.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb216\s3\li960\sl374 {\*\bkmkstart _1851}{\*\bkmkend _1851}\b\fs28\f1 9.3.3 SMS Service Group\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb144\li960\sl260 \b0\fs20\lang1033\f0 An SMS Service group defines an action to be performed by the SMS Box in response to a keyword and parameters in incoming SMS messages.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 If you are using the Alligata Server for SMS messaging, remember to include an SMS Box group and at least one SMSC group in the configuration. You also need to set the \fs18\f2 smsbox-port\fs20\f0  variable in the Core group (see Section 9.1).\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 You can include as many SMS Service groups in the configuration as you like. Each group defines a single SMS service.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 For instructions on how to implement SMS services, see Section 13.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl260 \lang1024  \fs18\f2 group = sms-service \fs20\f0 \keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260\fi-400 \tx1360 \~\tab Group. Introduces the SMS Service group. (Required.)\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl260  \fs18\f2 keyword = \i\fs16 word\i0\fs18  \fs20\f0 \keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260\fi-400 \tx1360 \~\tab Service Keyword. Defines the keyword that triggers the service. (Required.)\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260 \lang1033 The keyword can only be a single word, without spaces, and must always be the first word in incoming messages.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260 \fs18\f2 keyword\fs20\f0  is case-sensitive. For example, \fs18\f2 frog, Frog\fs20\f0  and \fs18\f2 FROG\fs20\f0  are treated as different keywords.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260 If \fs18\f2 keyword\fs20\f0  has the value \fs18\f2 default\fs20\f0 , then the SMS Service will be applied to all incoming messages that do not contain recognised keywords.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260 Example:\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260 \fs18\f2  keyword = football \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl260 \fs20\lang1024\f0  \fs18\f2 aliases = \i\fs16 word#1;word#2, etc. \i0\fs18 \fs20\f0 \keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260\fi-400 \tx1360 \~\tab Service Keyword Aliases. Defines alternative keywords that will trigger the service. Multiple aliases must be separated by semicolons ;. Each alias must be a single keyword, containing no spaces.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260 \lang1033 Example:\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260 \fs18\f2  aliases = Football;FOOTBALL \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl260 \fs20\lang1024\f0  \fs18\f2 url = \i\fs16 url\i0\fs18  \fs20\f0 \keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260\fi-400 \tx1360 \~\tab Retrieval URL. Specifies a URL from which to fetch data.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260 \lang1033 The URL can include parameters that are extracted from the incoming SMS message. These parameters are expanded by the SMS Box before the request is sent to the World Wide Web.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260 Possible parameters are as follows:\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li1360\sl260 {\*\bkmkstart _1898}{\*\bkmkend _1898}\b\lang1024 Table 9-5. Possible parameters in the URL template\sa100\keepn\par\trowd\trleft1360 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx5360\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li500\ri100\sl260 \fs20  \fs18\f2 %s\fs20\f0  \sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li500\ri100\sl260 \fs20 The next word from the incoming SMS message, starting with the second word (since the first word is the keyword). Characters that can be ambiguous in URLs are converted into hexadecimal ASCII codes, for example + is converted into %2B. After each %s, the URL parser moves on to the next word in the message. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft1360 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx5360\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li500\ri100\sl260 \fs20  \fs18\f2 %S\fs20\f0  \sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li500\ri100\sl260 \fs20 As \fs18\f2 %s\fs20\f0 , but * is converted to ~ (tilde). This can be useful for users whose mobile phones do not allow ~ to be entered. In addition, potentially ambiguous characters are not encoded. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft1360 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx5360\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li500\ri100\sl260 \fs20  \fs18\f2 %r\fs20\f0  \sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li500\ri100\sl260 \fs20 All the remaining words in the message. For example, if the message is 'foo bar foobar baz', and \fs18\f2 bar\fs20\f0  has already been parsed by a \fs18\f2 %s\fs20\f0 , then \fs18\f2 %r\fs20\f0  denotes \fs18\f2 foobar baz\fs20\f0  (\fs18\f2 foo\fs20\f0 , of course, being the keyword). \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft1360 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx5360\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li500\ri100\sl260 \fs20  \fs18\f2 %a\fs20\f0  \sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li500\ri100\sl260 \fs20 All words in the incoming SMS message, including the first word. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft1360 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx5360\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li500\ri100\sl260 \fs20  \fs18\f2 %t\fs20\f0  \sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li500\ri100\sl260 \fs20 The time the message was sent, formatted as \i\fs18\f2 YYYY-MM-DD[space]HH:MM\i0\fs20\f0 , for example \fs18\f2 2000-07-28 15:56\fs20\f0 . \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft1360 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx5360\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li500\ri100\sl260 \fs20  \fs18\f2 %p\fs20\f0  \sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li500\ri100\sl260 \fs20 The telephone number of the recipient of the SMS message. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft1360 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx5360\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li500\ri100\sl260 \fs20  \fs18\f2 %P\fs20\f0  \sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li500\ri100\sl260 \fs20 The telephone number of the sender of the SMS message. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft1360 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx5360\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li500\ri100\sl260 \fs20  \fs18\f2 %q\fs20\f0  \sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li500\ri100\sl260 \fs20 As \fs18\f2 %p\fs20\f0 , except that a leading 00 is replaced with +. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft1360 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx5360\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li500\ri100\sl260 \fs20  \fs18\f2 %Q\fs20\f0  \sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li500\ri100\sl260 \fs20 As \fs18\f2 %P\fs20\f0 , except that a leading 00 is replaced with +.\sa60\cell \row \pard\plain\sl-1\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li1360\sl260 \fs20\lang1033 These parameters can also be used with the \fs18\f2 file\fs20\f0  and \fs18\f2 text\fs20\f0  variables. See Section 13.1 for examples of how parameters can be used.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260 If an SMS Service group contains a \fs18\f2 url\fs20\f0  variable, it cannot also contain a \fs18\f2 file\fs20\f0  or a \fs18\f2 text\fs20\f0  (see below). There can only be one \fs18\f2 url\fs20\f0  in an SMS Service group.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260 \fs18\f2 url\fs20\f0  can be used in conjunction with prefix and suffix to retrieve a section of a Web page.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260 Example:\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260 \fs18\f2  url = http://www.awebsite.net/cgi/salary?user=%s&password=%s&employee=%r \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl260 \fs20\lang1024\f0  \fs18\f2 file = \i\fs16 file_name\i0\fs18  \fs20\f0 \keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260\fi-400 \tx1360 \~\tab Retrieval File. Specifies a file on a local disk from which to fetch data.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260 \lang1033 The whole of the file is retrieved, except for the last character (usually a line feed).\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260 If an SMS Service group contains a \fs18\f2 file\fs20\f0 , it cannot also contain a \fs18\f2 url\fs20\f0  or a \fs18\f2 text\fs20\f0 . There can only be one \fs18\f2 file\fs20\f0  in an SMS Service group.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260 \fs18\f2 file\fs20\f0  supports all the parameters listed under \fs18\f2 url\fs20\f0 .\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260 Examples:\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260 \fs18\f2  file = /replies/areply.txt \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260  file = /info/%s \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl260 \fs20\lang1024\f0  \fs18\f2 text = \i\fs16 character_string\i0\fs18  \fs20\f0 \keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260\fi-400 \tx1360 \~\tab Reply Text. Specifies a static text message to be sent to the client.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260 \lang1033 If an SMS Service group contains a \fs18\f2 text\fs20\f0 , it cannot also contain a \fs18\f2 url\fs20\f0  or a \fs18\f2 file\fs20\f0 . There can only be one \fs18\f2 text\fs20\f0  in an SMS Service group.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260 \fs18\f2 text\fs20\f0  supports all the parameters listed under \fs18\f2 url\fs20\f0 .\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260 Examples:\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260 \fs18\f2  text = All is well over here. \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260  text = You wrote: %r. \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl260 \fs20\lang1024\f0  \fs18\f2 prefix = \i\fs16 character_string\i0\fs18  \fs20\f0 \keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\li960\sl260  \fs18\f2 suffix = \i\fs16 character_string\i0\fs18  \fs20\f0 \keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260\fi-400 \tx1360 \~\tab Retrieval Prefix and Suffix. Used in conjunction with \fs18\f2 url\fs20\f0 . The Alligata Server retrieves all text between (but not including) the prefix and the suffix in the target Web page. Tags are stripped out.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260 \lang1033 If the target page contains more than one occurrence of the prefix or suffix, then the Alligata Server retrieves everything between the first occurrence of the prefix and the first succeeding occurrence of the suffix.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260 Examples:\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260 \fs18\f2  prefix = <!--begin today's weather--> \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260  <!--end today's weather--> \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl260 \fs20\lang1024\f0  \fs18\f2 faked-sender = \i\fs16 telephone_number\i0\fs18  \fs20\f0 \keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260\fi-400 \tx1360 \~\tab Faked Sender Number. Specifies the number shown as the sender's telephone number in outgoing SMS messages for this service.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260 \fs18\lang1033\f2 faked-sender\fs20\f0  will override any sender numbers specified elsewhere (for example, the \fs18\f2 global-sender\fs20\f0  variable in an SMS Box group).\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260 Note that most SMSCs will replace the faked-sender number with their own.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260 Example:\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260 \fs18\f2  faked-sender = 44998123456 \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl260 \fs20\lang1024\f0  \fs18\f2 max-messages = \i\fs16 number\i0\fs18  \fs20\f0 \keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260\fi-400 \tx1360 \~\tab Maximum Reply Messages. Specifies the maximum number of individual SMS messages allowed in a single reply.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260 \lang1033 If \fs18\f2 max-messages\fs20\f0  is not set in the configuration file, it defaults to a value of 1.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260 If \fs18\f2 max-messages\fs20\f0  is set to 0, then no replies will be sent, other than error messages.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260 Example:\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260 \fs18\f2  max-messages = 8 \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl260 \fs20\lang1024\f0  \fs18\f2 split-chars = \i\fs16 list_of_characters\i0\fs18  \fs20\f0 \keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260\fi-400 \tx1360 \~\tab Message Split Characters. Specifies characters that can be used to split a long outgoing message into several shorter ones.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260 \lang1033 The maximum length of a single SMS message will vary depending on whether it uses a 7-bit or an 8-bit format, and whether it has a user data header (UDH). A 7-bit message usually has a maximum length of 160 characters, an 8-bit message a maximum length of 140 characters.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260 Messages over the maximum length for a single message are split as specified in the configuration file. Where \fs18\f2 split-chars\fs20\f0  is not specified, the Alligata Server uses any character to split the message.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260 The Alligata Server only splits messages where necessary. For example, if the semicolon ; is specified as a split character, then a message of 200 characters containing six semicolons will be split into just two shorter messages, not seven.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260 Example:\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260 \fs18\f2  split-chars = ;:. \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl260 \fs20\lang1024\f0  \fs18\f2 split-suffix = \i\fs16 character_string\i0\fs18  \fs20\f0 \keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260\fi-400 \tx1360 \~\tab Message Split Suffix. Where a long message is split into two or more shorter ones, specifies a string to appear at the end of each message (except the last).\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260 \lang1033 Example:\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260 \fs18\f2  split-suffix = -cont.- \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl260 \fs20\lang1024\f0  \fs18\f2 omit-empty = \i\fs16 a numeric value\i0\fs18  \fs20\f0 \keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260\fi-400 \tx1360 \~\tab Omit Empty Messages. If set to a number other than 0, stops messages containing no data being sent to the user.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260 \lang1033 Example:\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260 \fs18\f2  omit-empty = 1 \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl260 \fs20\lang1024\f0  \fs18\f2 header = \i\fs16 character_string\i0\fs18  \fs20\f0 \keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260\fi-400 \tx1360 \~\tab Reply Header. Specifies a string to appear at the beginning of outgoing messages. In the case of split messages, the string appears on every message.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260 \lang1033 Example:\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260 \fs18\f2  header = Today's weather... \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl260 \fs20\lang1024\f0  \fs18\f2 footer = \i\fs16 character_string\i0\fs18  \fs20\f0 \keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260\fi-400 \tx1360 \~\tab Reply Footer. Specifies a string to appear at the end of outgoing messages. In the case of split messages, the string appears on every message.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260 \lang1033 Example:\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260 \fs18\f2  footer = -- sent by the Alligata Server -- \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl260 \fs20\lang1024\f0  \fs18\f2 accepted-smsc = \i\fs16 list_of_smsc_identifiers\i0\fs18  \fs20\f0 \keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260\fi-400 \tx1360 \~\tab Accepted SMSC identifiers. Only messages from SMSCs with these identifiers (see \fs18\f2 smsc-id\fs20\f0  in Section 9.3.2) are allowed to use this service.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260 \lang1033 Multiple values should be separated by semicolons ;.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260 Example:\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260 \fs18\f2  accepted-smsc = smsc1;smsc2 \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl260 \fs20\lang1024\f0  \fs18\f2 concatenation = \i\fs16 0_or_1\i0\fs18  \fs20\f0 \keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260\fi-400 \tx1360 \~\tab If set to 1, allows concatenation of multiple SMS messages in the client device.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260 \lang1033 Example:\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260 \fs18\f2  concatenation = 1 \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb216\s3\li960\sl374 {\*\bkmkstart _2111}{\*\bkmkend _2111}\b\fs28\lang1024\f1 9.3.4 Send SMS User Group\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb144\li960\sl260 \b0\fs20\lang1033\f0 The Alligata Server allows you to send SMS messages from a computer workstation using HTTP (see Section 13.2). To do this, you must configure at least one Send SMS user account. Each user account is defined in a separate Send SMS User group.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl260 \lang1024  \fs18\f2 group = sendsms-user \fs20\f0 \keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260\fi-400 \tx1360 \~\tab Group. Introduces the Send SMS User group. (Required.)\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl260  \fs18\f2 username = \i\fs16 string\i0\fs18  \fs20\f0 \keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260\fi-400 \tx1360 \~\tab Send SMS User Name. Specifies the account user name. (Required.)\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260 \lang1033 Example:\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260 \fs18\f2  username = colin \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl260 \fs20\lang1024\f0  \fs18\f2 password = \i\fs16 string\i0\fs18  \fs20\f0 \keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260\fi-400 \tx1360 \~\tab Send SMS Password. Specifies the account password. (Required.)\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260 \lang1033 Example:\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260 \fs18\f2  password = AVOcado \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl260 \fs20\lang1024\f0  \fs18\f2 user-deny-ip = \i\fs16 List_of_IP_addresses\i0\fs18  \fs20\f0 \keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260\fi-400 \tx1360 \~\tab Denied IP Addresses. Lists IP addresses from which SMS messages may not be sent using HTTP. Addresses in the list are separated by semicolons. An asterisk * can be used as a wildcard in place of any single group of digits. For example, *.*.*.* matches any IP address.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260 \lang1033 Example:\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260 \fs18\f2  user-deny-ip = 0.0.0.0;10.0.0.6 \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl260 \fs20\lang1024\f0  \fs18\f2 user-allow-ip = \i\fs16 List_of_IP_addresses\i0\fs18  \fs20\f0 \keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260\fi-400 \tx1360 \~\tab Allowed IP Addresses. Lists IP addresses from which SMS messages may be sent using HTTP.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260 \lang1033 Addresses are formatted in the same way as in \fs18\f2 user-deny-ip\fs20\f0 .\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260 user-allow-ip = 0.0.0.0;10.0.0.7\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl260 \lang1024  \fs18\f2 faked-sender = \i\fs16 telephone_number\i0\fs18  \fs20\f0 \keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260\fi-400 \tx1360 \~\tab Faked Sender Number. Specifies the telephone number shown as the sender's on client devices.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260 \lang1033 Note that most SMSCs will replace this number with their own.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260 \fs18\f2 faked-sender\fs20\f0  will override any sender numbers specified elsewhere (for example, the \fs18\f2 global-sender\fs20\f0  variable in the SMS Box group).\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260 Example:\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260 \fs18\f2  faked-sender = 44998123456 \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl260 \fs20\lang1024\f0  \fs18\f2 max-messages = \i\fs16 number\i0\fs18  \fs20\f0 \keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260\fi-400 \tx1360 \~\tab Maximum Messages. Specifies the maximum number of individual SMS messages into which a long message can be split.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260 \lang1033 If \fs18\f2 max-messages\fs20\f0  is not set in the configuration file, it defaults to a value of 1.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260 If \fs18\f2 max-messages\fs20\f0  is set to 0, then no messages will be sent.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260 Example:\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260 \fs18\f2  max-messages = 8 \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl260 \fs20\lang1024\f0  \fs18\f2 split-chars = \i\fs16 list_of_characters\i0\fs18  \fs20\f0 \keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260\fi-400 \tx1360 \~\tab Message Split Characters. Specifies characters that can be used to split a long message into several shorter ones.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260 \lang1033 The maximum length of a message is usually 140 or 160 characters, depending on the SMSC's protocol. Messages over this length are split as specified in \fs18\f2 max-messages\fs20\f0  and \fs18\f2 split-chars\fs20\f0 . If \fs18\f2 split-chars\fs20\f0  is not set, any character is used to split the message.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260 The Alligata Server will only split messages where necessary. For example, if the semicolon ; is specified as a split character, then a message of 200 characters containing six semicolons will be split into just two shorter messages, not seven.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260 Example:\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260 \fs18\f2  split-chars = .,': \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl260 \fs20\lang1024\f0  \fs18\f2 split-suffix = \i\fs16 character_string\i0\fs18  \fs20\f0 \keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260\fi-400 \tx1360 \~\tab Message Split Suffix. Where a long message is split into two or more shorter ones, specifies a string to appear at the end of each message (except the last).\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260 \lang1033 Example:\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260 \fs18\f2  split-suffix = -cont.- \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl260 \fs20\lang1024\f0  \fs18\f2 omit-empty = \i\fs16 number\i0\fs18  \fs20\f0 \keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260\fi-400 \tx1360 \~\tab Omit Empty Messages. If set to a number other than 0, stops messages containing no data being sent to the user.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260 \lang1033 Example:\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260 \fs18\f2  omit-empty = 1 \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl260 \fs20\lang1024\f0  \fs18\f2 header = \i\fs16 character_string\i0\fs18  \fs20\f0 \keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260\fi-400 \tx1360 \~\tab message Header. Specifies a string to appear at the beginning of outgoing messages. In the case of split messages, the string appears on every message.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260 \lang1033 Example:\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260 \fs18\f2  header = Message from your manager \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl260 \fs20\lang1024\f0  \fs18\f2 footer = \i\fs16 character_string\i0\fs18  \fs20\f0 \keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260\fi-400 \tx1360 \~\tab message Footer. Specifies a string to appear at the end of outgoing messages. In the case of split messages, the string appears on every message.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260 \lang1033 Example:\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260 \fs18\f2  footer = -- Sent by Alligata Server -- \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl260 \fs20\lang1024\f0  \fs18\f2 forced-smsc = \i\fs16 smsc_identifier\i0\fs18  \fs20\f0 \keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260\fi-400 \tx1360 \~\tab Forced SMSC Identifier. Forces SMS messages to be sent via the specified SMSC. See \fs18\f2 smsc-id\fs20\f0  in Section 9.3.2.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260 \lang1033 Example:\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260 \fs18\f2  forced-smsc = smsc1;smsc2 \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl260 \fs20\lang1024\f0  \fs18\f2 default-smsc = \i\fs16 smsc_identifier\i0\fs18  \fs20\f0 \keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260\fi-400 \tx1360 \~\tab Default SMSC Identifier. Specifies an SMSC via which messages are to be sent, unless an alternative SMSC is specified in the smsc parameter in the message itself (see Section 13.2.2).\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260 \lang1033 Example:\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260 \fs18\f2  default-smsc = smsc1 \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl260 \fs20\lang1024\f0  \fs18\f2 concatenation = \i\fs16 0_or_1\i0\fs18  \fs20\f0 \keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260\fi-400 \tx1360 \~\tab If set to 1, allows concatenation of multiple SMS messages in the client device.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260 \lang1033 Example:\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260 \fs18\f2  concatenation = 1 \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb259\s2\sl449 {\*\bkmkstart _2260}{\*\bkmkend _2260}\b\fs34\lang1024\f1 9.4 Over-the-air Configuration Variables (OTA Configuration Group)\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb173\li960\sl260 \b0\fs20\lang1033\f0 The OTA Configuration group sets the variables for over-the-air configuration of WAP client devices.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 There are two ways of configuring a client device: from a workstation using an HTTP request, or from the client device via an SMS Service group. See Section 13.3 for instructions on how to configure a client device.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl260 \lang1024  \fs18\f2 group = otaconfig \fs20\f0 \keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260\fi-400 \tx1360 \~\tab Group. Introduces the OTA Configuration group. (Required.)\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl260  \fs18\f2 location = \i\fs16 url\i0\fs18  \fs20\f0 \keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260\fi-400 \tx1360 \~\tab Home URL. The home URL of the client device. (Required.)\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260 \lang1033 Example:\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260 \fs18\f2  location = http://www.awapsite.net/ \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl260 \fs20\lang1024\f0  \fs18\f2 service = \i\fs16 string\i0\fs18  \fs20\f0 \keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260\fi-400 \tx1360 \~\tab Title of the service as it will appear on client devices. (Required.)\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260 \lang1033 Example:\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260 \fs18\f2  service = Acme WAP Service \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl260 \fs20\lang1024\f0  \fs18\f2 ipaddress = \i\fs16 IP_address\i0\fs18  \fs20\f0 \keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260\fi-400 \tx1360 \~\tab IP address of the Alligata Server's Bearer Box.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260 \lang1033 Example:\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260 \fs18\f2  ipaddress = 10.0.0.1 \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl260 \fs20\lang1024\f0  \fs18\f2 phonenumber = \i\fs16 telephone_number\i0\fs18  \fs20\f0 \keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260\fi-400 \tx1360 \~\tab Telephone number via which the client device establishes the point-to-point Protocol (PPP) connection with the Alligata Server.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260 \lang1033 Example:\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260 \fs18\f2  phonenumber = 44998123456 \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl260 \fs20\lang1024\f0  \fs18\f2 bearer = \i\fs16 string\i0\fs18  \fs20\f0 \keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260\fi-400 \tx1360 \~\tab Bearer type, either \fs18\f2 data\fs20\f0  or \fs18\f2 sms\fs20\f0 .\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl260  \fs18\f2 calltype = \i\fs16 string\i0\fs18  \fs20\f0 \keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260\fi-400 \tx1360 \~\tab Call type, either \fs18\f2 isdn\fs20\f0  or \fs18\f2 analogue\fs20\f0 .\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl260  \fs18\f2 connection = \i\fs16 string\i0\fs18  \fs20\f0 \keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260\fi-400 \tx1360 \~\tab Connection type, either \fs18\f2 cont\fs20\f0  (continuous) or \fs18\f2 temp\fs20\f0  (temporary). Defaults to \fs18\f2 cont\fs20\f0 .\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl260  \fs18\f2 pppsecurity = \i\fs16 on\i0\fs18  or \i\fs16 off\i0\fs18  \fs20\f0 \keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260\fi-400 \tx1360 \~\tab Enables CHAP authentication if set to \fs18\f2 on\fs20\f0 . Otherwise enables PAP authentication.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl260  \fs18\f2 authentication = \i\fs16 string\i0\fs18  \fs20\f0 \keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260\fi-400 \tx1360 \~\tab Authentication mode, either normal or secure. secure enables WTLS security. Defaults to normal.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl260  \fs18\f2 login = \i\fs16 user_name\i0\fs18  \fs20\f0 \keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260\fi-400 \tx1360 \~\tab User login name.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260 \lang1033 Example:\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260 \fs18\f2  login = colin \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl260 \fs20\lang1024\f0  \fs18\f2 secret = \i\fs16 password\i0\fs18  \fs20\f0 \keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260\fi-400 \tx1360 \~\tab User password.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260 \lang1033 Example:\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1360\sl260 \fs18\f2  password = KUMquat \hyphpar0\par\sect\sectd\plain\pgwsxn12240\pghsxn15840\marglsxn1440\margrsxn1440\margtsxn1440\margbsxn1920\headery0\footery0\pgndec\titlepg{\headerf\pard\sl-240\sb770\sa430\plain\tqc\tx4680\tqr\tx9360 {}\tab {}\tab {}\par}{\footerf\pard\sl-240\sb770\sa910\plain\tqc\tx4680\tqr\tx9360 {}\tab {}\tab {\i\fs20 \chpgn }\par}{\headerl\pard\sl-240\sb770\sa430\plain\tqc\tx4680\tqr\tx9360 {}\tab {}\tab {\i\fs20 Chapter 10. 10 Creating WAP Documents}\par}{\footerl\pard\sl-240\sb770\sa910\plain\tqc\tx4680\tqr\tx9360 {}\tab {}\tab {\i\fs20 \chpgn }\par}{\headerr\pard\sl-240\sb770\sa430\plain\tqc\tx4680\tqr\tx9360 {}\tab {}\tab {\i\fs20 Chapter 10. 10 Creating WAP Documents}\par}{\footerr\pard\sl-240\sb770\sa910\plain\tqc\tx4680\tqr\tx9360 {}\tab {}\tab {\i\fs20 \chpgn }\par}\pard\sb311\s1\sl539 {\*\bkmkstart _2363}{\*\bkmkend _2363}\b\fs41\f1 Chapter 10. 10 Creating WAP Documents\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb207\li960\sl260 \b0\fs20\lang1033\f0 The Alligata Server allows you to offer users of mobile WAP devices an access point to the Mobile Internet. You do not need to provide any WAP content of your own. However, you will probably want to set up your own WAP services - either static WML decks, or Common Gateway Interface (CGI) applications that retrieve data and format it automatically as WML.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 In order to disseminate information on the Mobile Internet, all you need is space on the Internet from which a standard HTTP server such as Apache can retrieve data. In other words, serving WAP data is almost exactly the same as serving Web data. The only difference from the point of view of the content provider is that the data sent to the client must be in WML rather than HTML format. To all intents and purposes, WAP files are Web files, but in a different format to that understood by PC Web browsers.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 When the HTTP server to which your Internet space is connected receives a request for WAP data, it finds the relevant file and sends it - or, if it is a CGI application, its WML output - to the IP address that requested it. It sends it using HTTP, like a Web file. The IP address to which it sends it is that of a WAP gateway (for example, an installation of the Alligata Server). The gateway compresses the data and sends it over the wireless network to the client mobile device that sent the original WAP request. See Section 3 for more information about what the WAP gateway does.\hyphpar0\par\sect\sectd\plain\pgwsxn12240\pghsxn15840\marglsxn1440\margrsxn1440\margtsxn1440\margbsxn1920\headery0\footery0\pgndec\titlepg{\headerf\pard\sl-240\sb770\sa430\plain\tqc\tx4680\tqr\tx9360 {}\tab {}\tab {}\par}{\footerf\pard\sl-240\sb770\sa910\plain\tqc\tx4680\tqr\tx9360 {}\tab {}\tab {\i\fs20 \chpgn }\par}{\headerl\pard\sl-240\sb770\sa430\plain\tqc\tx4680\tqr\tx9360 {}\tab {}\tab {\i\fs20 Chapter 11. 11 Introduction to WML}\par}{\footerl\pard\sl-240\sb770\sa910\plain\tqc\tx4680\tqr\tx9360 {}\tab {}\tab {\i\fs20 \chpgn }\par}{\headerr\pard\sl-240\sb770\sa430\plain\tqc\tx4680\tqr\tx9360 {}\tab {}\tab {\i\fs20 Chapter 11. 11 Introduction to WML}\par}{\footerr\pard\sl-240\sb770\sa910\plain\tqc\tx4680\tqr\tx9360 {}\tab {}\tab {\i\fs20 \chpgn }\par}\pard\sb311\s1\sl539 {\*\bkmkstart _2368}{\*\bkmkend _2368}\b\fs41\f1 Chapter 11. 11 Introduction to WML\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb207\li960\sl260 \b0\fs20\lang1033\f0 This section gives a summary of Wireless Markup Language (WML). WML is the formatting language used to encode WAP documents. Full details of WML can be found in the official WML specification on the WAP Forum's Web site at \fs18\f2 http://www.wapforum.org\fs20\f0 . here we aim to provide you with enough information to get started with WML and create some basic WML documents of your own. The Alligata Server package includes some example WML files, so that you can see how WML works in practice.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 WML is derived from Hypertext Markup Language (HTML), the tagging system used to format World Wide Web pages; if you are familiar with HTML, you should find WML fairly easy to learn. However, WML is tailored to display information on much smaller display areas than HTML, and to be compact enough to transmit efficiently, in compressed form, over low-bandwidth, high-latency wireless networks.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 Writing WML is straightforward: you can use either a text editor, or one of the increasing number of dedicated WML editors available on the market. To check the formatting of your WML, we recommend you get a WAP phone, or download one of several free WAP phone emulators from the World Wide Web.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 The rest of this section covers the main features of WML.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb259\s2\sl449 {\*\bkmkstart _2375}{\*\bkmkend _2375}\b\fs34\lang1024\f1 11.1 Content and Tags\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb173\li960\sl260 \b0\fs20\lang1033\f0 To begin with, here is some sample WML: the file \fs18\f2 logo.wml\fs20\f0  from the example site 'Static Simple' on the Alligata Server installation CD. If you installed the example WAP sites along with the Alligata Server, you can view this file by selecting 'Static Simple' from the 'Site Examples' menu (a full guide to the example sites is provided in Section 12):\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl234 \fs18\lang1024\f2 <?xml version="1.0"?>\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
 <!DOCTYPE wml PUBLIC "-//WAPFORUM//DTD WML 1.1//EN"   \sa0\par\fi0\sb0
     "http://www.wapforum.org/DTD/wml_1.1.xml">\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
 <wml>\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
    <card id="foo" name="bar" title="Static WML"\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
                    newcontext="true">\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
      <p>\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
      <img src="3glab.wbmp" alt="3G Lab Logo"  align="middle"/>\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
      <br/>\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
      This is static <big>WML</big> text.\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
   </p>\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
  </card>\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
 </wml>\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl260 \fs20\lang1033\f0 This file, like all WML documents, holds two main types of information: \b content\b0  and \b tags\b0 .\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 \b Content\b0  is generally textual information to be displayed on the client's microbrowser. It is encoded as plain text using the Unicode 2.0 character set. The example file's content is the sentence 'This is static WML text'.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 Some characters are not included in the Unicode character set, or are problematic to display because they are used by WML for purposes other than text display (for example the less than < and greater than > symbols, which denote the start and end of tags). Such characters are represented by entity references which begin with an ampersand &, and end with a semicolon ;. Some of the most common entity references in WML are:\sa200\par\trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx5160\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  \b\fs18\f2 &lt;\b0\fs20\f0  \sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 less than <\sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx5160\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  \b\fs18\f2 &gt;\b0\fs20\f0  \sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 greater than > \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx5160\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  \b\fs18\f2 &apos;\b0\fs20\f0  \sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 apostrophe ' \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx5160\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  \b\fs18\f2 &quot;\b0\fs20\f0  \sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 quotation mark " \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx5160\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  \b\fs18\f2 &nbsp;\b0\fs20\f0  \sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 non-breaking space \sa60\cell \row \pard\plain\sl-1\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl260 \b\fs20\lang1033 Tags\b0  are the pieces of information held within angled brackets < >. Tags can do one of several things: they can describe to the microbrowser how to format content; tell it to display non-textual items where they occur; or describe an action for the microbrowser to perform in certain circumstances. Tags are not visible to the user of the client device.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 Many tags occur in pairs, with a start tag immediately before the section of the file they relate to, and a corresponding end tag after it. An end tag always has a forward slash / immediately after the opening angled bracket.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 In the example file, you can see that the letters 'WML' have a \fs18\f2 <big>\fs20\f0  tag before them, and a \fs18\f2 </big>\fs20\f0  tag after them. These tags tell the microbrowser to display the content between them in large text. So on your microbrowser, the sentence will look like this:\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl234 \fs18\lang1024\f2 This is static WML text.\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl260 \fs20\lang1033\f0 A tag that does not have a closing tag is called an empty tag - empty because it has no content or other tags inside it. An example is the tag \fs18\f2 <img alt="3G Lab Logo" src="3glab.wbmp" align="middle"/>\fs20\f0 . This tag tells the browser to display the image file \fs18\f2 3glab.wbmp\fs20\f0  where it occurs. Note that an empty tag must end with a forward slash, so that the browser knows not to look for a companion end tag. In this respect WML differs from HTML, so if you are used to writing HTML, remember to include these slashes.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb259\s2\sl449 {\*\bkmkstart _2419}{\*\bkmkend _2419}\b\fs34\lang1024\f1 11.2 Elements and Attributes\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb173\li960\sl260 \b0\fs20\lang1033\f0 WML tags, like HTML tags, contain information about elements and attributes.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 An \b element\b0  is a structural unit of a WML document: every tag begins with an element indicator. In the example file (see Section 11.1), \fs18\f2 wml, card, p, img\fs20\f0  and \fs18\f2 big\fs20\f0  are all elements.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 An \b attribute\b0  provides information about a specific occurrence of an element. Attributes are included in an element's start tag, after the element indicator. In the example file in Section 11.1, the \fs18\f2 img\fs20\f0  element has several attributes associated with it. The attribute \fs18\f2 src="3glab.wbmp"\fs20\f0  tells the microbrowser to display the image \fs18\f2 3glab.wbmp\fs20\f0 ; \fs18\f2 alt="3G Lab Logo"\fs20\f0  tells it that if, for any reason, it can't display the image, it should display the text '3G Lab Logo' instead; and \fs18\f2 align="middle"\fs20\f0  tells the microbrowser to centre the image vertically on the card.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb259\s2\sl449 {\*\bkmkstart _2433}{\*\bkmkend _2433}\b\fs34\lang1024\f1 11.3 Cards and Decks\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb173\li960\sl260 \b0\fs20\lang1033\f0 Whereas HTML documents are organised into 'pages', WML documents are organised into units called 'cards' and 'decks'. A WML file is a deck, and a deck contains one or more cards. On loading a deck of several cards, a microbrowser will always display the first card by default.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 A card contains a 'screen's worth' of information. Because most WAP client devices have small display areas, the user will often have to scroll downwards to view the whole of a card; but the card should not be so long that they lose track of where they are. WML's navigation facilities enable you to jump from one card in a deck to another.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 Cards are marked up using the \fs18\f2 card\fs20\f0  element. There is no \fs18\f2 deck\fs20\f0  element - a deck comprises everything inside a file's \fs18\f2 wml\fs20\f0  element. See Section 11.5 for more information on the \fs18\f2 wml\fs20\f0  element.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb259\s2\sl449 {\*\bkmkstart _2442}{\*\bkmkend _2442}\b\fs34\lang1024\f1 11.4 XML Validation Tags\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl234 \b0\fs18\f2 <?xml version="1.0"?>\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
<!DOCTYPE wml PUBLIC "-//WAPFORUM//DTD WML 1.1//EN" \sa0\par\fi0\sb0
    "http://www.wapforum.org/DTD/wml_1.1.xml">\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl260 \fs20\lang1033\f0 The tag \fs18\f2 <?xml version="1.0"?>\fs20\f0  tells the browser that the file is an XML document, complying with version 1.0 of the XML specification. XML is a markup language of which WML is a specialised subset. So every valid WML document is implicitly also a valid XML document.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 The second tag tells the browser where to find the Document Type Definition (DTD) for files of its type - in this case, WML files. A DTD is an electronic specification for a file's format, declaring what elements are allowed within what other elements, and what attributes each element is allowed to contain. If the structure of the file does not match the structures allowed by the DTD, then the browser knows that it contains errors, and may display a message warning the user of this. In practice, browsers are often tolerant of errors and will do their best to display files, even if they are not structurally valid. However, you should always try to structure your WML files properly - apart from being good practice, this guarantees that they will display as you intend on all WML-compliant browsers.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 These two tags are not WML tags, but validation tags instructing the browser to parse the rest of the file as WML.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb259\s2\sl449 {\*\bkmkstart _2449}{\*\bkmkend _2449}\b\fs34\lang1024\f1 11.5 The wml Element\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb173\li960\sl260 \b0\fs20\lang1033\f0 The top-level element in every WML deck is \fs18\f2 wml\fs20\f0 . The \fs18\f2 <wml>\fs20\f0  tag announces to the browser that everything following it should be parsed as WML data, up to the \fs18\f2 </wml>\fs20\f0  tag.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 Therefore, the first tag in a deck (after the validation tags) should be \fs18\f2 <wml>\fs20\f0 , and the last tag in the deck should be \fs18\f2 </wml>\fs20\f0 .\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb259\s2\sl449 {\*\bkmkstart _2458}{\*\bkmkend _2458}\b\fs34\lang1024\f1 11.6 Paragraphs\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb173\li960\sl260 \b0\fs20\lang1033\f0 It is good practice to organise WML content into paragraphs. Paragraphs are defined by the p element. Note that in WML, unlike in HTML, each \fs18\f2 <p>\fs20\f0  tag \b must\b0  have a corresponding \fs18\f2 </p>\fs20\f0  closing tag. For example:\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl234 \fs18\lang1024\f2 <card>\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
  <p>This card is divided into paragraphs. This is the first paragraph.</p>\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
  <p>This is the second paragraph. It is also the last.</p>\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
</card>\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl260 \fs20\lang1033\f0 By default, text between \fs18\f2 <p>...</p>\fs20\f0  tags is left-aligned. You can centre- or right-align it using the \fs18\f2 align\fs20\f0  attribute with the value \fs18\f2 center\fs20\f0  or \fs18\f2 right\fs20\f0  respectively. For example:\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl234 \fs18\lang1024\f2 <p align="right">This paragraph is right-aligned.</p>\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb259\s2\sl449 {\*\bkmkstart _2471}{\*\bkmkend _2471}\b\fs34\f1 11.7 Hyperlinks\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb173\li960\sl260 \b0\fs20\lang1033\f0 Like HTML, WML allows you to include hyperlinks in your documents. A hyperlink can carry the user to a different place in the current deck, a different deck on the same WAP site, or a different WAP site.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 How a hyperlink is activated by the user is a matter for the browser manufacturer. A typical implementation might provide two buttons on the device to move a highlight backwards and forwards between hyperlinks, and a third button to activate the highlighted link.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 The hyperlink element in WML is \fs18\f2 a\fs20\f0  (as in HTML, this stands for 'anchor'). It takes an attribute \fs18\f2 href\fs20\f0 , which indicates the destination of the link. For example:\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl234 \fs18\lang1024\f2 Great oaks from <a \sa0\par\fi0\sb0
  href="http://www.littleacorns.com">little acorns</a> grow.\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl260 \fs20\lang1033\f0 Here, the words 'little acorns' are a hyperlink taking the user to the WAP site www.littleacorns.com. On a browser, the hyperlink may be indicated by underlining, in which case the sentence above would be displayed like this:\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl234 \fs18\lang1024\f2 Great oaks from little acorns grow.\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl260 \fs20\lang1033\f0 To create hyperlinks between cards in the same deck, give each \fs18\f2 card\fs20\f0  a unique \fs18\f2 id\fs20\f0  attribute, and refer to that \fs18\f2 id\fs20\f0  in the \fs18\f2 href\fs20\f0  attribute of hyperlinks to it. In the \fs18\f2 href\fs20\f0  attribute, precede the destination card's name with a hash #: this indicates that the link is to a card rather than to a file.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 The example file below contains two cards, linked by referring to one another's \fs18\f2 id\fs20\f0  attributes.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl234 \fs18\lang1024\f2 <?xml version="1.0"?>\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
 <!DOCTYPE wml PUBLIC "-//WAPFORUM//DTD WML 1.1//EN"\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
     "http://www.wapforum.org/DTD/wml_1.1.xml">\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
 <wml>\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
    <card id="card1">\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
   <p>\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
        This is a WML card.\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
      </p>\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
   <p>\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
        <a href="#card2">Next card...</a>\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
      </p>\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
    </card>\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
    <card id="card2">\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
   <p>\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
        And this is another.\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
      </p>\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
   <p>\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
        <a href="#card1">Previous card...</a>\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
      </p>\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
    </card>\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
 </wml>\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl260 \fs20\lang1033\f0 You can also link to a specific card in another deck by appending a hash # and the card's \fs18\f2 id\fs20\f0  to the URL. For example:\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl234 \fs18\lang1024\f2 <a href="anotherfile.wml#acertaincard">Go to that card</a>\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl260 \fs20\lang1033\f0 Here, the link 'Go to that card' takes the user to the card acertaincard in the deck anotherfile.wml.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb259\s2\sl449 {\*\bkmkstart _2494}{\*\bkmkend _2494}\b\fs34\lang1024\f1 11.8 Tasks (do Element)\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb173\li960\sl260 \b0\fs20\lang1033\f0 The \fs18\f2 do\fs20\f0  element allows you to incorporate simple interactive features into a WML card, without reference to any particular item on the card. How the user activates a \fs18\f2 do\fs20\f0  task depends on the device manufacturer's implementation of WML. The general idea is that a \fs18\f2 do\fs20\f0  function should be independent of where the user has scrolled to on the page, which hyperlink is currently highlighted, and so on. Typically, a \fs18\f2 do\fs20\f0  function will be activated by a physical button on the client device.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 A \fs18\f2 do\fs20\f0  element can perform one of four basic functions, depending on which of the following elements is present between its start and end tags. These elements are called 'tasks':\sa200\par\trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx5160\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  \fs18\f2 go\fs20\f0  \sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 Navigates to another WAP deck, site or card, by means of an \fs18\f2 href\fs20\f0  attribute. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx5160\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  \fs18\f2 prev\fs20\f0  \sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 Navigates to the previous URL on the client's history stack. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx5160\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  \fs18\f2 noop\fs20\f0  \sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 Does nothing. This can be useful for certain purposes, for example, to override a default task set in the \fs18\f2 template\fs20\f0  element of a deck (see Section 11.10). \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx5160\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  \fs18\f2 refresh\fs20\f0  \sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 Resets the current deck to the state it was in when it was first loaded. As well as resetting the visual display of a deck, \fs18\f2 refresh\fs20\f0  can also be made to reset variables (see Section 11.11). \sa60\cell \row \pard\plain\sl-1\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl260 \fs20\lang1033 Here is an example do statement:\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl234 \fs18\lang1024\f2  <do type="prev" label="Go Back" name="goback">\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
  <prev/>\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
 </do>\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl260 \fs20\lang1033\f0 This \fs18\f2 do\fs20\f0  element includes three attributes:\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1160\sl260\fi-200 \tx1160 \fs16\lang1024 \'95\tab \fs20 The \fs18\f2 type\fs20\f0  attribute gives the client browser an indication of what category of tasks the current task belongs to. Possible values include \fs18\f2 "accept", "prev", "help", "reset", "options"\fs20\f0  and \fs18\f2 "delete"\fs20\f0 . Note that the type attribute has no bearing on the actual function of the task element: it simply allows the client browser to decide how to display it. For example, the Nokia 7110 WAP phone displays a \fs18\f2 do\fs20\f0  element of the \fs18\f2 prev\fs20\f0  type in the bottom right corner of the screen and associates it with the right navigation button.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1160\sl260\fi-200 \tx1160 \fs16 \'95\tab \fs20 The \fs18\f2 label\fs20\f0  attribute tells the browser what wording to assign to the function on-screen. In some instances, a client device may automatically override a label attribute: for example, the Nokia 7110 phone always labels a \fs18\f2 do type="prev"\fs20\f0  function as 'Back', regardless of the content of the \fs18\f2 label\fs20\f0  attribute.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1160\sl260\fi-200 \tx1160 \fs16 \'95\tab \fs20 The \fs18\f2 name\fs20\f0  attribute gives the \fs18\f2 do\fs20\f0  function an identifier by which scripting languages such as WMLScript can refer to it.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 \lang1033 Suppose a client device associates \fs18\f2 do type="prev"\fs20\f0  with its right navigation button, and accepts a WML-defined \fs18\f2 label\fs20\f0  attribute. On such a device, our example do function might appear as shown in the bottom right corner of Figure 11.1.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960{\field\flddirty{\*\fldinst INCLUDEPICTURE "11-1.ps" }{\fldrslt }}\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 Figure 11.1\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb259\s2\sl449 {\*\bkmkstart _2554}{\*\bkmkend _2554}\b\fs34\lang1024\f1 11.9 Events\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb173\li960\sl260 \b0\fs20\lang1033\f0 WML allows you to specify automatic actions in a client device that are precipitated by particular 'events'. An 'event' must be associated with a particular element in a WML file - either \fs18\f2 card, wml\fs20\f0  or \fs18\f2 option\fs20\f0 . The response to an event is defined by the \fs18\f2 onevent\fs20\f0  element, which must occur within the element to which it relates. The type of event to respond to is indicated in the \fs18\f2 type\fs20\f0  attribute of the \fs18\f2 onevent\fs20\f0  element.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 Possible values for the \fs18\f2 type\fs20\f0  attribute are as shown in Figure 11.2.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 The action undertaken by the client device in response to an event must be defined by one of the task elements \fs18\f2 go, prev, noop\fs20\f0  or \fs18\f2 refresh\fs20\f0 . The task element must be situated within the \fs18\f2 onevent\fs20\f0  element.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 For example:\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl234 \fs18\lang1024\f2  <card>\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
  <onevent type="onenterbackward">\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
   <go href="backwardpeople.wml"/>\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
  </onevent>\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
  You didn&apos;t enter this card backwards.\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
 </card>\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl260 \fs20\lang1033\f0 If a user arrives at this card via a hyperlink or a \fs18\f2 go\fs20\f0  task, they will see the message\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl234 \fs18\lang1024\f2 You didn't enter this card backwards.\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl260 \fs20\lang1033\f0 However, if the user arrives at the card via a \fs18\f2 prev\fs20\f0  task, they will be redirected to the file \fs18\f2 backwardpeople.wml\fs20\f0 .\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 There is another way of handling an event that is shorter, but less flexible. Instead of using the \fs18\f2 onevent\fs20\f0  element, you can specify an action in the form of an attribute in the relevant \fs18\f2 card\fs20\f0  or \fs18\f2 wml\fs20\f0  element. For example, the sample WML above could be rewritten as follows, and would do exactly the same thing:\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl234 \fs18\lang1024\f2  <card onenterbackward="backwardpeople.wml">\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
     You didn&apos;t enter this card backwards.\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
 </card>\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl260 \fs20\lang1033\f0 Note that the only kind of action possible using this method is a jump to a URL (in this case, \fs18\f2 backwardpeople.wml\fs20\f0 ). This is equivalent to the \fs18\f2 go\fs20\f0  action. If you want to perform a \fs18\f2 prev\fs20\f0 , a \fs18\f2 noop\fs20\f0  or a \fs18\f2 refresh\fs20\f0  action, you have to use the longer method.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl260 {\*\bkmkstart _2587}{\*\bkmkend _2587}\b\lang1024 Table 11-1. Figure 11.2: WML event types\sa100\keepn\par\trowd\trleft960\trhdr \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx3760\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx6560\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \b\fs20\f1  \fs18\f2 <onevent type=>\fs20\f1  attribute value\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \b\fs20\f1 Occurs within element(s)...\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \b\fs20\f1 Function\sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx3760\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx6560\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  \fs18\f2 "ontimer"\fs20\f0  \sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  \fs18\f2 card, wml\fs20\f0  \sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 Executes the specified action when a timer expires. Timers are specified using the \fs18\f2 timer\fs20\f0  element. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx3760\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx6560\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  \fs18\f2 "onenterforward"\fs20\f0  \sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  \fs18\f2 card, wml\fs20\f0  \sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 Executes the specified action when the user arrives at the current card or deck via a hyperlink, a \fs18\f2 go\fs20\f0  task or any other method that involves moving forwards in the client microbrowser's history stack. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx3760\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx6560\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  \fs18\f2 "onenterbackward"\fs20\f0  \sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  \fs18\f2 card, wml\fs20\f0  \sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 Executes the specified action when the user arrives at the current card or deck via a \fs18\f2 prev\fs20\f0  task, or any other method that involves moving backwards in the client microbrowser's history stack. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx3760\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx6560\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  \fs18\f2 "onclick"\fs20\f0  \sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  \fs18\f2 option\fs20\f0  \sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 Executes the specified action when the user selects or deselects an option.\sa60\cell \row \pard\plain\sl-1\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb259\s2\sl449 {\*\bkmkstart _2624}{\*\bkmkend _2624}\b\fs34\f1 11.10 Templates\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb173\li960\sl260 \b0\fs20\lang1033\f0 Suppose you create a WML deck consisting of five cards, and you want a \fs18\f2 prev\fs20\f0  task on every card except the first. Rather than repeating the same \fs18\f2 do\fs20\f0  statement in each of the four cards - which is laborious for you as a WML author, and uses up precious bytes in the WML file - you can use the \fs18\f2 template\fs20\f0  element to specify a task to appear automatically on every card you want.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 Here is an example of a WML file that uses a \fs18\f2 template\fs20\f0  element:\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl234 \fs18\lang1024\f2  <?xml version="1.0"?>\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
 <!DOCTYPE wml PUBLIC "-//WAPFORUM//DTD WML 1.1//EN"\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
     "http://www.wapforum.org/DTD/wml_1.1.xml">\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
 \sa0\par\fi0\sb0
 <wml>\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
 \sa0\par\fi0\sb0
  <template>\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
   <do type="prev">\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
    <prev/>\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
   </do>\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
  </template>\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
  <card id="card1">\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
   <do type="prev">\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
    <noop/>\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
      </do>\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
      Love speaks, even when the lips are closed.\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
      <br/>\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
      <a href="#card2">\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
    Next proverb...\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
      </a>\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
    </card>\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
    <card id="card2">\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
   It is an ill dog that deserves not a crust. \sa0\par\fi0\sb0
   <br/>\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
   <a href="#card3">\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
         Next proverb...\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
      </a>\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
    </card>\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
    <card id="card3">\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
      He who wants a mule without fault, must walk on foot.\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
      <br/>\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
      <a href="#card4">\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
        Next proverb...\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
      </a>\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
    </card>\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
    <card id="card4">\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
   Never cast a clout till May be out.\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
      <br/>\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
      <a href="#card5">\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
       Next proverb...\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
      </a>\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
    </card>\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
    <card id="card5">\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
   Scabby donkeys scent each other over nine hills.\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
   <br/>\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
  </card>\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
 </wml>\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl260 \fs20\lang1033\f0 When this file is viewed on a WAP microbrowser, every card except the first one has a 'Back' link on it - even though, in the WML source, none of them contains a \fs18\f2 prev\fs20\f0  task. The explanation for this is that the \fs18\f2 prev\fs20\f0  task is held in the \fs18\f2 template\fs20\f0  element at the beginning of the file, and is applied automatically to every card in the deck, except the first.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 The reason there is no 'Back' link on the first card is that the first card contains a \fs18\f2 noop\fs20\f0  task. Because a task defined in a card always overrides a default task of the same type as specified in the \fs18\f2 template\fs20\f0 , and because \fs18\f2 noop\fs20\f0  does nothing, this card displays nothing in place of a 'Back' link.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb259\s2\sl449 {\*\bkmkstart _2641}{\*\bkmkend _2641}\b\fs34\lang1024\f1 11.11 Variables\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb173\li960\sl260 \b0\fs20\lang1033\f0 One of the most interesting features of WML is its ability to process variables. If you have any programming experience, you will know that a variable is a 'label' for a numeric value or a string of characters in a computer program. A common use of variables is to store input from a user or another external source - in other words, information that can't be written into the program itself.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 Variables in WML enable you to implement interactive features into your cards and decks without getting involved in the complexities of scripting languages like WMLScript. Note that the only variables allowed in WML are strings.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 There are a number of ways in which you can specify variables. One of the most useful is via the \fs18\f2 input\fs20\f0  element, which allows the user to enter information into the client device's interface.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 Here is a very simple WML deck that writes a 'proverb' based on terms the user enters through the \fs18\f2 input\fs20\f0  element:\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl234 \fs18\lang1024\f2  <?xml version="1.0"?>\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
 <!DOCTYPE wml PUBLIC "-//WAPFORUM//DTD WML 1.1//EN"\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
     "http://www.wapforum.org/DTD/wml_1.1.xml">\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
 <wml>\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
  <card id="card1" title="Proverb Wizard">\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
      Name an animal:\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
      <input name="animal"/>\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
      Name a vegetable:\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
      <input name="vegetable"/>\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
       <a href="#card2">\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
     View proverb...\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
       </a>\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
  </card>\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
  <card id="card2" title="Your proverb">\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
   <do type="accept" label="Another?">\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
    <go href="#card1"/>\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
   </do>\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
   A small $(animal) may eat a large $(vegetable).\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
  </card>\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
 </wml>\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl260 \fs20\lang1033\f0 When this file is opened on a microbrowser, the following card will be displayed:\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960{\field\flddirty{\*\fldinst INCLUDEPICTURE "11-3.ps" }{\fldrslt }}\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 Figure 11.3\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 As can be seen, there are two input fields. These are created by the tags \fs18\f2 <input name="animal"/>\fs20\f0  and \fs18\f2 <input name="vegetable"/>\fs20\f0  in the WML source. The \fs18\f2 name\fs20\f0  attributes tell the browser to create the variables \fs18\f2 animal\fs20\f0  and \fs18\f2 vegetable\fs20\f0  respectively, based on what the user types into each field.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 To select a horse for the animal and a radish for the vegetable, enter \b\fs18\f2 horse\b0\fs20\f0  into the first field and \b\fs18\f2 radish\b0\fs20\f0  into the second field. (The actual mechanism for entering text will depend on your microbrowser. For example, it may present you with an Edit option that you activate by pressing one of the phone's navigation buttons.)\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 The microbrowser display will now be as in Figure 11.4.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960{\field\flddirty{\*\fldinst INCLUDEPICTURE "11-4.ps" }{\fldrslt }}\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 Figure 11.4\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 Now, activate the 'View proverb...' link. A new card is loaded, showing a proverb based on the terms you entered in the first card (see Figure 11.5).\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960{\field\flddirty{\*\fldinst INCLUDEPICTURE "11-5.ps" }{\fldrslt }}\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 Figure 11.5\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 In the source WML you can see how the terms entered in the first card were passed to the second card. The variables \fs18\f2 animal\fs20\f0  and \fs18\f2 vegetable\fs20\f0  were set in the tags \fs18\f2 <input name="animal"/>\fs20\f0  and \fs18\f2 <input name="vegetable"/>\fs20\f0 .\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 The source text in the second card looks like this:\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl234 \fs18\lang1024\f2 A small $(animal) may eat a large $(vegetable).\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl260 \fs20\lang1033\f0 When the browser encounters a dollar symbol $, it knows that what follows in parentheses is a variable. So instead of displaying '(animal)' it looks for the variable called \fs18\f2 animal\fs20\f0  and displays its value - in this example, the string 'horse'. It does the same with the variable \fs18\f2 vegetable\fs20\f0 , substituting its name with 'radish'.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 (If you want to display a dollar symbol on the screen, use two dollar symbols $$ in your WML file.)\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb259\s2\sl449 {\*\bkmkstart _2689}{\*\bkmkend _2689}\b\fs34\lang1024\f1 11.12 Timers\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb173\li960\sl260 \b0\fs20\lang1033\f0 You can incorporate a timer into a WML card or deck using the \fs18\f2 timer\fs20\f0  element. When a timer runs out, an action is performed as specified in the \fs18\f2 ontimer\fs20\f0  event handler. See Section 11.9 for more details about event handling.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 \fs18\f2 timer\fs20\f0  takes a \fs18\f2 value\fs20\f0  attribute, specifying how long the timer should run for. The value is expressed in tenths of a second.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 Here is an example timer implementation:\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl234 \fs18\lang1024\f2  <?xml version="1.0"?>\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
 <!DOCTYPE wml PUBLIC "-//WAPFORUM//DTD WML 1.1//EN"\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
     "http://www.wapforum.org/DTD/wml_1.1.xml">\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
 <wml>\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
  <card id="wait">\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
   <onevent type="ontimer">\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
    <go href="#thankyou"/>\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
   </onevent>\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
   <timer value="40"/>\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
   <p>\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
   Please wait.\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
   </p>\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
  </card>\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
  <card id="thankyou">\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
   <p>\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
   Thank you for waiting.\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
   </p>\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
  </card>\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
 </wml>\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl260 \fs20\lang1033\f0 When you first load this file you will see the message 'Please wait.' After about four seconds, the first card will time out and the second card will appear, showing the message 'Thank you for waiting.'\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb259\s2\sl449 {\*\bkmkstart _2700}{\*\bkmkend _2700}\b\fs34\lang1024\f1 11.13 Images\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb173\li960\sl260 \b0\fs20\lang1033\f0 WML can include embedded images. These must be in two-colour wireless bitmap (WBMP) format. There are several free WBMP editors that can be downloaded from the World Wide Web.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 To embed an image in a file, use the \fs18\f2 img\fs20\f0  element with some or all of the attributes shown in Figure 11.6. For example:\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl234 \fs18\lang1024\f2 <img src="3glab.wbmp" alt="3G Lab Logo" align="middle"/>\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl260 {\*\bkmkstart _2706}{\*\bkmkend _2706}\b\fs20\f0 Table 11-2. Figure 11.6: Image attributes\sa100\keepn\par\trowd\trleft960\trhdr \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx5160\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \b\fs20\f1 Attribute (* = required) \sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \b\fs20\f1 Function\sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx5160\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  \fs18\f2 src\fs20\f0  *\'ef\'bf\'bd\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 Specifies the URL of the source file for the image. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx5160\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  \fs18\f2 alt\fs20\f0  *\'ef\'bf\'bd\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 Specifies text for the browser to display if the image cannot be displayed. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx5160\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  \fs18\f2 localsrc\fs20\f0  \sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 Specifies a local source for the image. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx5160\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  \fs18\f2 vspace\fs20\f0  \sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 Specifies the amount of blank space to appear above and below the image in the microbrowser display. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx5160\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  \fs18\f2 hspace\fs20\f0  \sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 Specifies the amount of blank space to appear to the left and the right of the image in the microbrowser display. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx5160\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  \fs18\f2 align\fs20\f0  \sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb100\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20\lang1033 Specifies the vertical alignment of the image in relation to the surrounding text. Must have the value \fs18\f2 "top", "middle"\fs20\f0  or \fs18\f2 "bottom"\fs20\f0 .\hyphpar0\par\pard\intbl\sb100\li100\ri100\sl260 To change the horizontal alignment of an image, enclose it in an appropriately formatted paragraph.\hyphpar0\par\pard\intbl\sb100\li100\ri100\sl260 For example: \hyphpar0\par\pard\intbl\sb200\li100\ri100\sl234 \fs18\f2 \sa0\par\fi0\sb0
     <p align="center">\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
     <img src="image.wbmp" \sa0\par\fi0\sb0
                   alt="An image"/>\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
     </p>\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
\sa260\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx5160\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs18\f2 height,width\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 Specify the height and width of the image in pixels. This allows the browser to reserve space for it while it loads therest of the card. If these attributes are omitted, the image is displayed at its actual size, though the microbrowser will probably not reserve space for it while the card is loading. If the height and width attributes do not match the actual height and width of an image, the microbrowser either scales the image to match the attributes, or else ignores the attributes and shows the image at its actual size.\sa60\cell \row \pard\plain\sl-1\hyphpar0\par\sect\sectd\plain\pgwsxn12240\pghsxn15840\marglsxn1440\margrsxn1440\margtsxn1440\margbsxn1920\headery0\footery0\pgndec\titlepg{\headerf\pard\sl-240\sb770\sa430\plain\tqc\tx4680\tqr\tx9360 {}\tab {}\tab {}\par}{\footerf\pard\sl-240\sb770\sa910\plain\tqc\tx4680\tqr\tx9360 {}\tab {}\tab {\i\fs20 \chpgn }\par}{\headerl\pard\sl-240\sb770\sa430\plain\tqc\tx4680\tqr\tx9360 {}\tab {}\tab {\i\fs20 Chapter 12. 12 Example WAP Sites}\par}{\footerl\pard\sl-240\sb770\sa910\plain\tqc\tx4680\tqr\tx9360 {}\tab {}\tab {\i\fs20 \chpgn }\par}{\headerr\pard\sl-240\sb770\sa430\plain\tqc\tx4680\tqr\tx9360 {}\tab {}\tab {\i\fs20 Chapter 12. 12 Example WAP Sites}\par}{\footerr\pard\sl-240\sb770\sa910\plain\tqc\tx4680\tqr\tx9360 {}\tab {}\tab {\i\fs20 \chpgn }\par}\pard\sb311\s1\sl539 {\*\bkmkstart _2748}{\*\bkmkend _2748}\b\fs41\f1 Chapter 12. 12 Example WAP Sites\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb207\li960\sl260 \b0\fs20\lang1033\f0 Included on the Alligata Server CD-ROM are five example WAP sites. If you installed the example sites along with the Alligata Server (see Sections 4 and 5), and you have an HTTP server such as Apache running on your computer, you can view them from a WAP phone or a WAP phone emulator.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 The example sites are illustrations of the kinds of service you can implement using WAP. You can create simple sites using just WML, or you can incorporate dynamic features into them using scripting languages like Perl and PHP. The most important thing to remember is to keep the files you serve as small and simple as possible. A compiled WML file should not be larger than 1400 bytes, which means the size of your source WML file should not exceed about 3000 characters.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 \b\lang1024 To view the example WAP sites: \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1320\sl260\fi-360 \tqr\tx1270\tx1320\tab \b0 1.\tab If you have not already done so, install the Alligata Server as described in Sections 4 and 5. If you are installing from a graphical user interface, select 'Alligata Server example sites' in the main installation dialog box. If you are installing from the command prompt, enter Y when you are asked, 'Install Alligata Server examples?'.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1320\sl260\fi-360 \tqr\tx1270\tx1320\tab 2.\tab Start the Bearer Box and the WAP Box using any of the methods described in Section 8.1.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1320\sl260\fi-360 \tqr\tx1270\tx1320\tab 3.\tab Start the Web server. For example, to start Apache:\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1320\sl260 \b Linux users:. \b0 Type \b\fs18\f2 /etc/rc.d/init.d/httpd start\b0\fs20\f0  at the command prompt, and press ENTER.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1320\sl260 \b Solaris users:. \b0 Type \b\fs18\f2 /etc/init.d/apache start\b0\fs20\f0  at the command prompt, and press ENTER.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1320\sl260\fi-360 \tqr\tx1270\tx1320\tab 4.\tab Configure your WAP client device to use your installation of the Alligata Server as its WAP gateway. An example configuration is shown in Figure 12.1.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li1320\sl260 {\*\bkmkstart _2772}{\*\bkmkend _2772}\b Table 12-1. Figure 12.1: Example WAP phone configuration\sa100\keepn\par\trowd\trleft1320 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx5340\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li460\ri100\sl260 \fs20 Home URL:\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li460\ri100\sl260 \fs20  \fs18\f2 http://www.mysite.net\fs20\f0  \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft1320 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx5340\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li460\ri100\sl260 \fs20 Service name:\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li460\ri100\sl260 \fs20  \fs18\f2 Alligata\fs20\f0  \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft1320 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx5340\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li460\ri100\sl260 \fs20 IP address: \sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li460\ri100\sl260 \fs20  \fs18\f2 10.0.0.1\fs20\f0  \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft1320 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx5340\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li460\ri100\sl260 \fs20 Dial-up phone number:\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li460\ri100\sl260 \fs20  \fs18\f2 0999123456\fs20\f0  \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft1320 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx5340\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li460\ri100\sl260 \fs20 Bearer type:\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li460\ri100\sl260 \fs20  \fs18\f2 data\fs20\f0  \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft1320 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx5340\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li460\ri100\sl260 \fs20 Call type:\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li460\ri100\sl260 \fs20  \fs18\f2 analogue\fs20\f0  \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft1320 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx5340\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li460\ri100\sl260 \fs20 Connection type:\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li460\ri100\sl260 \fs20  \fs18\f2 cont\fs20\f0  \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft1320 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx5340\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li460\ri100\sl260 \fs20 PPP security:\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li460\ri100\sl260 \fs20  \fs18\f2 off\fs20\f0  \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft1320 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx5340\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li460\ri100\sl260 \fs20 Authentication mode:\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li460\ri100\sl260 \fs20  \fs18\f2 normal\fs20\f0  \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft1320 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx5340\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li460\ri100\sl260 \fs20 Login:\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li460\ri100\sl260 \fs20  \fs18\f2 foo\fs20\f0  \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft1320 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx5340\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li460\ri100\sl260 \fs20 Password: \sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li460\ri100\sl260 \fs20  \fs18\f2 bar\fs20\f0  \sa60\cell \row \pard\plain\sl-1\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li1320\sl260 \fs20\lang1033 The login and password settings are those of your Point-to-Point Protocol (PPP) server.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1320\sl260\fi-360 \tqr\tx1270\tx1320\tab \lang1024 5.\tab On your WAP client device, navigate to the URL of the example sites index. By default this will be the URL of your Web server, followed by \fs18\f2 /wml/\fs20\f0 ; for example, \fs18\f2 http://acomputer.asite.net/wml/\fs20\f0 .\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1320\sl260\fi-360 \tqr\tx1270\tx1320\tab 6.\tab The card in Figure 12.2 is displayed.\hyphpar0\par\pard\li1320{\field\flddirty{\*\fldinst INCLUDEPICTURE "12-2.ps" }{\fldrslt }}\par\pard\sb100\li1320\sl260 \lang1033 Figure 12.2: Site Examples index\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1320\sl260 From this card, you can navigate to any of the example sites by selecting the relevant hyperlink. The example sites are described in Sections 12.1 to 12.5.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb259\s2\sl449 {\*\bkmkstart _2833}{\*\bkmkend _2833}\b\fs34\lang1024\f1 12.1 Example Site 1: Static WML (Simple)\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb173\li960\sl260 \b0\fs20\lang1033\f0 The simplest kind of WAP site consists of a set of static WML decks or cards. You can find examples of this kind of site by selecting 'Static Simple' or 'Static Complex' from the Site Examples index.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 The 'Static Simple' is discussed in Section 11.1. It is a single WML card that displays the message, 'This is static WML text.'\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb259\s2\sl449 {\*\bkmkstart _2837}{\*\bkmkend _2837}\b\fs34\lang1024\f1 12.2 Example Site 2: Static WML (Complex)\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb173\li960\sl260 \b0\fs20\lang1033\f0 If you select 'Static Complex' from the Site Examples index page, you will be presented with the logo shown in Figure 12.3.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960{\field\flddirty{\*\fldinst INCLUDEPICTURE "12-3.ps" }{\fldrslt }}\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 Figure 12.3\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 After a few seconds the logo will disappear, to be replaced by a set of links (see Figure 12.4).\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960{\field\flddirty{\*\fldinst INCLUDEPICTURE "12-4.ps" }{\fldrslt }}\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 Figure 12.4\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 If you follow a link, you will be taken to the corresponding piece of information. You can also use the link 'ACME home' to take you back to the home deck. (Since this is a \fs18\f2 prev\fs20\f0  task, it may simply be labelled 'Back' on your device's display - see Section 11.8.)\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 If you select 'ACME Vacancies', the card in Figure 12.5 will appear on your screen.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960{\field\flddirty{\*\fldinst INCLUDEPICTURE "12-5.ps" }{\fldrslt }}\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 Figure 12.5\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 If you select a link from this card, you will be taken to information about the relevant post. For example, 'Head of Marketing' will show you the card in Figure 12.6.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960{\field\flddirty{\*\fldinst INCLUDEPICTURE "12-6.ps" }{\fldrslt }}\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 Figure 12.6\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 From here you can use the 'ACME Vacancies' link to return to the list of jobs. (Again, this a \fs18\f2 prev\fs20\f0  task, so your device may just label it 'Back'.) To go right back to the home deck, keep navigating backwards in the same way.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 From the home deck, you can navigate through any series of cards in the way just described.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 The first file that the microbrowser loaded when it visited the site \fs18\f2 Static Complex\fs20\f0  is called \fs18\f2 index.wml\fs20\f0 . Its source is as follows:\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl234 \fs18\lang1024\f2  <?xml version="1.0"?>\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
