\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl260 \fs20\lang1033\f0 All the features of this file are described elsewhere in this guide. It is a deck of four cards, with the first card presenting hyperlinks to the other three. In the earlier example the link 'Head of Marketing' was chosen, which goes to the card \fs18\f2 job2\fs20\f0 .\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 Note that, once again, every card in the deck has a \fs18\f2 <prev/>\fs20\f0  link to return to the previous card.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb259\s2\sl449 {\*\bkmkstart _2933}{\*\bkmkend _2933}\b\fs34\lang1024\f1 12.3 Example Site 3: Perl System Resource Monitor\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb173\li960\sl260 \b0\fs20\lang1033\f0 A WAP site does not need to consist just of static WML files: it can also contain scripts that generate WML decks on the fly. To generate WML dynamically, you can use any of the same scripting languages that are available to generate HTML pages on the World Wide Web. All you have to remember is to make your output as concise as possible (less than 3000 characters) and that it needs to be valid WML.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 One language already widely used for Web scripting is the Practical Extraction and Report Language (Perl). Perl's powerful text processing capabilities make it equally suitable for WAP scripting.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 You call a WAP script from a mobile device exactly as you would call a Web script from a desktop computer: the relevant URL just has to point to the script file rather than a WML deck. For example, \fs18\f2 http://www.awapsite.net/anyscript.pl\fs20\f0  could execute the Perl script \fs18\f2 anyscript.pl\fs20\f0  on the WAP site \fs18\f2 http://www.awapsite.net/\fs20\f0 . (Of course, the script's output needs to be in WML, in order to display on a WAP microbrowser.)\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 To download Perl and to find out more about it, visit \fs18\f2 www.perl.com\fs20\f0 . Many books are also available on Perl programming.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 The Alligata Server's example Perl site retrieves system information from its host machine and sends it in WML format to the client device. Because the information is retrieved using Linux/UNIX commands, the Perl script must be running on a Linux or UNIX operating system.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 To view the Alligata Server example Perl site, select 'Sysinfo Perl' from the Site Examples index. You will see the card in Figure 12.7.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960{\field\flddirty{\*\fldinst INCLUDEPICTURE "12-7.ps" }{\fldrslt }}\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 Figure 12.7\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 From top to bottom, this information is as follows:\sa200\par\trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx5160\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 Linux-2.2.16\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 Operating system \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx5160\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 19 users\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 Number of logins since the system was started up \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx5160\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 Up: 11 Days 7h54m\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 Time the system has been running \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx5160\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 CPU: 24% AVG: 1.18\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 Current CPU load and load average \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx5160\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 RAM: 98% \sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 Amount of RAM used \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx5160\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 SWP: 8% \sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 Amount of swap space used \sa60\cell \row \pard\plain\sl-1\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl260 \fs20\lang1033 The first item in the list is a hyperlink that takes you to a card showing the names of the users currently logged in to the server machine (see Figure 12.8).\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960{\field\flddirty{\*\fldinst INCLUDEPICTURE "12-8.ps" }{\fldrslt }}\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 Figure 12.8\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 The WML generated by the Perl script for this file is as follows:\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl234 \fs18\lang1024\f2  <?xml version="1.0"?>\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
