12.4\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb259\s2\sl449 {\*\bkmkstart _2980}{\*\bkmkend _2980}\b\fs34\f1 12.4 Example Site 4: PHP System Resource Monitor\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb173\li960\sl260 \b0\fs20\lang1033\f0 PHP is a server-side embedded scripting language optimised for use on the World Wide Web. Like Perl, it can be made to generate dynamic output in WML as easily as in HTML. Again, the important thing to remember is that a dynamically generated WML file, like a static WML file, must be less than about 3000 bytes long before compression.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 PHP is an open source programming language. You can learn about it at the PHP Web site at www.php.net (http://www.php.net). This site includes a complete online PHP manual, a Quick Reference Guide and a Frequently Asked Questions page.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 The Alligata Server package includes two example PHP sites. The first retrieves system information from its host machine's operating system using the \fs18\f2 uptime\fs20\f0  and \fs18\f2 free\fs20\f0  Linux/UNIX commands and the PHP \fs18\f2 fsockopen\fs20\f0  function.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 To view the PHP System Resource Monitor, select the link 'Sysinfo PHP' from the Site Examples index.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 You will see the site's main index card (see Figure 12.9).\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960{\field\flddirty{\*\fldinst INCLUDEPICTURE "12-9.ps" }{\fldrslt }}\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 Figure 12.9\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 Follow any of the three links, 'Load/Uptime', 'Memory' or 'Port Status', to display the relevant card. Example cards are shown below. For ease of legibility, the content of these cards is shown in full, rather than on a microbrowser screen. The information in the 'Load/Uptime' card is explained in the right column; the information in the 'Memory' and the 'Port Status' cards is self-explanatory.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl234 \fs18\lang1024\f2 Load/Uptime\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
