\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb259\s2\sl449 {\*\bkmkstart _3000}{\*\bkmkend _3000}\b\fs34\f1 12.5 Example Site 5: 'PAT' PHP Mail\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb173\li960\sl260 \b0\fs20\lang1033\f0 The Alligata Server's second example PHP site, 'PAT', enables you to read your e-mail from a WAP client device. It uses PHP's IMAP functions to retrieve messages from your e-mail server, embeds them in WML, and sends them to your mobile device. Despite their name, IMAP functions in PHP can be used with protocols other than IMAP, namely POP3, NNTP and local mailbox access methods.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 Before PAT will work on your system, you need to make one edit to the file \fs18\f2 index.wml\fs20\f0  in the directory \fs18\f2 /home/httpd/alligata/wml/dynamic/complex/pat/\fs20\f0  (Linux) or \fs18\f2 /opt/TGLBallex/alligata/wml/dynamic/complex/pat/\fs20\f0  (Solaris). In the \fs18\f2 <go/>\fs20\f0  tag, insert the name of your e-mail server after \fs18\f2 server=\fs20\f0 . For example, if your e-mail server is \fs18\f2 imap.asite.net\fs20\f0 , the tag should be as follows:\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl234 \fs18\lang1024\f2 <go href="view.php3?username=$username&amp;password=$passwo\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
rd&amp;server=imap.asite.net"/>\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl260 \fs20\lang1033\f0 To view the PAT mail site, select the link 'PAT: PHP mail' from the Site Examples index. You will see a login card:\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960{\field\flddirty{\*\fldinst INCLUDEPICTURE "12-10.ps" }{\fldrslt }}\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 Figure 12.10\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 This card is from the file \fs18\f2 index.wml\fs20\f0 . Enter your e-mail user name and password and you will be taken to the top of your e-mail inbox:\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960{\field\flddirty{\*\fldinst INCLUDEPICTURE "12-11.ps" }{\fldrslt }}\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 Figure 12.11\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 The inbox shows each message's number in the list and its subject. Owing to the size limitations of WML files, the inbox is divided into segments of five messages. To view the next five messages in the inbox, follow the hyperlink 'NEXT 5' at the bottom of the card.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 To view a message, select the hyperlinked number preceding it. The message is prefixed by details of the subject, the send date and time, and the sender:\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960{\field\flddirty{\*\fldinst INCLUDEPICTURE "12-12.ps" }{\fldrslt }}\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 Figure 12.12\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960{\field\flddirty{\*\fldinst INCLUDEPICTURE "12-13.ps" }{\fldrslt }}\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 Figure 12.13\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 Where a message is longer than 700 characters, it is split into several smaller messages.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 PAT is composed of several files. These are summarised in Figure 12.14.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl260 {\*\bkmkstart _3038}{\*\bkmkend _3038}\b\lang1024 Table 12-2. Figure 12.14: Summary of PAT files\sa100\keepn\par\trowd\trleft960\trhdr \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx5160\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \b\fs20\f1 File\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \b\fs20\f1 Function\sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx5160\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 Main files:\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sl-120\par\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx5160\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  \fs18\f2 index.wml\fs20\f0  \sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 Contains the login card. Receives the user name and password from the user and invokes \fs18\f2 view.php3\fs20\f0 . \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx5160\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  \fs18\f2 view.php3\fs20\f0  \sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 Invokes \fs18\f2 lib/mailbox.php3\fs20\f0  to display the mailbox. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx5160\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  \fs18\f2 message.php3\fs20\f0  \sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 Invokes \fs18\f2 lib/mailmessage.php3\fs20\f0  to display a message. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx5160\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 Library files: \sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sl-120\par\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx5160\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  \fs18\f2 lib/mailbox.php3\fs20\f0  \sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 Sets up the class \fs18\f2 mailbox\fs20\f0 , which defines the contents of a mailbox. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx5160\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  \fs18\f2 lib/mailmessage.php3\fs20\f0  \sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 Sets up the class \fs18\f2 mailMessage\fs20\f0 , which defines the contents of a message. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx5160\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  \fs18\f2 lib/mesgretrieve.php3\fs20\f0  \sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 Prepares messages for WML display.\sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx5160\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  \fs18\f2 lib/wmlheader.php3\fs20\f0  \sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 Defines a header for a WML file. \sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx5160\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20  \fs18\f2 lib/escaper.php3\fs20\f0  \sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 Converts escape characters.\sa60\cell \row \pard\plain\sl-1\hyphpar0\par\sect\sectd\plain\pgwsxn12240\pghsxn15840\marglsxn1440\margrsxn1440\margtsxn1440\margbsxn1920\headery0\footery0\pgndec\titlepg{\headerf\pard\sl-240\sb770\sa430\plain\tqc\tx4680\tqr\tx9360 {}\tab {}\tab {}\par}{\footerf\pard\sl-240\sb770\sa910\plain\tqc\tx4680\tqr\tx9360 {}\tab {}\tab {\i\fs20 \chpgn }\par}{\headerl\pard\sl-240\sb770\sa430\plain\tqc\tx4680\tqr\tx9360 {}\tab {}\tab {\i\fs20 Chapter 13. 13 SMS Messaging}\par}{\footerl\pard\sl-240\sb770\sa910\plain\tqc\tx4680\tqr\tx9360 {}\tab {}\tab {\i\fs20 \chpgn }\par}{\headerr\pard\sl-240\sb770\sa430\plain\tqc\tx4680\tqr\tx9360 {}\tab {}\tab {\i\fs20 Chapter 13. 13 SMS Messaging}\par}{\footerr\pard\sl-240\sb770\sa910\plain\tqc\tx4680\tqr\tx9360 {}\tab {}\tab {\i\fs20 \chpgn }\par}\pard\sb311\s1\sl539 {\*\bkmkstart _3087}{\*\bkmkend _3087}\b\fs41\f1 Chapter 13. 13 SMS Messaging \keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb207\li960\sl260 \b0\fs20\lang1033\f0 The Alligata Server enables you to use SMS in three ways:\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1160\sl260\fi-200 \tx1160 \fs16\lang1024 \'95\tab \fs20 To implement dynamic services such as retrieval of information from Web pages, in response to keywords contained in incoming SMS messages from mobile phones.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1160\sl260\fi-200 \tx1160 \fs16 \'95\tab \fs20 To send SMS messages to mobile phones from a personal computer, using HTTP commands.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1160\sl260\fi-200 \tx1160 \fs16 \'95\tab \fs20 To configure WAP phones to use a particular WAP service from a computer workstation.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 \lang1033 Before you can set up SMS keyword services or send SMS messages from a computer, you need to create an SMS Box group and an SMSC group in the configuration file (see Section 9). You must also have a subscription to an SMSC or a connection to a GSM modem, as explained in Section 9.3.2. Some example settings are shown in Figure 13.1.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 Figure 13.1: Example SMS Box and SMSC group configuration settings\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl234 \fs18\lang1024\f2 group = smsbox\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
bearerbox-host = localhost\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
sendsms-port = 13013\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
global-sender = 123\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
log-file = /alligata/admin/smsbox.log\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
log-level = 0\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
group = smsc\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
smsc = at\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
modemtype = wavecom\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
device = /dev/ttyS2\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb259\s2\sl449 {\*\bkmkstart _3100}{\*\bkmkend _3100}\b\fs34\f1 13.1 Implementing SMS Keyword Services\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb173\li960\sl260 \b0\fs20\lang1033\f0 Once you have configured an SMS Box group and an SMSC group in the configuration file, you need to create an SMS Service group for each service you want to implement. An example SMS Service group is shown in Figure 13.2. (See Section 9.3.3 for explanations of the configuration variables.)\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 Figure 13.2: Example SMS Service group configuration settings\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl234 \fs18\lang1024\f2 group = sms-service\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
keyword = proverb\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
aliases = Proverb;PROVERB;potd;Potd;POTD\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
url = http://www.awebsite.net/potd.html\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
prefix = <!--beginprov-->\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
suffix = <!--endprov-->\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
split-chars = ;:.\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
split-suffix = -cont-\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
header = "Today's proverb -- "\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
max-messages = 10\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl260 \fs20\lang1033\f0 This group sets up an SMS service that returns a proverb from the Web page \fs18\f2 http://www.awebsite.net/potd.html\fs20\f0  when it receives the message 'proverb' from a mobile device. From the Web page, it retrieves everything between (but not including) the strings \fs18\f2 <!--beginprov-->\fs20\f0  and \fs18\f2 <!--endprov-->\fs20\f0 . The content of \fs18\f2 potd.html\fs20\f0  could look like this:\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl234 \fs18\lang1024\f2  <html>\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
  <head>\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
        <title>SMS proverb of the day</title>\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
  </head>\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
  <body bgcolor="#FFFFFF" text="#000000">\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
   <!--beginprov-->\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
   A cat in gloves catches no mice.\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
   <!--endprov-->\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
  </body>\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
 </html>\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl260 \fs20\lang1033\f0 In this case, a user sending the message 'proverb' to the Alligata Server would receive the reply message\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl234 \fs18\lang1024\f2 Today's proverb -- A cat in gloves catches no mice.\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl260 \fs20\lang1033\f0 The Alligata Server does not require the prefix and suffix of the message to be in comments \fs18\f2 <!--...-->\fs20\f0 , but bear in mind that if they are not, they will be visible in the Web page if it is viewed from a desktop browser.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 The maximum length of an SMS message is usually 140 or 160 characters (depending on whether it is in 7-bit or 8-bit format). If the text retrieved by the Alligata Server is longer than this limit, the Alligata Server splits it into several messages. To do this, it uses the variables \fs18\f2 split-chars\fs20\f0  and \fs18\f2 split-suffix\fs20\f0 . The message is split at an occurrence of one of the characters specified in \fs18\f2 split-chars\fs20\f0 , and all sections of the message except the last have the text specified in \fs18\f2 split-suffix\fs20\f0  appended to them. The Alligata Server splits the message at the nearest previous occurrence of a split character to the maximum message length. For example, suppose the proverb in \fs18\f2 potd.html\fs20\f0  is a particularly long one:\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl234 \fs18\lang1024\f2  <html>\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
  <head>\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
      <title>SMS proverb of the day</title>\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
  </head>\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
  <body bgcolor="#FFFFFF" text="#000000">\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
   <!--beginprov-->\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
   Monday's child is fair of \sa0\par\fi0\sb0
         face, Tuesday's child is \sa0\par\fi0\sb0
         full of grace; Wednesday's \sa0\par\fi0\sb0
         child is full of woe, \sa0\par\fi0\sb0
         Thursday's child has far \sa0\par\fi0\sb0
         to go; Friday's child is \sa0\par\fi0\sb0
         loving and giving, Saturday's \sa0\par\fi0\sb0
         child works hard for its living; \sa0\par\fi0\sb0
         and the child that is born on the \sa0\par\fi0\sb0
         Sabbath day, is fair and wise and \sa0\par\fi0\sb0
         good and gay.\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
   <!--endprov-->\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
  </body>\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
 </html>\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl260 \fs20\lang1033\f0 In this instance, the message needs to be split, and the user will probably receive it in three smaller messages:\sa200\par\trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx5160\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 Message 1:\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl234 \fs18\f2 Today's proverb -- Monday's child is fair of face, Tuesday's child is full of grace; Wednesday's child is full of woe, Thursday's child has far to go; -cont.-\sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx5160\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 Message 2:\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl234 \fs18\f2 Today's proverb -- Friday's child is loving and giving, Saturday's child works hard for its living; -cont.-\sa60\cell \row \trowd\trleft960 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx5160\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw20\clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw20\cellx9360 \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl260 \fs20 Message 3:\sa60\cell \plain \pard\intbl\sb60\li100\ri100\sl234 \fs18\f2 Today's proverb -- and the child that is born on the Sabbath day, is fair and wise and good and gay.\sa60\cell \row \pard\plain\sl-1\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl260 \fs20\lang1033 The string \fs18\f2 -cont.-\fs20\f0  in the first and second messages indicates that they are part of a longer message, more of which is to follow. It is specified using the \fs18\f2 split-suffix\fs20\f0  configuration variable.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 Note that the incoming SMS message must \b only\b0  contain the keyword, unless the SMS service allows parameters after it. Parameters are discussed in Section 13.1.1.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb216\s3\li960\sl374 {\*\bkmkstart _3143}{\*\bkmkend _3143}\b\fs28\lang1024\f1 13.1.1 SMS Service Parameters\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb144\li960\sl260 \b0\fs20\lang1033\f0 The configuration variables \fs18\f2 url, file\fs20\f0  and \fs18\f2 text\fs20\f0  can include parameters that the Alligata Server substitutes with terms from the incoming message. Each parameter is indicated by a percent character % followed by a letter. The parameters you can use are listed in full in Section 9.3.3.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 Parameter values should be included in incoming messages after the service keyword, using the following syntax:\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 \b\fs18\f2  \i\fs16 keyword parameter#1 parameter#2\i0\fs18 , etc. \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 \b0\fs20\f0 As an example, suppose your company has created a CGI server application that returns an employee's salary. You want the Personnel department to be able to gain access to this information from their SMS phones, but no one else. In this case, you could configure the SMS Service group shown in Figure 13.3.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 Figure 13.3: Example SMS Service group for a salary retrieval application\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl234 \fs18\lang1024\f2 keyword = salary\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
aliases = Salary;SALARY\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
url = http://intranet.awebsite.net/cgi/salary?user=%s&password=%s&employee=%r\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
header = "Salary info -- "\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl260 \fs20\lang1033\f0 When the Alligata Server receives an SMS message starting with the keyword 'salary' (or one of its aliases), it replaces the first \fs18\f2 %s\fs20\f0  in \fs18\f2 url\fs20\f0  with the next word in the message after the keyword; replaces the second \fs18\f2 %s\fs20\f0  with the next word after that; and replaces the \fs18\f2 %r\fs20\f0  with the rest of the message.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 For example, if the CGI account has the user name \b personnel\b0  and the password \b STARfruit\b0 , then a Personnel member can access the salary details of the employee Emma Thompson by sending the Alligata Server the following SMS message:\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 \b\fs18\f2  salary personnel STARfruit emma thompson \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 \b0\fs20\f0 Upon receiving this message, the Alligata Server sends the following HTTP GET request:\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 \b\fs18\f2  http://intranet.awebsite.net/cgi/salary?user=personnel& password=STARfruit&employee=emma+thompson \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 \b0\fs20\f0 When the HTTP reply arrives, the Alligata Server converts it into an SMS message or messages, and sends it back to the Personnel member's mobile phone.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb259\s2\sl449 {\*\bkmkstart _3169}{\*\bkmkend _3169}\b\fs34\lang1024\f1 13.2 Sending SMS Messages Using HTTP\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb173\li960\sl260 \b0\fs20\lang1033\f0 For every user you want to be able to send SMS messages using HTTP, you need to define a Send SMS User group in the configuration file. An example Send SMS User group is shown in Figure 13.4.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 Figure 13.4: Example Send SMS User group\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl234 \fs18\lang1024\f2 group = sendsms-user\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
username = colin\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
password = AVOcado\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
user-allow-ip = 10.0.0.5\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
max-messages = 10\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
split-chars = .;,\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
split-suffix = -cont.-\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
header = "Msg from Colin -- "\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl260 \fs20\lang1033\f0 This configuration group allows the user \b colin\b0  with the password \b AVOcado\b0  to send SMS messages from the IP address 10.0.0.5. As with SMS keyword services, outgoing messages that are over the maximum length of a single SMS message can be split into several smaller messages, up to the maximum specified in \fs18\f2 max-messages\fs20\f0 . The variables \fs18\f2 split-chars, split-suffix\fs20\f0  and \fs18\f2 header\fs20\f0  work exactly as in SMS Service groups (see Section 13.1).\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 \b\fs18\f2 http://\i\fs16 hostname:port\i0\fs18 /cgi-bin/sendsms? username=\i\fs16 username\i0\fs18 & password=\i\fs16 password\i0\fs18 & from=\i\fs16 sender's_number\i0\fs18 & to=\i\fs16 receiver's_number\i0\fs18 & text=\i\fs16 message_text\i0\fs18  \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 \b0\fs20\f0 The elements in bold italic type should be replaced with the relevant information. For example:\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 \b\fs18\f2  http://localhost:13013/cgi-bin/sendsms?username=colin& password=AVOcado&from=0999666666&to=0898999999&text= All+sales+staff+return+to+office+immediately \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 \b0\fs20\f0 The URL should contain no carriage returns and no spaces. Spaces in the message should be represented by plus signs +.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb216\s3\li960\sl374 {\*\bkmkstart _3192}{\*\bkmkend _3192}\b\fs28\lang1024\f1 13.2.1 User Data Headers (\fs25\f2 udh\fs28\f1  parameter)\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb144\li960\sl260 \b0\fs20\lang1033\f0 An SMS message can include a \b user data header (UDH)\b0  that contains non-textual information for use by a mobile phone. For example, a UDH can tell a phone to handle the main body of the message in a particular way, such as storing it as a ring tone or as a graphical image to appear when the phone is switched on. For information on the format of UDH messages for a particular phone, consult the phone's documentation or visit the manufacturer's Web site.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 When you send an SMS message from a computer workstation using HTTP, you can include a UDH by using the optional \fs18\f2 udh\fs20\f0  parameter. For example:\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 \b\fs18\f2  http://localhost:13013/cgi-bin/sendsms?username=colin& password=AVOcado&from=0999666666&to=0898999999&text= %52%62%1a%bb%31%0d%21%84%77%ba%f7%f2&udh=%31%a7%99%f3%80%db \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 \b0\fs20\f0 All non-alphanumeric characters in the message text and the UDH value must be in hexadecimal format and preceded by a percent sign %.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb216\s3\li960\sl374 {\*\bkmkstart _3202}{\*\bkmkend _3202}\b\fs28\lang1024\f1 13.2.2 \fs25\f2 smsc\fs28\f1  Parameter\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb144\li960\sl260 \b0\fs20\lang1033\f0 You can use the optional smsc parameter to force an SMS message to be sent via a particular SMSC. The smsc parameter should have the value set by the \fs18\f2 smsc-id\fs20\f0  variable in the relevant SMSC group of the configuration file (see Section 9.3.2). For example:\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 \b\fs18\f2  http://localhost:13013/cgi-bin/sendsms?username=colin& password=AVOcado&from=0999666666&to=0898999999&text= All+sales+staff+return+to+office+immediately&smsc=smsc5 \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb216\s3\li960\sl374 {\*\bkmkstart _3209}{\*\bkmkend _3209}\fs28\lang1024\f1 13.2.3 SMS Messaging Using HTML Forms\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb144\li960\sl260 \b0\fs20\lang1033\f0 Typing complex URLs into a browser's 'Location' box can be tedious, and if you expect to be sending a lot of SMS messages, it is worth creating an HTML form for the task. Use the HTTP GET method, and define variables in HTML input objects. Constant values can be embedded in hidden objects. Variable names should be specified in the name attributes of the relevant elements, and the corresponding values in \fs18\f2 value\fs20\f0  attributes. For example:\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl234 \fs18\lang1024\f2  <html>\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
 <head>\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
  <title>SMS Message Sender</title>\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
 </head>\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
 <body bgcolor="#FFFFFF" text="#000000">\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
  <h1>SMS Message Sender</h1>\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
  <form name="sendsms" method="get" \sa0\par\fi0\sb0
            action="http://localhost:13013/cgi-bin/sendsms">\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
   <input type="hidden" name="username" value="tester">\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
   <input type="hidden" name="password" value="foobar">\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
   <input type="hidden" name="from" value="0999666666">\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
   <p>\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
    Telephone number:<br>\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
    <input type="text" size="30" name="to">\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
   </p>\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
   <p>\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
    Message:<br>\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
    <textarea cols="25" rows="5" name="text">\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
</textarea>\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
   </p>\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
   <p>\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
    User Data Header (optional):<br>\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
    <input type="text" size="30" name="udh">\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
   </p>\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
   <input type="submit" value="Send Message">\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
   <br>\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
  </form>\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
 </body>\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
 </html>\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl260 \fs20\lang1033\f0 The appearance of this form in a Web browser is shown in Figure 13.5.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960{\field\flddirty{\*\fldinst INCLUDEPICTURE "13-5.ps" }{\fldrslt }}\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 Figure 13.5: Example HTML form for SMS messaging\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb259\s2\sl449 {\*\bkmkstart _3220}{\*\bkmkend _3220}\b\fs34\lang1024\f1 13.3 Over-the-air (OTA) Configuration of WAP Client Devices Using SMS\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb173\li960\sl260 \b0\fs20\lang1033\f0 Changing the settings for a WAP service provider from a mobile device can be laborious, with up to a dozen variables to alter on each device. If you are a system administrator needing to configure all your company's WAP phones to the same service, the process becomes particularly time-consuming. However, the Alligata Server allows you to automate device configuration by defining automatic WAP service settings in the configuration file. By sending a single SMS-format message to each phone from a computer workstation, you can add a whole configuration to its settings. It is also possible for the user of a client device to invoke the OTA configuration themselves. This enables a user to add WAP services to their mobile phone with a minimum of effort.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 The Alligata Server's OTA configuration feature is currently known to work on the Nokia 7110, Nokia 6210 and Nokia 9110i phones.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 In order to implement OTA configuration, you need to set up three configuration groups in the configuration file:\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1160\sl260\fi-200 \tx1160 \fs16\lang1024 \'95\tab \fs20 an OTA Configuration group, containing the settings to be sent to the client device\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1160\sl260\fi-200 \tx1160 \fs16 \'95\tab \fs20 a Send SMS User group, setting up a user account for sending of OTA messages\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1160\sl260\fi-200 \tx1160 \fs16 \'95\tab \fs20 an SMS Service group, pointing to the Alligata Server's \fs18\f2 sendota\fs20\f0  CGI application\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 \lang1033 For details of configuration groups and variables, see Section 9.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 An example set of configuration groups for OTA configuration is shown in Figure 13.6.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 \b\lang1024 To configure a WAP client device over the air (system administrator): \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1160\sl260\fi-200 \tx1160 \b0\fs16 \'95\tab \fs20 Send a message to the Alligata Server via HTTP using the following syntax:\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1160\sl260 \b\fs18\lang1033\f2  http://\i\fs16 hostname:port\i0\fs18 /cgi-bin/sendota? username=\i\fs16 username\i0\fs18 &password=\i\fs16 password\i0\fs18 &phonenumber=\i\fs16 destination_phone_number\i0\fs18  \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1160\sl260 \b0\fs20\f0 For example:\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1160\sl260 \b\fs18\f2  http://localhost:13013/cgi-bin/sendota?username=otauser& password=foo&phonenumber=449999123456 \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1160\sl260 \b0\fs20\f0 The client device will receive a message containing the appropriate configuration information.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 \b\lang1024 To configure a WAP client device over the air (client):. \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1320\sl260\fi-360 \tqr\tx1270\tx1320\tab \b0 1.\tab Send an SMS message to the Alligata Server's telephone number consisting of the keyword for the OTA configuration service. For example, for the configuration in Figure 13.6:\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1320\sl260 \b\fs18\lang1033\f2  ota \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1320\sl260\fi-360 \tqr\tx1270\tx1320\tab \b0\fs20\lang1024\f0 2.\tab After a while, you will receive an SMS message from the Alligata Server containing the new settings. How long this message takes to arrive depends on the capabilities and load of your SMSC.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li1320\sl260\fi-360 \tqr\tx1270\tx1320\tab 3.\tab Use the relevant feature of your device to add the configuration settings to your list of WAP providers and, if required, make the new service your default one.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb100\li960\sl260 \lang1033 Figure 13.6: Example settings for OTA configuration\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb200\li960\sl234 \fs18\lang1024\f2 group = sms-service\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
